window.LEARN = [{"id":"devops-1-the-memos-project-explained","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"1","title":"The Memos project, explained","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/README.md","label":"README"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall: say this out loud daily</b> “I deployed the open-source Memos note-taking app to AWS at <b>tm.myegh.com</b>. The network is a VPC in <b>eu-west-1 across two AZs</b>, with <b>public and private subnets and</b> <b>a NAT gateway in each AZ</b>. The app is packaged with a <b>three-stage Dockerfile</b> that cut the image from <b>2.79 GB to 75.8 MB, about 97% smaller</b>, and runs as a non-root user. Images go to <b>ECR</b> with <b>immutable</b> <b>SHA tags</b> and scan-on-push. It runs on <b>ECS Fargate in private subnets</b>, behind a public <b>ALB</b> that redirects HTTP to HTTPS and ends TLS with an <b>ACM</b> certificate that Terraform validates automatically through <b>Route 53</b>. Everything is <b>Terraform</b>: a separate <b>bootstrap</b> stack for the state bucket and OIDC role, then <b>seven modules</b> (VPC, security, ACM, ALB, ECR, IAM, ECS), with <b>S3 remote state and native locking</b>. <b>GitHub Actions</b> builds and deploys using <b>OIDC</b>, so there are no stored AWS keys. <b>tfsec</b> scans the Terraform on every run, a post-deploy check hits <b>/healthz</b>, and I can <b>roll back</b> by redeploying any earlier SHA.”"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Request:</b> tm.myegh.com → Route 53 alias → ALB (80 → 301 → 443) → TLS ends → target group → task on <b>:8081</b>","<b>Deploy:</b> push → build.yml (tfsec, OIDC, build or reuse SHA image → ECR) → deploy.yml (tfsec, terraform plan/apply with the new image) → curl /healthz","<b>SGs:</b> ALB in 80/443 from anywhere, out <b>only to the ECS SG on 8081</b> · ECS in <b>8081 from the ALB SG</b> <b>only</b>, out <b>443 only</b>"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Roles:</b> execution = ECS pulls the image and writes logs · task = the app (no permissions; it doesn’t call AWS)","<b>Known limits:</b> SQLite data lives inside the container · 1 task · the OIDC role is admin · TG health check uses <code>/</code>"]}]}]},{"title":"What the project is","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Memos is an open-source note-taking web app (Go backend, React frontend). I took its code and made it run on AWS, set up the way a real company runs a production service: private networking, HTTPS, infrastructure as code, and an automated, keyless pipeline."}]},{"title":"What the project is","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Fact</b>","<b>Value</b>"],["URL","https://tm.myegh.com"],["Repo","github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra"],["Region / AZs","eu-west-1 · eu-west-1a and eu-west-1b"],["VPC","10.0.0.0/16 · public 10.0.1.0/24, 10.0.2.0/24 · private 10.0.3.0/24, 10.0.4.0/24"],["App port","<b>8081</b> (set with <code>MEMOS_PORT=8081</code>; Memos’s own default is 5230)"],["Task size","256 CPU units (0.25 vCPU), 512 MB, <b>1 task</b>"],["Terraform","1.13.5 in the pipeline · AWS provider ~&gt; 5.0"]]}]},{"title":"What the project is","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Fact</b>","<b>Value</b>"],["State","S3 bucket <code>memos-terraform-state-enamul</code>, key <code>prod/terraform.tfstate</code>, <code>use_lockfile = true</code>"]]}]},{"title":"The parts and what each does","part":"1/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Part</b>","<b>What it does in Memos</b>"],["<b>Dockerfile</b>","Three stages: <b>Node 22</b> builds the frontend, <b>Go 1.25</b> compiles a static binary (<code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code>), and <b>Alpine 3.21</b> holds only the binary, CA certificates and timezone data. It runs as user <b>1001</b>, not root."],["<b>ECR</b>","Private image registry. Tags are <b>IMMUTABLE</b> (a tag can never be overwritten), <b>scan on push</b> finds known vulnerabilities, and <code>force_delete = true</code> lets <code>terraform destroy</code> remove it even with images inside."]]}]},{"title":"The parts and what each does","part":"2/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Part</b>","<b>What it does in Memos</b>"],["<b>ECS cluster +</b> <b>service</b>","with <b>no public IP</b>, and registers it with the target group. <code>memos-cluster</code> and <code>memos-service</code>. The service keeps <b>1</b> Fargate task running in the private subnets"],["<b>Task</b> <b>definition</b>","Image (the SHA-tagged ECR URL), 0.25 vCPU / 512 MB, port 8081, the two IAM roles, and logs to CloudWatch group <code>/ecs/memos</code> (7-day retention)."]]}]},{"title":"The parts and what each does","part":"3/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Part</b>","<b>What it does in Memos</b>"],["<b>ALB</b>","Internet-facing, in the public subnets. <b>Port 80</b> only redirects (301) to HTTPS. <b>Port 443</b> uses the ACM certificate with the TLS 1.3 policy <code>ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06</code> and forwards to the target group. It drops invalid HTTP headers."],["<b>Target group</b>","Type <code>ip</code> (required for Fargate), port 8081. Health check: path <code>/</code>, healthy on codes 200–399, every 30 s, 2 passes or 2 failures to change state."]]}]},{"title":"The parts and what each does","part":"4/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Part</b>","<b>What it does in Memos</b>"],["<b>ACM</b>","A certificate for <code>myegh.com</code> and <code>tm.myegh.com</code>. Terraform creates the DNS validation records in Route 53 itself (<code>for_each</code> over <code>domain_validation_options</code>), then waits for validation."],["<b>Route 53</b>","An <b>alias A record</b> <code>tm.myegh.com</code> → the ALB, with <code>evaluate_target_health = true</code>. The hosted zone already existed and is read with a <code>data</code> source."]]}]},{"title":"The parts and what each does","part":"5/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Part</b>","<b>What it does in Memos</b>"],["<b>VPC</b>","Two public and two private subnets across two AZs, one internet gateway, <b>two NAT gateways (one per</b> <b>AZ)</b> with Elastic IPs, one public route table, and <b>one private route table per AZ</b>, each pointing at its own NAT gateway."],["<b>IAM</b>","Execution role with <code>AmazonECSTaskExecutionRolePolicy</code> (pull from ECR, write logs). Task role with no permissions. The GitHub OIDC role lives in bootstrap."]]}]},{"title":"The parts and what each does","part":"6/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Part</b>","<b>What it does in Memos</b>"],["<b>Bootstrap</b> <b>stack</b>","Separate Terraform with its own local state. It creates the <b>S3 state bucket</b> (versioning, AES-256 encryption, public access blocked, HTTPS-only bucket policy, <code>prevent_destroy</code>) and the <b>GitHub OIDC</b> <b>provider + role</b>. These survive a destroy of the main infrastructure."],["<b>GitHub</b> <b>Actions</b>","(manual tear-down). <code>build.yml</code> (build and push), <code>deploy.yml</code> (Terraform deploy, then a health check) and <code>destroy.yml</code>"]]}]},{"title":"The architecture","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":" User ──https://tm.myegh.com──► Route 53 (alias A record → ALB)\n                                     │\n┌──────────── VPC 10.0.0.0/16 · eu-west-1a + eu-west-1b ────────────┐\n│ PUBLIC subnets 10.0.1.0/24 · 10.0.2.0/24  (route 0.0.0.0/0 → IGW) │\n│   ALB  :80 → 301 → :443  (ACM cert, TLS 1.3 policy)               │\n│   NAT gateway in 1a        NAT gateway in 1b                      │\n│        │ HTTP :8081 · health check GET / (200–399)                │\n│        ▼                                                          │\n│ PRIVATE subnets 10.0.3.0/24 · 10.0.4.0/24 (each → its own NAT)    │\n│   ECS Fargate: 1 Memos task on :8081 (user 1001, SQLite inside)   │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n Task → NAT → ECR (image) and CloudWatch Logs (/ecs/memos)"}]},{"title":"Flow 1: a user opens the site","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["The user types tm.myegh.com.","DNS asks Route 53, which answers with the ALB’s IP addresses (alias record).","If the browser uses <b>http://</b> (port 80), the ALB replies <b>301</b> and sends it to <b>https://</b>.","The browser connects to the ALB on <b>port 443</b>. The ALB presents the ACM certificate and decrypts the request (<b>TLS termination</b>).","The HTTPS listener forwards the request to the <b>target group</b>.","The target group sends it to the healthy task’s private IP on <b>port 8081</b>."]}]},{"title":"Flow 1: a user opens the site","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":7,"items":["Memos answers, and the reply goes back through the ALB to the user."]}]},{"title":"Flow 2: I push new code","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>build.yml</b> runs on a push to <code>main</code> that changes <code>app/**</code>, the <code>Dockerfile</code> or <code>.dockerignore</code>:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Check out the code.","<b>tfsec</b> scans the Terraform.","<b>OIDC:</b> assume the role in the <code>AWS_ROLE_ARN</code> secret and get temporary credentials.","Log in to ECR.","If an image tagged with this commit SHA <b>already exists, reuse it</b>. If not, <code>docker build</code> and <code>docker push</code>. (Tags are immutable, so pushing the same tag twice would fail.)"]}]},{"title":"Flow 2: I push new code","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>deploy.yml</b> starts automatically when build.yml <b>succeeds</b> (<code>workflow_run</code>):"}]},{"title":"Flow 2: I push new code","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":6,"items":["Check out, tfsec again, OIDC again.","Check that the tag is a <b>full 40-character SHA</b> and that the image exists in ECR, then build the full image URL.","<code>terraform fmt -check</code> → <code>init</code> → <code>validate</code> → <code>plan -var=\"image_url=…\" -out=tfplan</code> → <code>apply tfplan</code>.","Terraform registers a <b>new task definition revision</b> with the new image and updates the ECS service. ECS starts a new task, waits for it to pass the target group health check, then stops the old one (rolling update)."]}]},{"title":"Flow 2: I push new code","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":10,"items":["<b>health-check job:</b> waits 60 s, then <code>curl https://tm.myegh.com/healthz</code>. Anything other than <b>200</b> fails the pipeline."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Rollback:</b> run deploy.yml by hand (<code>workflow_dispatch</code>) with the full SHA of an earlier image. It redeploys that exact image. It’s manual, not automatic."}]},{"title":"Security setup","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Layer</b>","<b>Control</b>"],["Network","Tasks in private subnets, <code>assign_public_ip = false</code>; public subnets also don’t auto-assign public IPs"],["ALB SG","In: 80 and 443 from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> · Out: <b>only to the ECS SG on 8081</b>"],["ECS SG","In: <b>8081 only from the ALB SG</b> · Out: <b>TCP 443 only</b> (ECR, S3 image layers, CloudWatch via NAT)"],["TLS","HTTP → HTTPS redirect · TLS 1.3 policy · invalid headers dropped"],["Container","Non-root user 1001 · tiny Alpine runtime with no compilers or source"]]}]},{"title":"Security setup","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Layer</b>","<b>Control</b>"],["Images","Immutable tags · scan on push"],["Pipeline","OIDC (no stored keys) · tfsec on every build and deploy · <code>terraform fmt -check</code> and <code>validate</code>"],["State","Encrypted, versioned, private bucket; HTTPS-only policy; native locking; <code>prevent_destroy</code>"]]}]},{"title":"Security setup","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Conscious trade-offs, documented with</b> <code>tfsec:ignore</code> <b>comments in the code:</b> the ALB must be public; ports 80/443 must be open to everyone; ECS egress allows any host on 443; ECR and logs use AWS default encryption, not customer-managed KMS keys; <b>no VPC Flow Logs</b> and <b>no Container Insights</b>, to save cost. Being able to say “I knew about each of these, chose it for cost, and wrote down why” is a strong interview answer."}]},{"title":"Known limitations: know these cold","part":"1/8","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"An interviewer who reads the repo will find these. Raise them yourself first."}]},{"title":"Known limitations: know these cold","part":"2/8","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>#</b>","<b>Limitation</b>","<b>Why it matters</b>","<b>The fix</b>"],["1","<b>Data is a SQLite file inside the container</b> (<code>MEMOS_DATA=/var/opt/memos</code>, no volume)","Every deploy or task replacement starts a new container, and <b>the notes are lost</b>","Mount <b>EFS</b> at <code>/var/opt/memos</code>, or better, move to <b>RDS PostgreSQL</b> (Memos supports <code>-</code> <code>-driver postgres</code>), with the connection string in Secrets Manager"]]}]},{"title":"Known limitations: know these cold","part":"3/8","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>#</b>","<b>Limitation</b>","<b>Why it matters</b>","<b>The fix</b>"],["2","<b>One task</b> (<code>desired_count = 1</code>)","The network spans two AZs, but the app doesn’t; if the task or its AZ fails, the site is down until ECS replaces it","2+ tasks across AZs, plus Service Auto Scaling. <b>Fix #1 first</b>, because two tasks can’t share one SQLite file"]]}]},{"title":"Known limitations: know these cold","part":"4/8","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>#</b>","<b>Limitation</b>","<b>Why it matters</b>","<b>The fix</b>"],["3","<b>OIDC role has</b> <code>AdministratorAccess</code>, and its trust allows","Any workflow on any branch or PR in the repo can become an","A least-privilege policy (ECR push, ECS, the specific resources Terraform manages, the state bucket); restrict <code>sub</code> to"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>repo:EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra:*</code>"]}]},{"title":"Known limitations: know these cold","part":"5/8","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","","admin in the account","environment <code>repo:…:ref:refs/heads/main</code> or a GitHub"],["4","<b>Target group health check uses</b> <code>/</code>, while the app has a real <code>/healthz</code>","page, so it’s slower and isn’t a true health signal <code>/</code> returns the web","Set the health check path to <code>/healthz</code> (the pipeline already checks it)"]]}]},{"title":"Known limitations: know these cold","part":"6/8","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["5","<b>No tests, no image scan gate,</b> <code>-auto-</code> <code>approve</code> <b>on main</b>","Broken code or a risky change can reach production with no human check","Run the Go tests, add Trivy, run <code>plan</code> on pull requests, add a production <b>environment with</b> <b>required approval</b>"],["6","<b>Rollback is manual</b>","A failed health check fails the pipeline but","ECS <b>deployment circuit breaker</b> with"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>rollback = true</code>"]}]},{"title":"Known limitations: know these cold","part":"7/8","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","","leaves the bad version running",""],["7","<b>Port 8081 is hard-coded in several places</b> (task, TG, health check, both SG rules)","Changing it means editing five places, and a mismatch means unhealthy targets","One <code>container_port</code> variable passed to every module"],["8","<b>Unused</b> <code>route53</code> <b>module</b> (its module block was accidentally put in creates the record directly) <code>modules/route53/outputs.tf</code>; the root","Dead code is confusing","Call the module from the root or delete it"]]}]},{"title":"Known limitations: know these cold","part":"8/8","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>README mismatches to fix:</b> it says ECR lives in bootstrap (it’s in <code>infra/modules/ecr</code>), it mentions “tfsec/Checkov” (only tfsec runs), and it says Fargate “scales to zero” (this setup always runs 1 task)."}]}]},{"id":"devops-2-linux","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"2","title":"Linux","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/etc</code> config · <code>/var/log</code> logs · <code>/home</code> users · <code>/tmp</code> scratch · “root” = <code>/</code>, the root user, <code>/root</code>","<b>r4 w2 x1</b> · 755 scripts · 644 files · 600/400 keys · a directory without <code>x</code> = can’t <code>cd</code>","<code>usermod -aG</code> (always <code>-a</code>) · <code>kill</code> = SIGTERM 15 · <code>kill -9</code> = SIGKILL · Ctrl+C = SIGINT 2","<code>systemctl start</code> = now · <code>enable</code> = at boot","<code>&gt;</code> overwrite · <code>&gt;&gt;</code> append · <code>2&gt;</code> errors · <code>2&gt;&amp;1</code> both · <code>|</code> pipe"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["Busiest IP: <code>awk '{print $1}' f | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -1</code>"]}]}]},{"title":"What Linux is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Linux is an operating system. Strictly, “Linux” is the <b>kernel</b>: the core program that controls the CPU, memory, disks and running programs. A <b>distribution</b> (Ubuntu, Debian, Amazon Linux) is the kernel plus tools and a package manager."},{"type":"p","html":"Most servers, cloud machines and containers run Linux, so almost all DevOps work happens on Linux. My Memos container runs Linux."}]},{"title":"Terminal, shell, kernel","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Terminal:</b> the window you type in.","<b>Shell:</b> the program that reads what you type and runs it. On Linux this is usually <b>Bash</b>.","<b>Kernel:</b> carries out the work on the hardware."]},{"type":"p","html":"A command has three parts: <code>ls -la /etc</code> = command (<code>ls</code>), options (<code>-la</code>), argument (<code>/etc</code>). Commands are case-sensitive."}]},{"title":"The file system","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Everything is in one tree starting at <code>/</code>, called the <b>root directory</b>."},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Folder</b>","<b>What’s in it</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/etc</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Configuration files (plain text)"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/var/log</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Log files. Check here first when something breaks."],["One folder per user"]]}]},{"title":"The file system","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/home</code>","<code>/root</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["The root user’s home folder"],["Temporary files, cleared on reboot"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/tmp</code>","<code>/usr/bin</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Most commands"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/opt</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Third-party software"]]}]},{"title":"The file system","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/proc</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Live information about running processes"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Paths:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["An <b>absolute</b> path starts with <code>/</code> and works from anywhere: <code>cd /var/log</code>.","A <b>relative</b> path starts from where you are: <code>cd log</code> only works if a <code>log</code> folder is here.","<code>~</code> = your home folder · <code>.</code> = this folder · <code>..</code> = the folder above.","A file whose name starts with <code>.</code> is hidden. <code>ls -a</code> shows it."]}]},{"title":"The file system","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"“Root” means three different things: the <code>/</code> directory, the <code>root</code> superuser, and <code>/root</code> (that user’s home)."}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Command</b>","<b>Does</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>pwd</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Shows which folder you’re in"],["Lists files, including hidden ones, with details"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>ls -la</code>","<code>cd</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Changes folder"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>mkdir -p a/b/c</code>"]}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Makes folders, including any missing parents"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>touch f</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","Creates an empty file"],["<code>cp -r</code> · <code>mv</code> · <code>rm -r</code>","Copy · move/rename · delete (permanent, with no recycle bin)"],["<code>cat</code> · <code>head</code> · <code>tail -n 20</code>","Show a file · its start · its end"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>tail -f file</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Shows new lines as they’re written (watch a log live)"]]}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>wc -l</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Counts lines"]]}]},{"title":"Users, groups and sudo","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Every file and process belongs to a <b>user</b>.","Users can be in <b>groups</b>, and a group can be given access to files.","<b>root</b> can do anything. <code>sudo command</code> runs one command as root.","<code>sudo adduser ana</code> creates a user. <code>sudo usermod -aG devops ana</code> adds ana to the devops group."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ Always use <code>-aG</code>. Without <code>-a</code>, the user is <b>removed from all their other groups</b>."}]},{"title":"Permissions","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Every file has an <b>owner</b>, a <b>group</b>, and three sets of permissions: owner, group, others."},{"type":"pre","text":"-rwxr-xr--  1  ana  devops  512  deploy.sh\n │└┬┘└┬┘└┬┘       │    │\n │ │  │  └ others: r-- (read only)\n │ │  └─── group : r-x (read and run)\n │ └────── owner : rwx (read, write, run)\n └──────── type  : - file, d directory, l link"}]},{"title":"Permissions","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Letter</b>","<b>On a file</b>","<b>On a directory</b>"],["r (4)","read it","list the names inside"],["w (2)","change it","create or delete files inside"],["x (1)","run it","enter it with <code>cd</code>"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Numbers:</b> add the values for each set. rwx = 4+2+1 = 7, r-x = 5, r– = 4."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>755</code> = rwxr-xr-x: scripts and folders","<code>644</code> = rw-r–r–: normal files","<code>600</code> or <code>400</code> = owner only: SSH private keys"]}]},{"title":"Permissions","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Rules:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Linux uses only one set, the first that matches: owner → group → others.","If you can <code>ls</code> a folder but can’t <code>cd</code> into it, it’s missing <code>x</code>.","<code>chmod u+x script.sh</code> lets the owner run it. <code>chown ana:devops file</code> changes owner and group."]}]},{"title":"Processes and services","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>process</b> is a running program, and it has a <b>PID</b> (process ID).","<code>ps aux</code> lists all processes. <code>ps aux | grep nginx</code> finds one.","<b>PID 1</b> is <code>systemd</code>, which starts everything else.","<code>kill PID</code> sends <b>SIGTERM (15)</b>: asks the program to stop cleanly.","<code>kill -9 PID</code> sends <b>SIGKILL (9)</b>: forces it to stop immediately. Use only if SIGTERM fails.","Ctrl+C sends <b>SIGINT (2)</b>. Ctrl+Z sends <b>SIGTSTP (20)</b> and pauses the program."]}]},{"title":"Processes and services","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ If a running script is filling a log, deleting the script file doesn’t stop it, because it’s already loaded in memory. You must kill the process."},{"type":"p","html":"A <b>service</b> is a program that runs in the background (a web server, SSH). <code>systemctl</code> manages services:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>systemctl status nginx</code>: is it running?","<code>sudo systemctl start nginx</code>: start it now.","<code>sudo systemctl enable nginx</code>: start it automatically at every boot."]}]},{"title":"Processes and services","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <b>start</b> and <b>enable</b> are separate. “Stopped” means not running now; “disabled” means it won’t start at boot."}]},{"title":"Input and output streams","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Every command has three streams:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>stdin (0):</b> input","<b>stdout (1):</b> normal output","<b>stderr (2):</b> error messages"]},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Symbol</b>","<b>Effect</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>&gt;</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Send output to a file, <b>replacing</b> its contents"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>&gt;&gt;</code>"]}]},{"title":"Input and output streams","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Add output to the <b>end</b> of a file"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>2&gt;</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","Send only errors to a file"],["<code>&amp;&gt;</code> or <code>&gt; f 2&gt;&amp;1</code>","Send output and errors to the same file"],["","Throw errors away"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>2&gt;/dev/null</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<code>\\|</code> (pipe)","Give the output of one command to the next as its input"]]}]},{"title":"Text tools","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>grep</b> keeps lines that match: <code>grep -i error app.log</code> (<code>-i</code> ignores case, <code>-c</code> counts, <code>-v</code> inverts, <code>-n</code> shows line numbers, <code>-r</code> searches folders).","<b>awk</b> splits each line into columns: <code>awk '{print $1}' access.log</code> prints column 1.","<b>sed</b> finds and replaces: <code>sed 's/old/new/g' file</code>. With <code>-i</code> it changes the file itself.","<b>sort</b> orders lines. <b>uniq -c</b> counts repeated lines, but only lines that are <b>next to each other</b>, so always <code>sort</code> first."]}]},{"title":"Text tools","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Example: find the IP that made the most requests</b> 🧠"},{"type":"pre","text":"awk '{print $1}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -1"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<code>awk</code> takes the IP address from each line.","<code>sort</code> puts identical IPs next to each other.","<code>uniq -c</code> counts each IP.","<code>sort -rn</code> orders by count, highest first.","<code>head -1</code> shows the top one."]}]},{"title":"Installing software","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>sudo apt update</code> refreshes the list of available packages. It installs nothing.","<code>sudo apt install nginx</code> installs a package.","“Unable to locate package” usually means you forgot <code>apt update</code>."]}]},{"title":"SSH","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"SSH gives you an encrypted terminal on another machine."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["You have a <b>key pair</b>: a <b>private key</b> (stays on your laptop, never shared) and a <b>public key</b> (placed on the server).","<code>ssh -i key.pem ec2-user@IP</code> logs in. The default user is <code>ec2-user</code> on Amazon Linux and <code>ubuntu</code> on","Ubuntu.","The error “permissions are too open” means other users can read your private key. Fix it with <code>chmod 400</code> <code>key.pem</code>."]}]},{"title":"SSH","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> the container runs Linux. When a task fails, its stdout and stderr appear in <b>CloudWatch Logs</b>. That’s the same idea as reading <code>/var/log</code> on a server."}]}]},{"id":"devops-3-bash-scripting","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"3","title":"Bash scripting","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>#!/bin/bash</code> · <code>chmod +x</code> · <code>./script.sh</code>","<code>name=\"x\"</code> (no spaces) · always quote: <code>\"$var\"</code> · <code>$(cmd)</code> captures output","<code>$1 $# \"$@\" $0 $?</code> · <code>[[ ]]</code> tests · <code>if … then … fi</code>","Exit codes: <b>0 ok</b> · 1 error · 127 not found","<code>set -euo pipefail</code> = stop on error · unset var = error · pipe fails if any part fails"]}]}]},{"title":"What a script is","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A Bash script is a text file of commands that run from top to bottom. It lets you repeat a task by running one file."},{"type":"pre","text":"#!/bin/bash\necho \"Hello\""}]},{"title":"What a script is","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["The first line (<b>shebang</b>) says “run this with Bash”.","Make it runnable: <code>chmod +x hello.sh</code>. Run it: <code>./hello.sh</code>.","<code>./</code> is needed because your current folder isn’t in <code>PATH</code> (the list of folders the shell searches for","commands)."]}]},{"title":"Variables","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Set one: <code>name=\"Ahmed\"</code>, with <b>no spaces</b> around <code>=</code>.","Read it: <code>$name</code> or <code>${name}</code>.","Double quotes insert the value: <code>\"Hello $name\"</code> → Hello Ahmed. Single quotes don’t: <code>'Hello $name'</code> prints <code>$name</code> literally.","<b>Always quote variables:</b> <code>\"$file\"</code>. If the value contains a space, an unquoted variable is split into two words.","Store a command’s output: <code>count=$(ls | wc -l)</code>."]}]},{"title":"Script arguments","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"When you run <code>./script.sh one two</code>:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>$1</code> = one, <code>$2</code> = two","<code>$#</code> = how many arguments (2)","<code>\"$@\"</code> = all arguments","<code>$0</code> = the script’s name","<code>$?</code> = the exit code of the last command"]}]},{"title":"Maths","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<code>$(( ))</code> does whole-number maths: <code>echo $((7 / 2))</code> gives 3 (no decimals). <code>%</code> is the remainder."}]},{"title":"Tests and if statements","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A test checks whether something is true. Use <code>[[ ]]</code> with spaces inside the brackets."},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Test</b>","<b>True when</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>-eq -ne -lt -le -gt -ge</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["number equal, not equal, less than, etc."]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>==</code> <code>!=</code>"]}]},{"title":"Tests and if statements","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","text is the same / different"],["<code>-z</code> / <code>-n</code>","text is empty / not empty"],["<code>-f</code> / <code>-d</code> / <code>-e</code>","is a file / is a directory / exists"],["<code>&amp;&amp;</code> / <code>\\|\\|</code> / <code>!</code>","and / or / not"]]}]},{"title":"Tests and if statements","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"if [[ $score -ge 90 ]]; then\n  echo \"Excellent\"\nelif [[ $score -ge 80 ]]; then\n  echo \"Good\"\nelse\n  echo \"Try again\"\nfi"},{"type":"p","html":"Every <code>if</code> and <code>elif</code> needs <code>then</code>. The block ends with <code>fi</code>."}]},{"title":"Loops","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"for name in alice bob; do echo \"$name\"; done      # once per item\nfor i in {1..5}; do echo \"$i\"; done               # a range of numbers\nwhile [[ $count -le 5 ]]; do ((count++)); done    # while a test is true"},{"type":"p","html":"<code>break</code> leaves the loop. <code>continue</code> skips to the next round."}]},{"title":"Functions","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"greet() {\n  local name=\"$1\"      # local = exists only inside the function\n  echo \"Hello $name\"\n}\ngreet Sam"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>echo</code> sends data out of a function; capture it with <code>result=$(greet Sam)</code>.","<code>return 1</code> sets a status code, not data."]}]},{"title":"Exit codes","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Every command finishes with a number:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>0</b> = success","<b>1</b> = general error","<b>2</b> = wrong usage","<b>126</b> = found but not executable","<b>127</b> = command not found","<b>130</b> = stopped with Ctrl+C"]}]},{"title":"Exit codes","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Pipelines and other scripts read these codes to decide whether to continue. A script should <code>exit 1</code> with a clear message when something is wrong. Send errors to stderr with <code>echo \"Error\" &gt;&amp;2</code>."}]},{"title":"Strict mode","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Put this near the top of every script:"},{"type":"pre","text":"set -euo pipefail"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>-e</code>: stop at the first command that fails.","<code>-u</code>: treat a variable that was never set as an error.","<code>-o pipefail</code>: a pipeline fails if <b>any</b> command in it fails, not just the last.","Add <code>-x</code> while debugging: it prints each command before it runs."]}]},{"title":"A safe script template","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"#!/bin/bash\nset -euo pipefail\nfile=\"${1:-}\"                 # first argument, or empty if missing\nif [[ -z \"$file\" ]]; then\n  echo \"Usage: $0 <file>\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\nif [[ ! -f \"$file\" ]]; then\n  echo \"Error: $file not found\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\necho \"Lines in $file: $(wc -l < \"$file\")\""}]},{"title":"A safe script template","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<code>${1:-}</code> gives an empty value when there’s no argument. Without it, <code>set -u</code> would stop the script before it"},{"type":"p","html":"could print the usage message."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> a GitHub Actions <code>run:</code> step is Bash. If any command returns a non-zero exit code, the step fails and the pipeline stops. That’s how a failed <code>docker build</code> stops a deployment."}]}]},{"id":"devops-4-networking","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"4","title":"Networking","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"modules/vpc"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"modules/security"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["IP = L3 · TCP/UDP = L4 · MAC = L2 · HTTP/DNS = L7 · All People Seem To Need Data Processing","TCP = handshake, reliable · UDP = fast, no guarantee","Addresses = 2^(32 − prefix) · /24 = 256 · /16 = 65,536 · <b>AWS usable = total − 5</b>","DNS: resolver → root → TLD → authoritative → cached for the TTL","<b>Timeout = blocked</b> (firewall/SG) · <b>refused = nothing listening</b>"]}]}]},{"title":"Addresses","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>IP address:</b> says where a device is on a network. IPv4 looks like <code>10.0.1.25</code> (32 bits, about 4.3 billion addresses). IPv6 looks like <code>2001:db8::1</code> (128 bits).","<b>MAC address:</b> the ID of the network card itself, such as <code>00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E</code>. Used only inside the local network.","<b>Port:</b> a number that says which program on the machine should receive the data. Examples: 22 SSH, 53 DNS, 80 HTTP, 443 HTTPS, 8081 (my Memos container)."]}]},{"title":"TCP and UDP","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>TCP</b>","<b>UDP</b>"],["Setup","three-way handshake first: SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK","none, it just sends"],["Reliability","resends lost data, keeps order","no guarantee"],["Speed","slower","faster"],["Used by","web, SSH, email","DNS lookups, video calls, gaming"]]}]},{"title":"The OSI model","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"The OSI model splits networking into seven layers. Each layer does one job."}]},{"title":"The OSI model","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Layer</b>","<b>Job</b>","<b>Examples</b>"],["7 Application","services that apps use","HTTP, DNS, SSH"],["6 Presentation","data format, encryption","JSON, TLS"],["5 Session","opens and closes sessions","sockets"],["4 Transport","delivery between ports","TCP, UDP"],["3 Network","addressing and routing between networks","IP, routers"],["2 Data link","delivery on the local network","MAC, switches"],["1 Physical","signals on the cable or radio","cables, Wi-Fi"]]}]},{"title":"The OSI model","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Memory line (7 down to 1): All People Seem To Need Data Processing."},{"type":"p","html":"When you send a request, each layer adds its own header around the data: TCP adds ports, IP adds IP addresses, Ethernet adds MAC addresses. This is called <b>encapsulation</b>. The receiver removes them in reverse order."}]},{"title":"Private IP ranges","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"These ranges are never routed on the public internet. They’re used inside private networks:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>10.0.0.0/8</code> (common for AWS VPCs)"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<code>172.16.0.0/12</code>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>192.168.0.0/16</code> (home routers)"]}]},{"title":"CIDR and subnetting","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"CIDR notation writes a range of IPs as a starting address and a number: <code>10.0.0.0/24</code>."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["The number after <code>/</code> is how many bits are fixed. The remaining bits can vary.","Number of addresses = <b>2^(32 − number)</b>."]}]},{"title":"CIDR and subnetting","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>CIDR</b>","<b>Addresses</b>"],["/32","1"],["/28","16 (smallest AWS subnet)"],["/24","256"],["/20","4,096"],["/16","65,536 (largest AWS VPC)"],["/0","all addresses (<code>0.0.0.0/0</code> = “anywhere”)"]]}]},{"title":"CIDR and subnetting","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["In a normal network, usable addresses = total − 2 (the network address and the broadcast address).","<b>In AWS, usable = total − 5.</b> AWS reserves the first four (network, VPC router, DNS, future use) and the last one.","Example: a /24 in AWS has 256 − 5 = <b>251</b> usable.","Splitting <code>10.0.0.0/24</code> into four /26 subnets (64 each) gives ones starting at .0, .64, .128 and .192."]}]},{"title":"DNS","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"DNS turns a name (tm.myegh.com) into an IP address. Computers connect using IPs, so every visit starts with a DNS lookup."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>How a lookup works:</b>"}]},{"title":"DNS","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Your computer checks its own cache and <code>/etc/hosts</code>.","It asks a <b>resolver</b> (usually run by your ISP or company).","If the resolver doesn’t have the answer, it asks a <b>root server</b>, which replies with where the <code>.com</code> servers are.","The resolver asks a <b>TLD server</b> (<code>.com</code>), which replies with the domain’s name servers.","The resolver asks the <b>authoritative name server</b> (for my domain, Route 53), which gives the real IP."]}]},{"title":"DNS","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":6,"items":["The resolver stores the answer for the record’s <b>TTL</b> (time to live, in seconds) and gives it to your computer."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Record types:</b>"}]},{"title":"DNS","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Type</b>","<b>Maps</b>"],["A","name → IPv4 address"],["AAAA","name → IPv6 address"],["CNAME","name → another name"],["MX","domain → mail server"],["TXT","free text (ownership checks, email rules)"],["NS","domain → its name servers"]]}]},{"title":"DNS","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"If you change a record and nothing seems to change, the old answer is still cached until its TTL runs out. Tools: <code>dig +short name</code>, <code>nslookup name</code>."}]},{"title":"Routing and NAT","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Routing:</b> each router has a <b>routing table</b> that says which way to send traffic for each destination network.","<b>NAT (Network Address Translation):</b> private IPs can’t be used on the internet. NAT replaces the private source IP with a public IP on the way out and reverses it on the way back. <b>PAT</b> is the version where many devices share one public IP, separated by port number.","NAT isn’t a firewall. You still need firewall rules."]}]},{"title":"Troubleshooting order","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Check from the bottom up:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Does the name resolve?</b> <code>dig +short name</code>","<b>Is the port reachable?</b> <code>nc -zv IP 22</code>"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Timeout</b> (it hangs): something is blocking the traffic, usually a firewall or security group.","<b>Connection refused:</b> the traffic reached the machine, but no program is listening on that port."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":3,"items":["<b>Is the service running?</b> <code>systemctl status name</code>, and <code>ss -tlnp</code> to see listening ports."]}]},{"title":"Troubleshooting order","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ No reply to <code>ping</code> doesn’t prove a machine is down. Many servers and AWS security groups block ping (ICMP)."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b>"}]},{"title":"Troubleshooting order","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>DNS:</b> Route 53 is authoritative for myegh.com; <code>tm.myegh.com</code> is an alias A record pointing at the ALB.","<b>HTTPS:</b> TCP port 443 (layer 4) carrying HTTP (layer 7). TLS ends at the ALB. Port 80 only returns a 301 redirect.","<b>CIDR:</b> the VPC is <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>, split into four <code>/24</code> subnets, each with 251 usable addresses in AWS.","<b>NAT:</b> tasks have private IPs and reach ECR and CloudWatch through the NAT gateway in their own AZ, on TCP 443 only."]}]},{"title":"Troubleshooting order","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<b>Debugging:</b> if the site times out, check SGs and routes. If the ALB returns 502, 503 or 504, the ALB was reached, but the task is refusing, missing or too slow (§13)."]}]}]},{"id":"devops-5-git","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"5","title":"Git","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Working dir → <code>add</code> → staging → <code>commit</code> → repo · commit ID = SHA","Pushed? <code>revert</code> · local? <code>reset --soft HEAD~1</code> · lost? <code>reflog</code> · pause? <code>stash</code>","<code>git diff</code> = vs staging · <code>--staged</code> = vs last commit","Rebase only your own branches · leaked secret: <b>revoke first</b>, then scrub the history","🔧 <code>.gitignore</code> line <code>memos</code> (meant for the binary) also hid <code>cmd/memos/</code> → commit it; anchor the pattern as <code>/memos</code>"]}]}]},{"title":"What Git does","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Git records every change to a project’s files, who made it and when. You can go back to any earlier version, and many people can work on the same code. In DevOps, Git holds application code, Terraform, Dockerfiles and pipeline files."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Git vs GitHub:</b> Git is the program on your computer. GitHub is a website that stores Git repositories and adds pull requests, reviews and Actions."}]},{"title":"How Git stores work","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Each <b>commit</b> is a snapshot of the whole project at that moment.","Every commit has a unique ID called a <b>SHA</b> (a 40-character hash of its contents).","A <b>branch</b> is a name that points to a commit and moves forward with each new commit.","<b>HEAD</b> shows which branch or commit you’re currently on.","Everything is stored in the hidden <code>.git</code> folder. Delete it and the history is gone."]}]},{"title":"The three areas","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Working directory:</b> the files you edit.","<b>Staging area:</b> changes you’ve chosen for the next commit (<code>git add</code>).","<b>Repository:</b> saved commits (<code>git commit</code>)."]},{"type":"p","html":"Saving a file doesn’t record it in Git. You must <code>add</code> and then <code>commit</code>."}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Command</b>","<b>Does</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git status</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Shows changed, staged and untracked files. Run it constantly."]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git add file</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Stages a file"],["Saves the staged changes as a commit"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git commit -m \"msg\"</code>","<code>git log --oneline --graph --all</code>"]}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Shows history as a compact tree"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git diff</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Working files vs the staging area"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git diff --staged</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Staging area vs the last commit (what you’re about to commit)"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git switch -c feature/x</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Creates a new branch and switches to it"]]}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git push -u origin feature/x</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Uploads the branch to GitHub"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git pull --rebase</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Gets new commits from GitHub and puts yours on top"]]}]},{"title":"Merging","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git merge feature</code> brings a branch’s commits into the current branch.","<b>Fast-forward:</b> if main hasn’t changed, Git just moves main forward. No new commit.","<b>Three-way merge:</b> if both branches changed, Git creates a merge commit.","<b>Conflict:</b> both branches changed the same lines. Git marks the file with <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>=======</code> and <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>. Edit it to the correct version, delete the markers, then <code>git add</code> and <code>git commit</code>. <code>git merge --abort</code> cancels."]}]},{"title":"Undoing","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Situation</b>","<b>Command</b>","<b>Effect</b>"],["Undo a commit already pushed","","Makes a new commit that reverses it. History is kept, so it’s"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git revert &lt;sha&gt;</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","safe for shared branches."],["Undo my last local commit, keep","Removes the commit; changes stay staged"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git reset --soft</code>"]}]},{"title":"Undoing","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["the changes"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>HEAD~1</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Undo and throw away the","Removes the commit and the changes"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git reset --hard</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["changes"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>HEAD~1</code>"]}]},{"title":"Undoing","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Put work aside without committing","<code>git stash</code> / <code>git</code>","Saves and restores uncommitted changes"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>stash pop</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Copy one commit from another"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git cherry-pick</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["branch","Applies just that commit"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>&lt;sha&gt;</code>"]}]},{"title":"Undoing","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Find a “lost” commit"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>git reflog</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Lists everywhere HEAD has been"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Rebase</b> replays your commits on top of the latest main, giving a straight line of history. Use it only on your own branches, never on branches other people already have."}]},{"title":"Secrets and .gitignore","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>.gitignore</code> lists files Git must never track: <code>.env</code>, <code>*.tfstate</code>, <code>.terraform/</code>, <code>node_modules/</code>.","If a secret is committed, deleting it in a new commit isn’t enough, because it stays in history."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Revoke the key immediately.</b>","Then remove it from history with <code>git filter-repo</code>."]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Bots scan GitHub for leaked keys within minutes."]}]},{"title":"Team workflow","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<code>git switch main</code> and <code>git pull</code>.","<code>git switch -c feature/name</code>.","Make small commits with clear messages.","<code>git push -u origin feature/name</code>.","Open a <b>pull request</b>, get it reviewed, then merge."]},{"type":"p","html":"Never commit straight to main in a team."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b>"}]},{"title":"Team workflow","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>app/.gitignore</code> has the line <code>memos</code>, meant for the compiled binary. With no slash, a pattern matches","<b>anywhere</b>, so it also ignored the folder <code>cmd/memos/</code>. <code>main.go</code> never reached GitHub and the build failed. Fix: commit the folder; the correct pattern is <code>/memos</code>. Debug with <code>git check-ignore -v</code> <code>app/cmd/memos/main.go</code>.","Images are tagged with the full <b>commit SHA</b>, and deploy.yml rejects anything that isn’t a 40-character SHA, so every running version maps to exactly one commit."]}]},{"title":"Team workflow","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["The build workflow only runs when <code>app/**</code>, the <code>Dockerfile</code> or <code>.dockerignore</code> change (a <code>paths:</code> filter), so a README edit doesn’t redeploy.","Improvement: conventional commit messages (<code>feat:</code>, <code>fix:</code>, <code>chore:</code>). Early commits such as “update” and “new file” say nothing about the change."]}]}]},{"id":"devops-6-docker","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"6","title":"Docker","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile","label":"Dockerfile (3 stages)"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile.single-stage","label":"Dockerfile.single-stage"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Dockerfile → build → <b>image</b> (read-only) → run → <b>container</b>","Container shares the host kernel (light, seconds) · VM has its own OS (heavier, stronger isolation)","<b>RUN = build time · CMD = each start</b> · EXPOSE only records the port · <code>:latest</code> ≠ newest","<b>Multi-stage:</b> build stage compiles, runtime stage ships only the program → smaller, faster, safer","Names resolve only on a user-defined network · <code>depends_on</code> = start order only"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["🔧 3 stages: Node → Go (<code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code>) → Alpine · user 1001 · <b>:8081</b> · 2.79 GB → 75.8 MB"]}]}]},{"title":"The problem Docker solves","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"An app needs a specific language version, libraries and settings. If a server has different versions, the app breaks (“it works on my machine”). Docker packages the app <b>together with</b> everything it needs, so it runs the same on any machine that has Docker."}]},{"title":"The key terms","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Dockerfile:</b> a text file of instructions for building an image.","<b>Image:</b> the built result: the app, its libraries and its settings, in one read-only package. It can’t be changed; to change it, you rebuild it.","<b>Container:</b> a running copy of an image. One image can run as many containers.","<b>Registry:</b> a place that stores images (Docker Hub, AWS ECR).","<b>Tag:</b> a version label on an image, such as <code>memos:abc1234</code>."]}]},{"title":"The key terms","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Order: Dockerfile → <code>docker build</code> → image → <code>docker push</code> to a registry → <code>docker run</code> → container."}]},{"title":"Containers vs virtual machines","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>Virtual machine</b>","<b>Container</b>"],["Contains","a full guest operating system plus the app","just the app and its libraries"],["Kernel","its own","shares the host’s kernel"],["Start-up","minutes","seconds"],["Size","large","small"],["Isolation","stronger","process-level"]]},{"type":"p","html":"In the cloud, containers usually run <b>on</b> virtual machines. With Fargate, AWS manages those machines for you."}]},{"title":"Dockerfile instructions","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Instruction</b>","<b>Does</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>FROM</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["The base image to start from. Always first."]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>WORKDIR</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Sets the folder for the following instructions"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>COPY</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Copies files from your computer into the image"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>RUN</code>"]}]},{"title":"Dockerfile instructions","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Runs a command <b>while building</b> the image (for example, installing packages)"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>ENV</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Sets an environment variable"],["<b>Records</b> which port the app uses. It doesn’t open or publish the port."]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>EXPOSE</code>","<code>CMD</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["The default command when a container <b>starts</b>. Can be overridden."]]}]},{"title":"Dockerfile instructions","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>ENTRYPOINT</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["A fixed start command; CMD then supplies its default arguments"]]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <b>RUN</b> happens once, at build time. <b>CMD</b> happens every time a container starts."}]},{"title":"Layers and caching","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Each instruction creates a <b>layer</b>.","Docker reuses (caches) layers that haven’t changed.","If one layer changes, it and <b>every layer after it</b> are rebuilt.","So copy the dependency file and install dependencies first, and copy the app code last. Then a code change doesn’t reinstall everything.","<code>.dockerignore</code> keeps files out of the build (<code>.git</code>, <code>.env</code>), which makes builds smaller and keeps secrets out","of the image."]}]},{"title":"Multi-stage builds","part":"1/6","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A multi-stage Dockerfile has more than one <code>FROM</code>."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Stage 1 (build):</b> a large image with compilers and build tools. It compiles the app.","<b>Stage 2 (runtime):</b> a small, clean image. It copies in <b>only the finished program</b> from stage 1.","Only the last stage becomes the final image."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>My real Dockerfile, annotated:</b>"}]},{"title":"Multi-stage builds","part":"2/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"# Stage 1: build the React frontend\nFROM node:22-alpine AS frontend\nWORKDIR /build/web\nRUN npm install --global pnpm@10.24.0\nCOPY app/web/package.json app/web/pnpm-lock.yaml ./   # dependency files first (cached layer)\nRUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile\nCOPY app/web/ ./                                       # then the code\nRUN pnpm release"}]},{"title":"Multi-stage builds","part":"3/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"# Stage 2: build the Go backend, with the frontend embedded\nFROM golang:1.25-alpine AS backend\nWORKDIR /build\nRUN apk add --no-cache git ca-certificates\nCOPY app/go.mod app/go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download                                    # cached until go.mod/go.sum change\nCOPY app/ ./\nCOPY --from=frontend /build/server/router/frontend/dist ./server/router/frontend/dist\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -tags netgo,osusergo -o /memos ./cmd/memos"}]},{"title":"Multi-stage builds","part":"4/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"# Stage 3: tiny runtime image\nFROM alpine:3.21 AS runtime\nRUN apk add --no-cache ca-certificates tzdata && \\\n    addgroup -S -g 1001 appuser && \\\n    adduser -S -D -u 1001 -G appuser appuser && \\\n    mkdir -p /var/opt/memos && chown appuser:appuser /var/opt/memos\nCOPY --from=backend /memos /usr/local/bin/memos\nWORKDIR /var/opt/memos\nENV MEMOS_PORT=8081 MEMOS_DATA=/var/opt/memos\nUSER 1001:1001                                         # not root\nEXPOSE 8081\nENTRYPOINT [\"memos\"]"}]},{"title":"Multi-stage builds","part":"5/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code> → a fully <b>static</b> binary that needs no C libraries.","<code>-ldflags=\"-s -w\"</code> removes debug symbols, so the binary is smaller.","<code>--frozen-lockfile</code> installs exactly the locked versions (a repeatable build)."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Why it matters:</b>"}]},{"title":"Multi-stage builds","part":"6/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A smaller image downloads faster, so deploys are faster.","Fewer tools and libraries inside means fewer security vulnerabilities.","The compiler isn’t shipped to production."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ The runtime stage must contain <b>everything</b> the program needs to run. If you leave out a library, the container fails at start-up."}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Command</b>","<b>Does</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>docker build -t name:tag .</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Builds an image from the Dockerfile in this folder"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>docker run -d -p 8080:8081 --name memos</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Starts a container in the background; host port 8080 → container port"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>img</code>"}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","8081"],["<code>docker ps</code> / <code>docker ps -a</code>","Running containers / all containers"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>docker logs -f name</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Shows the container’s output. <b>The first thing to check when it fails.</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>docker exec -it name sh</code>"]}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","Opens a shell inside a running container"],["<code>docker stop</code> / <code>docker rm</code> / <code>docker rmi</code>","Stop a container / delete a container / delete an image"],["<b>Command</b>","<b>Does</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>docker system prune</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Removes stopped containers, unused networks and dangling images"]]}]},{"title":"Everyday commands","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>-p HOST:CONTAINER</code> publishes a port. Without it, nothing outside can reach the container.","The app inside must listen on <code>0.0.0.0</code>, not <code>127.0.0.1</code>, or it can’t be reached from outside the container."]}]},{"title":"Docker networking","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>bridge</b> (default): a private network on the host.","<b>host:</b> the container uses the host’s network directly.","<b>none:</b> no network.","On a <b>user-defined network</b> (<code>docker network create mynet</code>), containers can reach each other <b>by container</b> <b>name</b>. On the default bridge they can’t."]}]},{"title":"Docker Compose","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A <code>docker-compose.yml</code> file describes several containers. <code>docker compose up -d</code> starts them all; <code>docker</code> <code>compose down</code> removes them."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Compose creates a network automatically, and each service name becomes its hostname."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <code>depends_on</code> only controls start order. It doesn’t wait until the database is ready to accept connections."}]},{"title":"Pushing to ECR","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"aws ecr get-login-password --region eu-west-1 | docker login -u AWS --password-stdin\n<account>.dkr.ecr.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com\ndocker tag memos:abc1234 <account>.dkr.ecr.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/memos:abc1234\ndocker push <account>.dkr.ecr.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/memos:abc1234"},{"type":"p","html":"The login password lasts 12 hours."},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <code>:latest</code> is only the default tag name. It doesn’t mean “newest”. Use the commit SHA as the tag so every image is unique and traceable."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b>"}]},{"title":"Pushing to ECR","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Result:</b> single-stage <b>2.79 GB</b> (633 MB content) → multi-stage <b>75.8 MB</b> (19.7 MB content), <b>about 97%</b> <b>smaller</b>, measured with <code>docker images</code> against <code>Dockerfile.single-stage</code> (kept in the repo so anyone can reproduce it).","<b>Layer caching:</b> <code>package.json</code> + lockfile and <code>go.mod</code> + <code>go.sum</code> are copied and installed <b>before</b> the code, so a code change doesn’t re-download every dependency.","<code>.dockerignore</code> excludes <code>.git</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>.env*</code>, <code>.terraform</code> and <code>*.tfstate</code>: a smaller build","context, and no secrets in the image."]}]},{"title":"Pushing to ECR","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<b>Security:</b> runs as <b>non-root user 1001</b>; the runtime image has no compiler, no shell tools beyond Alpine’s base, and no source code.","<b>Real fixes along the way:</b> the Go base image was older than <code>go.mod</code> needed (temporarily <code>GOTOOLCHAIN=auto</code>, now <code>golang:1.25</code>); an early build needed <code>libc6-compat</code>, until <code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code> removed the C dependency (possible because Memos’s SQLite driver, <code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>, is pure Go)."]}]},{"title":"Pushing to ECR","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <code>MEMOS_DATA=/var/opt/memos</code> is <b>inside the container</b> with no volume, so the SQLite database is lost whenever the task is replaced. That’s limitation #1 in §1."}]}]},{"id":"devops-7-aws-foundations-iam-and-ec2","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"7","title":"AWS foundations: IAM and EC2","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/iam/main.tf","label":"modules/iam"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/oidc.tf","label":"bootstrap/oidc.tf"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Region → AZs (separate data centres) → multi-AZ = high availability","<b>User</b> = long-term credentials · <b>Role</b> = assumed, temporary credentials · <b>Policy</b> = what’s allowed","<b>Explicit Deny &gt; Allow &gt; default deny</b> · least privilege","<b>Trust policy</b> = who can assume · <b>permission policy</b> = what the role can do","SCPs only limit, never grant · user data runs at <b>first boot only</b> · Spot = up to 90% off, 2-minute warning"]}]}]},{"title":"Regions and availability zones","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>region</b> is a geographic area with several AWS data centres, such as eu-west-1 (Ireland) or eu-west-2 (London).","An <b>availability zone (AZ)</b> is one or more separate data centres inside a region, with their own power and networking. Most regions have three. Names add a letter: eu-west-1a, eu-west-1b.","Running in <b>two or more AZs</b> means the app keeps working if one AZ fails. This is <b>high availability</b>.","<b>Edge locations</b> are smaller sites close to users, used by CloudFront to cache content."]}]},{"title":"Regions and availability zones","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["Most services are <b>regional</b>. IAM, Route 53 and CloudFront are <b>global</b>.","Choose a region by: legal data rules, distance to users, which services are available, and price."]}]},{"title":"IAM: who can do what","part":"1/6","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"IAM (Identity and Access Management) controls access to an AWS account."}]},{"title":"IAM: who can do what","part":"2/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Term</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>User</b>","One person or app, with long-term credentials (a password and/or access keys)"],["<b>Group</b>","A set of users who share permissions. Attach permissions to groups, not individuals."],["<b>Role</b>","An identity that has <b>no password and no keys</b>. A person or service <b>assumes</b> it and gets <b>temporary credentials</b>."],["<b>Policy</b>","A JSON document listing what is allowed or denied"]]}]},{"title":"IAM: who can do what","part":"3/6","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>A policy has:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Effect</code>: Allow or Deny","<code>Action</code>: what can be done, e.g. <code>s3:GetObject</code>, <code>ecr:BatchGetImage</code>","<code>Resource</code>: which resources, as <b>ARNs</b> (Amazon Resource Names, the unique ID of a resource)","<code>Condition</code> (optional): when, e.g. only with MFA"]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>How AWS decides:</b>"}]},{"title":"IAM: who can do what","part":"4/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Everything is <b>denied by default</b>.","An <b>Allow</b> grants access.","An <b>explicit Deny always wins</b> over any Allow."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Two kinds of policy on a role:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Trust policy:</b> says <b>who is allowed to assume</b> the role. It has a <code>Principal</code>.","<b>Permission policy:</b> says <b>what the role can do</b> once assumed."]}]},{"title":"IAM: who can do what","part":"5/6","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Least privilege:</b> give only the permissions that are actually needed. Avoid <code>*</code> outside labs."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Account safety:</b> put MFA on the root user, create a budget alert, then stop using root. Services should use <b>roles</b>, never hard-coded access keys. Access keys belong only on the CLI or SDK, kept secret and rotated."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>At company scale:</b>"}]},{"title":"IAM: who can do what","part":"6/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>IAM Identity Center (SSO):</b> people sign in once through the company login and get access to only the accounts they need.","<b>AWS Organizations:</b> manages many AWS accounts together, grouped into OUs (for example Dev, Staging, Prod).","<b>SCPs (service control policies):</b> limits on what anyone in an account can do, even admins. An SCP <b>never</b> <b>grants</b> permissions; it only limits them."]}]},{"title":"The AWS CLI","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>aws configure</code> stores access keys and the default region in <code>~/.aws/</code>.","<code>aws sts get-caller-identity</code> shows which identity you’re using. It’s the quickest check that credentials","work."]}]},{"title":"EC2 (virtual servers)","part":"1/8","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"EC2 rents virtual machines called <b>instances</b>. You control the OS and software."}]},{"title":"EC2 (virtual servers)","part":"2/8","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Instance type</b>, e.g. <code>t3.micro</code> or <code>m5.2xlarge</code>: the letter is the family (T = burstable and cheap, M = general, C = compute, R = memory, G/P = GPU), the number is the generation, and the size is how much CPU and RAM.","<b>AMI:</b> the image an instance is launched from (the OS and software).","<b>User data:</b> a script that runs <b>once, at first boot</b>, as root. Used to install software automatically.","<b>Key pair:</b> needed for SSH.","<b>Security group:</b> the instance’s firewall (§8)."]}]},{"title":"EC2 (virtual servers)","part":"3/8","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Pricing:</b>"},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Option</b>","<b>How it works</b>","<b>Saving</b>"],["On-Demand","pay per second, no commitment","none"],["Reserved / Savings Plans","commit for 1 or 3 years","up to ~72%"],["Spot","spare capacity AWS can take back with 2 minutes’ warning","up to ~90%"],["Dedicated","hardware not shared with other customers","most expensive"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Storage:</b>"}]},{"title":"EC2 (virtual servers)","part":"4/8","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>EBS:</b> a network disk attached to one instance, in one AZ. Data survives a stop.","<b>EFS:</b> a shared file system many instances can use at once, across AZs.","<b>Instance store:</b> fast local disk; data is lost when the instance stops.","<b>Snapshot:</b> a backup of an EBS volume."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Connecting:</b> SSH (port 22 + key) · EC2 Instance Connect (in the browser) · <b>Session Manager</b> (no port 22 and no keys; needs the SSM agent and an IAM role; the most secure)."}]},{"title":"EC2 (virtual servers)","part":"5/8","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>IP addresses:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>private IP</b> works only inside the VPC and stays for the instance’s life.","A <b>public IP</b> usually changes after a stop and start.","An <b>Elastic IP</b> is a fixed public IP. AWS now charges for every public IPv4 address, so release Elastic IPs you’re not using."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b>"}]},{"title":"EC2 (virtual servers)","part":"6/8","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Region <b>eu-west-1</b>, <b>two AZs</b>. I use <b>Fargate</b>, not EC2, so there are no servers to manage.","<b>Three roles:</b>"]}]},{"title":"EC2 (virtual servers)","part":"7/8","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<code>github-actions-role</code> (in bootstrap). Trust policy: <code>Principal.Federated</code> = the GitHub OIDC provider, <code>Action</code> = <code>sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code>, conditions <code>aud</code> = <code>sts.amazonaws.com</code> (StringEquals) and <code>sub</code> like <code>repo:EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra:*</code> (StringLike). Permissions: ⚠️ <code>AdministratorAccess</code>, which is too broad. The fix is least privilege.","<code>memos-ecs-execution-role</code>: trusted by <code>ecs-tasks.amazonaws.com</code>, with the AWS managed policy <code>AmazonECSTaskExecutionRolePolicy</code> (pull from ECR, write CloudWatch logs)."]}]},{"title":"EC2 (virtual servers)","part":"8/8","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":3,"items":["<code>memos-ecs-task-role</code>: trusted by <code>ecs-tasks.amazonaws.com</code>, with <b>no policies</b>, because Memos doesn’t call AWS APIs. That’s least privilege done right."]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>default_tags</code> in the provider tags every resource with <code>Project = Memos</code> and <code>ManagedBy = Terraform</code>."]}]}]},{"id":"devops-8-vpc-networking","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"8","title":"VPC networking","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"modules/vpc"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"modules/security"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Public subnet = its route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 → IGW.</b> That’s the only difference.","Private → <b>NAT gateway</b> (outbound only) · for HA, one NAT per AZ","<b>SG:</b> per resource, stateful, allow only · <b>NACL:</b> per subnet, stateless, allow + deny, numbered","Can’t connect? Check the <b>SG, NACL and route table</b>","Endpoints: <b>gateway</b> (S3, DynamoDB, free) · <b>interface</b> (ECR etc., paid)"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["🔧 10.0.0.0/16 · 2 public + 2 private /24s · <b>2 NAT gateways (one per AZ)</b> · one private route table per AZ"]}]}]},{"title":"What a VPC is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A <b>VPC (Virtual Private Cloud)</b> is a private network in AWS. You choose its IP range (for example <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>) and decide how traffic enters and leaves."}]},{"title":"Subnets","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>subnet</b> is a smaller IP range inside the VPC, and it belongs to <b>one AZ</b>.","To be highly available, create subnets in at least two AZs.","A subnet is <b>public</b> or <b>private</b> only because of its <b>route table</b>:","<b>Public subnet:</b> its route table sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> (all non-local traffic) to the <b>internet gateway</b>.","<b>Private subnet:</b> it doesn’t. It sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to a <b>NAT gateway</b>, or has no internet route at all."]}]},{"title":"Route tables","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A route table is a list of rules: “traffic for this destination goes to this target”."},{"type":"pre","text":"Public route table             Private route table\n10.0.0.0/16  → local           10.0.0.0/16  → local\n0.0.0.0/0    → igw-xxxx        0.0.0.0/0    → nat-xxxx"},{"type":"p","html":"Every route table has a <code>local</code> route so resources inside the VPC can reach each other. Each subnet is linked to exactly one route table."}]},{"title":"Internet gateway (IGW)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Connects the VPC to the internet. One per VPC."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ Attaching an IGW does nothing on its own. A subnet only has internet access when its route table points <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> at the IGW <b>and</b> its resources have public IPs."}]},{"title":"NAT gateway","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Lets resources in <b>private</b> subnets start connections <b>out</b> to the internet (to download updates or pull images).","Blocks connections that start from the internet.","Lives in a <b>public</b> subnet and uses an Elastic IP.","Billed per hour and per GB of data, even when idle.","It works in one AZ. <b>For full high availability, use one NAT gateway per AZ.</b> If there’s only one and its AZ fails, private subnets in the other AZ lose outbound internet."]}]},{"title":"Security groups and NACLs","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Both are firewalls, working at different levels."}]},{"title":"Security groups and NACLs","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>Security group (SG)</b>","<b>Network ACL (NACL)</b>"],["Protects","one resource (its network interface)","a whole subnet"],["Rule types","<b>allow only</b>","allow and deny"],["State","<b>stateful:</b> if a request is allowed in, the reply is allowed out automatically","<b>stateless:</b> you must allow traffic in both directions, including the reply ports 1024–65535"],["Rule order","all rules are checked together","rules are checked in number order; the first match wins"]]}]},{"title":"Security groups and NACLs","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>Security group (SG)</b>","<b>Network ACL (NACL)</b>"],["Default","inbound all blocked, outbound all allowed","default NACL allows everything; a new custom NACL denies everything"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Referencing a security group:</b> a rule’s source can be another security group instead of an IP. “Allow port 8081 from the ALB’s security group” lets the ALB reach every task, however many there are and whatever their IPs."}]},{"title":"Security groups and NACLs","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"When something can’t connect, check three things: <b>security group, NACL, route table.</b>"}]},{"title":"VPC endpoints","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Without endpoints, private resources reach AWS services (S3, ECR) through the NAT gateway and the internet. A <b>VPC endpoint</b> keeps that traffic inside AWS’s network."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Gateway endpoint:</b> only for S3 and DynamoDB. Added as a route. <b>Free.</b>","<b>Interface endpoint:</b> most other services (ECR, Secrets Manager, CloudWatch Logs). Creates a network interface with a private IP in your subnet. <b>Paid.</b>"]},{"type":"p","html":"Endpoints can lower NAT costs and keep traffic private."}]},{"title":"Other VPC terms","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Bastion host:</b> a small server in a public subnet used to SSH into private servers. Session Manager is the modern replacement.","<b>VPC peering:</b> connects two VPCs privately. Their IP ranges must not overlap, and it’s <b>not transitive</b> (A↔︎B and B↔︎C does not give A↔︎C).","<b>Transit Gateway:</b> a central hub that connects many VPCs."]}]},{"title":"Building a VPC from scratch (order)","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Create the VPC with a CIDR range.","Create public and private subnets in at least two AZs.","Create and attach an internet gateway.","Public route table: <code>0.0.0.0/0 → IGW</code>, linked to the public subnets.","Create a NAT gateway (with an Elastic IP) in a public subnet.","Private route table: <code>0.0.0.0/0 → NAT</code>, linked to the private subnets.","Create the security groups."]}]},{"title":"Building a VPC from scratch (order)","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Delete in reverse order: instances → NAT gateway → Elastic IP → IGW → subnets and route tables → VPC."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos (the vpc and security modules):</b>"}]},{"title":"Building a VPC from scratch (order)","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["VPC <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>, with DNS support and DNS hostnames on.","Public <code>10.0.1.0/24</code> (1a) and <code>10.0.2.0/24</code> (1b); private <code>10.0.3.0/24</code> (1a) and <code>10.0.4.0/24</code> (1b). <code>map_public_ip_on_launch = false</code> everywhere.","One IGW, and one public route table (<code>0.0.0.0/0 → IGW</code>) shared by both public subnets."]}]},{"title":"Building a VPC from scratch (order)","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Two NAT gateways</b>, one in each public subnet, each with its own Elastic IP, and <b>one private route table per</b> <b>AZ</b> pointing at the NAT gateway in the same AZ. If one AZ fails, the other AZ’s private subnet keeps its outbound internet. That’s the HA pattern from this section, and it costs two NAT gateways.","Security group rules are separate resources (<code>aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule</code> / <code>egress_rule</code>) that reference each other: ALB out to ECS on 8081, ECS in from the ALB on 8081, ECS out on 443 only."]}]},{"title":"Building a VPC from scratch (order)","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["Default NACLs, no VPC endpoints, no flow logs (cost, documented). Improvement: interface endpoints for ECR and CloudWatch Logs plus a gateway endpoint for S3 would cut NAT data charges.","Built with <code>count</code> over the CIDR lists, giving <code>aws_subnet.public[0]</code> and <code>aws_subnet.public[1]</code>."]}]}]},{"id":"devops-9-load-balancing-scaling-and-dns-on-aws","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"9","title":"Load balancing, scaling and DNS on AWS","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/alb/main.tf","label":"modules/alb"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/acm/main.tf","label":"modules/acm"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf (Route 53 record)"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Vertical = bigger machine · horizontal = more machines · HA = survive failure","<b>ALB</b> L7 (HTTP, paths, containers) · <b>NLB</b> L4 (speed, static IPs) · GWLB L3 (appliances)","Listener → rules → <b>target group</b> + health check (200 = healthy)","TLS ends at the ALB with <b>ACM</b> · client IP in <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> · draining 300 s","<b>Alias record:</b> works at the domain root, free, points to AWS resources (a CNAME can’t)"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["🔧 :80 → 301 → :443 (ACM, TLS 1.3 policy) → TG (<code>ip</code>, 8081, health check <code>/</code>, 200–399) · alias A record"]}]}]},{"title":"Scaling terms","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Vertical scaling (scale up):</b> a bigger machine (more CPU and RAM). There’s a maximum size.","<b>Horizontal scaling (scale out):</b> more machines sharing the work.","<b>Elasticity:</b> scaling out and back in automatically as demand changes.","<b>High availability (HA):</b> the app keeps running when part of the system fails, usually by running in several AZs. A brief interruption during failover is allowed.","<b>Fault tolerance:</b> no interruption at all when a part fails. Stricter and more expensive than HA."]}]},{"title":"Scaling terms","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Availability</b>","<b>Downtime allowed per year</b>"],["99.9%","about 8.8 hours"],["99.99%","about 53 minutes"]]}]},{"title":"What a load balancer does","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A load balancer receives all user requests and sends each one to a healthy backend."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["It gives users one address instead of many server IPs.","It spreads requests across servers.","It runs <b>health checks</b> and stops sending traffic to unhealthy servers.","It can handle HTTPS so the servers don’t have to (<b>TLS termination</b>).","It can span several AZs."]}]},{"title":"Types of load balancer","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Type</b>","<b>Layer</b>","<b>Understands</b>","<b>Use for</b>"],["<b>ALB</b> (Application)","7","HTTP: paths, hostnames, headers","web apps, containers, microservices"],["<b>NLB</b> (Network)","4","TCP/UDP/TLS connections only","very high speed, low latency, static IPs"],["<b>GWLB</b> (Gateway)","3","IP packets","routing traffic through firewall appliances"]]}]},{"title":"How an ALB is put together","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Listener:</b> a port and protocol the ALB accepts, e.g. HTTPS on 443.","<b>Rules:</b> checked in priority order. A rule can <b>forward</b> to a target group, <b>redirect</b> (e.g. HTTP 80 → HTTPS 443), or return a <b>fixed response</b>.","<b>Target group:</b> the backends to send traffic to (EC2 instances, <b>IP addresses</b>, or Lambda functions) plus the <b>health check</b> settings."]}]},{"title":"How an ALB is put together","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":4,"items":["<b>Health check:</b> the ALB regularly requests a path (e.g. <code>/healthz</code>) on each target. A reply with a code in the configured range (by default 200; mine accepts 200–399) means healthy. Anything else, or no reply, counts as a failure; after enough failures the target is marked unhealthy and gets no traffic."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Other ALB details:</b>"}]},{"title":"How an ALB is put together","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>TLS certificate:</b> comes from <b>ACM</b> (free; renews automatically).","<b>SNI:</b> lets one HTTPS listener hold several certificates for different domains.","The app sees the ALB’s IP, not the user’s. The user’s real IP is in the <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> header.","<b>Deregistration delay</b> (connection draining): when a target is removed, the ALB stops sending new requests but lets current ones finish. Default 300 seconds."]}]},{"title":"How an ALB is put together","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<b>Sticky sessions:</b> send one user to the same target every time. It’s usually better to store sessions in a shared database so any target can serve anyone."]}]},{"title":"Security groups with a load balancer","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>ALB SG:</b> allow 80/443 from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>.","<b>App SG:</b> allow the app port <b>only from the ALB SG</b>.","Result: the internet can reach the ALB, and only the ALB can reach the app."]}]},{"title":"Auto Scaling","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["An <b>Auto Scaling Group (ASG)</b> keeps a number of EC2 instances between a <b>minimum</b> and a <b>maximum</b>, aiming for a <b>desired</b> count. It replaces unhealthy instances.","It uses a <b>launch template</b> (AMI, instance type, SG, user data, IAM role).","<b>Scaling policies:</b>","<b>Target tracking:</b> “keep average CPU at 40%”. AWS adds or removes capacity to stay there.","<b>Step:</b> “if CPU &gt; 70%, add 2”.","<b>Scheduled:</b> scale at set times."]}]},{"title":"Auto Scaling","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":7,"items":["<b>Predictive:</b> AWS learns patterns and scales ahead of them.","For ECS, the same idea is <b>ECS Service Auto Scaling</b>, which changes the <b>number of tasks</b>."]}]},{"title":"Route 53","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Route 53 is AWS’s DNS service. It can also register domains."}]},{"title":"Route 53","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Hosted zone:</b> holds all the DNS records for one domain.","<b>Alias record:</b> a Route 53 feature. It’s an A or AAAA record that points to an AWS resource (ALB, CloudFront, S3 website).","It works at the <b>zone apex</b> (the bare domain, <code>example.com</code>). A CNAME can’t.","Queries to AWS targets are free.","It follows the resource automatically if its IPs change.","<b>Routing policies</b> decide which answer DNS gives:","<b>Simple:</b> one answer."]}]},{"title":"Route 53","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":8,"items":["<b>Weighted:</b> split traffic by percentage (useful for a canary release).","<b>Latency:</b> send users to the fastest region for them.","<b>Failover:</b> a primary, plus a backup if the primary’s health check fails.","<b>Geolocation:</b> by the user’s country or continent.","<b>Geoproximity:</b> by distance, adjustable with a bias.","<b>Multi-value:</b> up to 8 healthy answers."]}]},{"title":"Route 53","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":14,"items":["<b>Registrar vs DNS host:</b> you can buy a domain from one company and host its DNS in Route 53 by setting the domain’s NS records to Route 53’s name servers.","<b>TTL tip:</b> lower the TTL a day before a migration so the change spreads quickly."]}]},{"title":"CloudFront","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"CloudFront is AWS’s <b>CDN</b> (content delivery network). It stores copies of content at edge locations near users so pages load faster and the origin server does less work."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Cache hit:</b> the edge already has the file and serves it.","<b>Cache miss:</b> the edge fetches it from the <b>origin</b> (S3 or an ALB), serves it, and stores it.","For an S3 origin, use <b>OAC (Origin Access Control)</b> so only CloudFront can read the bucket."]}]},{"title":"CloudFront","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>HTTP listener (80):</b> redirects to 443 with a <b>301</b>.","<b>HTTPS listener (443):</b> the ACM certificate, the <code>ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06</code> policy (TLS 1.3 and 1.2 only), forwarding to the target group. <code>drop_invalid_header_fields = true</code>.","<b>Target group:</b> type <code>ip</code>, HTTP on 8081. Health check: path <code>/</code>, matcher <code>200-399</code>, every 30 s, 5 s timeout, 2 checks to become healthy or unhealthy. ⚠️ It should use <code>/healthz</code>."]}]},{"title":"CloudFront","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>ACM:</b> a certificate for <code>myegh.com</code>, with <code>tm.myegh.com</code> as an extra name (SAN). Terraform creates the DNS validation records and waits for validation. <code>create_before_destroy</code> means a replacement certificate exists before the old one is removed.","<b>Route 53:</b> the existing hosted zone is read with <code>data \"aws_route53_zone\"</code>; an alias A record <code>tm.myegh.com</code> → the ALB, with <code>evaluate_target_health = true</code>.","No auto scaling and only one task. Both are stated future improvements."]}]}]},{"id":"devops-10-containers-and-serverless-on-aws","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"10","title":"Containers and serverless on AWS","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile","label":"Dockerfile (3 stages)"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile.single-stage","label":"Dockerfile.single-stage"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"modules/ecs"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"modules/ecr"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Cluster → <b>service</b> (keeps N tasks running) → <b>task</b> (a running <b>task definition</b>)","<b>Fargate:</b> no servers · <code>awsvpc</code> networking → each task has its own IP → <b>TG type</b> <code>ip</code>","<b>Execution role</b> = pull from ECR, logs, secrets · <b>task role</b> = the app’s AWS calls","ECS default = rolling update · rollback = previous SHA","Secrets → <b>Secrets Manager</b> / SSM Parameter Store, never in code"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["Lambda: event-driven, <b>15 min max</b> · 6 Well-Architected pillars","🔧 1 task · 0.25 vCPU / 512 MB · task role has no permissions · logs <code>/ecs/memos</code> (7 days) · ECR immutable + scan on push"]}]}]},{"title":"Why container orchestration","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Running one container by hand is easy. Running many, across many machines, needs a system that decides where each container runs, restarts failed ones, changes the count, and connects them to load balancers. That system is an <b>orchestrator</b>. On AWS the main ones are <b>ECS</b> and <b>EKS</b>."}]},{"title":"ECS terms","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Term</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>Cluster</b>","A logical group that tasks run in"],["<b>Task</b> <b>definition</b>","The settings for a task: image, CPU, memory, port, environment variables, IAM roles, logging"],["<b>Task</b>","One running copy of a task definition (one or more containers)"],["<b>Service</b>","Keeps a set number of tasks running, replaces failed ones, and registers them with the target group. Also handles rolling deployments."]]}]},{"title":"Launch types: EC2 vs Fargate","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>EC2 launch type</b>","<b>Fargate</b>"],["Servers","you create and maintain EC2 instances","none to manage"],["Scaling","scale both tasks and instances","scale tasks only"],["Pay for","the EC2 instances","vCPU and memory per task"],["Choose when","you need special hardware or tight cost control","the default for new work: simpler"]]}]},{"title":"Launch types: EC2 vs Fargate","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Fargate networking:</b> each Fargate task uses <code>awsvpc</code> <b>mode</b>, so it gets its own network interface and private IP in your subnet. Because the ALB sends traffic to that IP, the target group’s type must be <code>ip</code>, not <code>instance</code>."}]},{"title":"ECS IAM roles","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Task execution role:</b> used by <b>ECS itself</b> to start the task: pull the image from ECR, write logs to CloudWatch, and read secrets from Secrets Manager or Parameter Store. Fargate needs it.","<b>Task role:</b> used by <b>the application</b> inside the container when it calls AWS services (for example, reading from S3).","If a task can’t pull its image, check the <b>execution role</b> first."]}]},{"title":"ECR","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Private image repositories, with access controlled by IAM.","<b>Image scanning</b> finds known vulnerabilities.","<b>Lifecycle policies</b> delete old images automatically.","If a pull fails, check IAM permissions and the network path (NAT or endpoints)."]}]},{"title":"Deployments on ECS","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["The default is a <b>rolling update</b>: ECS starts new tasks, waits for them to pass health checks, then stops the old ones.","<b>Rollback:</b> point the service back at the previous task definition, which uses the previous SHA image."]}]},{"title":"EKS","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>EKS</b> is AWS’s managed <b>Kubernetes</b>. AWS runs the Kubernetes control plane; you choose how the worker nodes run (managed node groups, self-managed nodes, or Fargate)."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>ECS:</b> simpler and AWS-only.","<b>EKS:</b> the open-source industry standard, portable across clouds, with a steeper learning curve."]}]},{"title":"Secrets for containers","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Secrets Manager:</b> for credentials such as database passwords. Supports rotation.","<b>SSM Parameter Store:</b> for configuration values (and secrets as SecureString).","The task definition references them, and the execution role reads them at start-up. They are never written in code or the image."]}]},{"title":"Serverless and Lambda","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"“Serverless” means you deploy code and AWS runs the servers. You never manage them."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Lambda:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Runs your code when an event triggers it (an S3 upload, an API request, a schedule).","Maximum run time: <b>15 minutes</b>.","Memory: 128 MB to 10 GB. More memory also gives more CPU.","You pay per request and per amount of compute time.","Scales automatically."]}]},{"title":"Serverless and Lambda","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>EC2 vs Lambda:</b> use EC2 (or containers) for long-running servers. Use Lambda for short, event-driven tasks."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Example patterns:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Image uploaded to S3 → triggers Lambda → creates a thumbnail → saves it to a <b>different</b> bucket. ⚠️ Saving to the same bucket triggers the function again, in an endless loop.","<b>EventBridge</b> schedule (e.g. every hour) → triggers Lambda → runs a backup."]}]},{"title":"Well-Architected Framework","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"AWS’s six pillars for good systems:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Operational excellence:</b> automate, monitor, make small changes.","<b>Security:</b> protect data, systems and accounts.","<b>Reliability:</b> keep working and recover from failures.","<b>Performance efficiency:</b> the right resources, scaled to demand.","<b>Cost optimisation:</b> don’t pay for what you don’t need.","<b>Sustainability:</b> use fewer resources."]}]},{"title":"Well-Architected Framework","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"In interviews, the best answer is often “it depends on the data, the users and the budget”, followed by a clear choice and the reason for it."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Security services:</b>"}]},{"title":"Well-Architected Framework","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>CloudTrail:</b> a log of every API call: who did what, when.","<b>GuardDuty:</b> detects threats from logs.","<b>Inspector:</b> scans EC2, ECR images and Lambda for vulnerabilities.","<b>AWS Config:</b> records resource settings and flags rule breaks.","<b>Security Hub:</b> collects findings from all of these in one place.","<b>Shift left:</b> check for security problems as early as possible, in pull requests and the pipeline (e.g. <b>Checkov</b> for Terraform, <b>Trivy</b> for images), not after deployment."]}]},{"title":"Well-Architected Framework","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos (the ecs and ecr modules):</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Cluster <code>memos-cluster</code>, with Container Insights <b>disabled</b> (cost, documented).","Task definition family <code>memos-task</code>: <code>FARGATE</code>, <code>awsvpc</code>, 256 CPU / 512 MB, one container named <code>memos</code> on port 8081, <code>awslogs</code> driver to <code>/ecs/memos</code> (7-day retention).","Service <code>memos-service</code>: <code>desired_count = 1</code>, private subnets, the ECS SG, <code>assign_public_ip = false</code>, attached to the target group on container port 8081."]}]},{"title":"Well-Architected Framework","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Deploying:</b> Terraform changes the image → a new task definition revision → an ECS rolling update. There’s no deployment circuit breaker yet.","<b>ECR</b> repository <code>memos</code>: <b>immutable</b> tags, <b>scan on push</b>, <code>force_delete = true</code>, AWS default encryption."]}]}]},{"id":"devops-11-terraform","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"11","title":"Terraform","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/provider.tf","label":"infra/provider.tf (S3 backend)"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/state.tf","label":"bootstrap/state.tf"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Code = WANT · State = KNOWS · AWS = EXISTS</b> · declarative · idempotent","<code>init → fmt → validate → plan → apply</code> · <code>+</code> create · <code>~</code> change · <code>-</code> destroy · <code>-/+</code> replace","State holds secrets: never in Git · S3 backend · <code>use_lockfile = true</code> (1.10+)","Drift = reality ≠ state → <code>plan</code> → fix in code · <code>moved</code> = no recreate · <code>import</code> = adopt","Precedence: default &lt; <code>TF_VAR</code> &lt; tfvars &lt; auto.tfvars &lt; <code>-var</code>"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["<b>for_each &gt; count</b> for changing lists · modules connect output → input","🔧 bootstrap stack (state bucket + OIDC) + 7 modules: vpc, security, acm, alb, ecr, iam, ecs · <code>prod/terraform.tfstate</code> · <code>use_lockfile</code>"]}]}]},{"title":"What Terraform is","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Terraform is an <b>infrastructure as code (IaC)</b> tool. You write files describing the infrastructure you want (VPCs, subnets, load balancers), and Terraform creates it through the cloud provider’s API."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Why use code instead of clicking in the console:</b>"}]},{"title":"What Terraform is","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["The same code creates the same infrastructure every time.","The code lives in Git, so changes are reviewed and can be rolled back.","<code>plan</code> shows exactly what will change before anything happens.","Dev and prod can be built from the same code with different values."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Key words:</b>"}]},{"title":"What Terraform is","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Declarative:</b> you describe the end result (“two subnets”), not the steps. Terraform works out the steps.","<b>Idempotent:</b> running the same code again gives the same result. Nothing is created twice. If nothing changed, nothing happens.","<b>Orchestration vs configuration management:</b> Terraform creates infrastructure. Tools like Ansible configure software on existing servers.","<b>Cloud-agnostic:</b> one tool and language (HCL) for AWS, Azure, GCP and others, through <b>providers</b>."]}]},{"title":"Three things to keep apart","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Code (</b><code>.tf</code> <b>files):</b> what I <b>want</b>.","<b>State (</b><code>terraform.tfstate</code><b> ):</b> what Terraform <b>knows</b> it manages.","<b>The real cloud:</b> what actually <b>exists</b>."]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>terraform plan</code> compares these and lists the differences."}]},{"title":"Providers","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A <b>provider</b> is a plugin that lets Terraform talk to a platform’s API."}]},{"title":"Providers","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"terraform {\n  required_providers {\n    aws = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/aws\"\n      version = \"~> 5.0\"     # any 5.x version, not 6 (what Memos uses)\n    }\n  }\n}\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  region = \"eu-west-1\"\n}"}]},{"title":"Providers","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Credentials come from the environment (environment variables, an AWS profile, or OIDC in a pipeline), never from the code."}]},{"title":"Resources","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_ecr_repository\" \"memos\" {\n  name         = \"memos\"\n  force_delete = true\n}"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>aws_ecr_repository</code> = the resource <b>type</b> (defined by the provider).","<code>memos</code> = my <b>name</b> for it.","Its <b>address</b> is <code>aws_ecr_repository.memos</code>.","Values are referenced as <code>type.name.attribute</code>, e.g. <code>aws_ecr_repository.memos.repository_url</code>."]}]},{"title":"The workflow","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Command</b>","<b>Does</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform init</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Sets up the backend, downloads providers and modules, writes the lock file. Run first, and again after adding providers or modules or changing the backend."]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform fmt</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Formats the code to the standard style"]]}]},{"title":"The workflow","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Checks the syntax and references"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>validate</code>"},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Shows what will change. Changes nothing."]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform plan</code>","<code>terraform</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Shows the plan, asks for <code>yes</code>, makes the changes, updates state"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>apply</code>"}]},{"title":"The workflow","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Command</b>","<b>Does</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Deletes everything this code manages"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>destroy</code>"},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Reading a plan:</b>"}]},{"title":"The workflow","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>+</code> will be created","<code>~</code> will be changed in place","<code>-</code> will be destroyed","<code>-/+</code> will be destroyed and recreated (replaced)"]},{"type":"p","html":"The summary line reads like <code>Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.</code> ⚠️ Always check the “to destroy” number. A <code>-/+</code> on a database or a bucket with data needs stopping and checking."}]},{"title":"The workflow","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Pipeline pattern:</b> <code>terraform plan -out=tfplan</code>, review it, then <code>terraform apply tfplan</code> applies exactly what was reviewed."}]},{"title":"State","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["The state file maps each resource in the code to a real resource ID in AWS."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ State can contain <b>secrets in plain text</b>. Never commit it to Git. Add <code>*.tfstate</code> to <code>.gitignore</code>."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Do</b> commit <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code>, which pins exact provider versions for the whole team."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Local vs remote state:</b>"}]},{"title":"State","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Local:</b> a file on your laptop. Fine for learning; lost if the laptop dies; can’t be shared.","<b>Remote (backend):</b> stored centrally, e.g. in an S3 bucket. Everyone and every pipeline uses the same state."]}]},{"title":"State","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"terraform {\n  backend \"s3\" {                               # my real backend (infra/provider.tf)\n    bucket       = \"memos-terraform-state-enamul\"\n    key          = \"prod/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region       = \"eu-west-1\"\n    encrypt      = true\n    use_lockfile = true      # S3 native locking (Terraform 1.10+)\n  }\n}"}]},{"title":"State","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>State locking:</b> while one person or pipeline is applying, others are blocked. Without it, two applies at once can corrupt the state.","Since Terraform 1.10, <code>use_lockfile = true</code> locks using a file in the bucket. The older method used a <b>DynamoDB table</b> and is now deprecated.","Turn on <b>versioning</b> on the state bucket so old state can be recovered."]}]},{"title":"Drift","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Drift</b> is when the real infrastructure no longer matches the state, usually because someone changed it in the console."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform plan</code> shows the drift as changes.","Fix it through the code: either change the code to match, or apply to put the resource back.","<code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code> shows drift without proposing other changes."]}]},{"title":"Import and moved","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Import:</b> brings a resource that was built by hand under Terraform’s control without recreating it. Write an <code>import { to = ..., id = ... }</code> block, then plan until it shows no changes.","<code>moved</code> <b>block:</b> tells Terraform a resource’s address changed (for example, it was moved into a module), so it’s","renamed in state instead of destroyed and recreated."]}]},{"title":"Variables, locals and outputs","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Kind</b>","<b>Defined with</b>","<b>Used as</b>","<b>Purpose</b>"],["Input","","",""]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>variable \"x\" {}</code> <code>var.x</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["variable","Values passed in from outside"],["Local value",""]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>locals { x = ...</code> <code>local.x</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Values calculated inside the code and reused"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>}</code>"]}]},{"title":"Variables, locals and outputs","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Output"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>output \"x\" {}</code> <code>module.name.x</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Values passed out: shown after apply, or given to other modules"]]}]},{"title":"Variables, locals and outputs","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"variable \"env\" {\n  type        = string\n  description = \"Environment name\"\n  validation {\n    condition     = contains([\"dev\", \"prod\"], var.env)\n    error_message = \"env must be dev or prod.\"\n  }\n}"},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Where values come from, lowest to highest priority:</b> 🧠"}]},{"title":"Variables, locals and outputs","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<code>default</code> in the variable block","Environment variable <code>TF_VAR_name</code>","<code>terraform.tfvars</code>","<code>*.auto.tfvars</code>","<code>-var</code> or <code>-var-file</code> on the command line (always wins)"]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Types:</b> <code>string</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>list(...)</code>, <code>map(...)</code>, <code>object({...})</code>, <code>set(...)</code>."},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <code>sensitive = true</code> hides a value on screen only. It’s still stored in plain text in state."}]},{"title":"Modules","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A <b>module</b> is a folder of <code>.tf</code> files that does one job. The folder you run Terraform in is the <b>root module</b>; the modules it calls are <b>child modules</b>."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Why use modules:</b> reuse the same code in many places, keep large projects organised, and keep settings consistent between environments."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>A good module:</b>"}]},{"title":"Modules","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["does one job,","takes values as variables instead of hard-coding them,","outputs what callers need (IDs, ARNs),","is documented,","doesn’t contain the provider configuration."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Passing values:</b> variables are <b>not</b> shared automatically. Each module only sees what is passed into it. Modules connect by passing one module’s <b>output</b> into another module’s <b>input</b>:"}]},{"title":"Modules","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"# from my infra/main.tf\nmodule \"alb\" {\n  source          = \"./modules/alb\"\n  vpc_id          = module.vpc.vpc_id\n  public_subnets  = module.vpc.public_subnets              # output of vpc → input of alb\n  security_group  = module.security.alb_security_group_id\n  certificate_arn = module.acm.certificate_arn\n}"}]},{"title":"Modules","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"module \"ecs\" {\n  source             = \"./modules/ecs\"\n  private_subnet_ids = module.vpc.private_subnets\n  target_group_arn   = module.alb.target_group_arn\n  execution_role_arn = module.iam.execution_role_arn\n  task_role_arn      = module.iam.task_role_arn\n  image_url          = var.image_url != \"\" ? var.image_url : module.ecr.repository_url\n  # ...\n}"}]},{"title":"Modules","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Terraform works out the build order from these references: vpc first, then security, acm and the rest, and ecs last."}]},{"title":"Loops and conditions","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>count = 3</code><b> :</b> creates 3 copies, addressed <code>[0]</code>, <code>[1]</code>, <code>[2]</code>. ⚠️ Removing an item from the middle shifts the","later ones, which can cause them to be replaced.","<code>for_each</code><b> :</b> creates one copy per key of a map or set, addressed by key (<code>[\"dev1\"]</code>). Removing one affects","only that one. <b>Prefer it for lists that change.</b>","<b>Conditional:</b> <code>condition ? true_value : false_value</code>. <code>count = var.env == \"prod\" ? 1 : 0</code> creates a resource only in prod."]}]},{"title":"Loops and conditions","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["<code>dynamic</code> <b>block:</b> generates repeated nested blocks, such as several ingress rules in one security group.","<code>lifecycle</code><b> :</b> <code>prevent_destroy = true</code> (protect a database), <code>create_before_destroy = true</code> (no gap when","replacing), <code>ignore_changes = [tags]</code>.","<code>depends_on</code><b> :</b> forces an order Terraform can’t work out from references."]}]},{"title":"Useful functions","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<code>merge</code> (combine maps, e.g. tags) · <code>lookup</code> (map value with a default) · <code>coalesce</code> (first non-empty value) · <code>try</code>"},{"type":"p","html":"(fallback if an expression errors) · <code>concat</code> (join lists) · <code>join</code> / <code>split</code> · <code>jsonencode</code> (write IAM policies) · <code>file</code> / <code>templatefile</code> (read a file, e.g. a script)."}]},{"title":"How to build a Terraform project","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Get one resource working in a single file with plain values.","Understand every line.","Read the provider’s argument reference before adding anything.","Add variables, then split into modules, one step at a time, running <code>plan</code> after each step."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Two stacks:</b>"]}]},{"title":"How to build a Terraform project","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<code>bootstrap/</code>: run once, with local state. It creates the S3 state bucket (versioning, AES-256, public access block, HTTPS-only policy, <code>prevent_destroy</code>) and the GitHub OIDC provider and role.","<code>infra/</code>: the application, with state in S3 (<code>prod/terraform.tfstate</code>, <code>use_lockfile = true</code>)."]}]},{"title":"How to build a Terraform project","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Seven modules called from</b> <code>infra/main.tf</code><b> :</b> <code>vpc</code> → <code>security</code> (needs <code>vpc_id</code>) → <code>acm</code> (needs the zone ID) → <code>alb</code> (needs subnets, SG, certificate) → <code>ecr</code> → <code>iam</code> → <code>ecs</code> (needs subnets, SG, image, roles, target group). The DNS record is a resource in the root module. (A <code>route53</code> module exists in the folder but isn’t called.)","<b>Conditional:</b> <code>image_url = var.image_url != \"\" ? var.image_url : module.ecr.repository_url</code>. The pipeline passes the SHA-tagged image with <code>-var</code>.","<code>for</code> <b>expression +</b> <code>for_each</code><b> :</b> the ACM validation records are <code>for_each = { for option in</code>"]}]},{"title":"How to build a Terraform project","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<code>aws_acm_certificate.this.domain_validation_options : option.domain_name =&gt; {...} }</code>, addressed <code>[\"myegh.com\"]</code> and <code>[\"tm.myegh.com\"]</code>.","<code>count</code><b> :</b> subnets, NAT gateways, Elastic IPs, private route tables.","<code>lifecycle</code><b> :</b> <code>prevent_destroy</code> on the state bucket; <code>create_before_destroy</code> on the certificate.","<code>import</code> <b>blocks:</b> used once to adopt the existing ACM validation records, then removed.","<code>data</code> <b>sources:</b> the Route 53 zone and <code>aws_availability_zones</code>."]}]},{"title":"How to build a Terraform project","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":9,"items":["Values in <code>terraform.tfvars</code>: project <code>memos</code>, environment <code>prod</code>, region, domain, CIDRs, AZs.","<b>Clean-ups to admit if asked:</b> 8081 is hard-coded in the alb and security modules; the <code>availability_zones</code> input is passed but unused (the VPC module reads the AZs from a data source); and the IAM outputs file is misspelt <code>ouputs.tf</code> (it still works, because Terraform reads every <code>.tf</code> file)."]}]}]},{"id":"devops-12-ci-cd-with-github-actions","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"12","title":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/build.yml","label":"build.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":"deploy.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/destroy.yml","label":"destroy.yml"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["CI = build + test every change · Delivery = deployable with approval · Deployment = auto-release","Workflow → <code>on:</code> → jobs (own runner) → steps (<code>uses:</code> / <code>run:</code>)","<b>OIDC:</b> <code>id-token: write</code> → GitHub signed token → STS <b>AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</b> → trust policy checks <code>aud</code> + <code>sub</code> → <b>temporary credentials</b>","Rolling · blue/green · canary · rollback = previous SHA"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["🔧 build.yml (tfsec → OIDC → build or reuse SHA image) → deploy.yml (tfsec → plan/apply <code>image_url</code>) → curl /healthz · rollback = run deploy with an old SHA"]}]}]},{"title":"Definitions","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>CI (Continuous Integration):</b> developers merge changes often, and each change is automatically built and tested. Problems are found early, while changes are small.","<b>Continuous Delivery:</b> every change that passes the pipeline is ready to release. Release to production needs one manual approval.","<b>Continuous Deployment:</b> every change that passes the pipeline is released automatically, with no manual step."]}]},{"title":"How GitHub Actions is organised","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Workflow:</b> a YAML file in <code>.github/workflows/</code>. It’s the pipeline.","<b>Event (</b><code>on:</code><b> ):</b> what starts it: <code>push</code>, <code>pull_request</code>, <code>schedule</code> (cron) or <code>workflow_dispatch</code> (a manual button).","<b>Job:</b> a group of steps that runs on a fresh virtual machine called a <b>runner</b> (e.g. <code>ubuntu-latest</code>). Jobs run at the same time unless one says <code>needs: other-job</code>.","<b>Step:</b> one action (<code>uses: owner/repo@version</code>) or shell commands (<code>run:</code>)."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>From my real</b> <code>build.yml</code> <b>(trimmed):</b>"}]},{"title":"How GitHub Actions is organised","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"name: Build and Push\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n    paths: ['app/**', 'Dockerfile', '.dockerignore']   # only when the app changes\n  workflow_dispatch:\nenv:\n  AWS_REGION: eu-west-1\n  ECR_REPOSITORY: memos\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions:\n      id-token: write          # needed for OIDC\n      contents: read\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: aquasecurity/tfsec-action@v1.0.3   # scan the Terraform\n        with:\n          working_directory: infra\n      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4\n        with:\n          role-to-assume: ${{ secrets.AWS_ROLE_ARN }}\n          aws-region: ${{ env.AWS_REGION }}\n      - id: login-ecr\n        uses: aws-actions/amazon-ecr-login@v2\n      - name: Build or reuse image\n        env:\n          ECR_REGISTRY: ${{ steps.login-ecr.outputs.registry }}\n          IMAGE_TAG: ${{ github.sha }}\n        run: |\n          IMAGE=\"$ECR_REGISTRY/$ECR_REPOSITORY:$IMAGE_TAG\"\n          # if this SHA is already in ECR, reuse it; otherwise build and push"}]},{"title":"How GitHub Actions is organised","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>YAML rules:</b> indent with <b>spaces, never tabs</b>; there’s a space after every <code>:</code>; <b>quote version numbers</b> (<code>\"3.10\"</code>, or YAML reads it as 3.1)."}]},{"title":"How GitHub Actions is organised","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Expressions:</b> <code>${{ github.sha }}</code> (commit ID), <code>${{ github.ref_name }}</code> (branch), <code>${{ secrets.NAME }}</code> (hidden values), <code>${{ vars.NAME }}</code> (normal settings). <b>Conditions:</b> <code>if: success()</code>, <code>if: failure()</code>, <code>if:</code> <code>always()</code>, <code>if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'</code>. <b>Matrix:</b> runs the same job once per value, e.g. for several language versions. <b>Environments:</b> named targets (staging, production) with their own secrets. They can <b>require</b> <b>a reviewer’s approval</b> before a job runs. This is called <b>gating</b>."}]},{"title":"Secrets","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Stored in the repo settings and read with <code>${{ secrets.NAME }}</code>. GitHub shows them as <code>***</code> in logs.","Safer to pass them as environment variables than to paste them straight into a script line."]}]},{"title":"OIDC, step by step","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>The problem:</b> a pipeline needs AWS access. Storing long-term AWS access keys in GitHub is risky: they never expire, and if they leak, anyone can use them."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>The solution, OIDC (OpenID Connect):</b>"}]},{"title":"OIDC, step by step","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>One-time setup in AWS:</b> create an <b>IAM OIDC identity provider</b> for <code>token.actions.githubusercontent.com</code>, and an <b>IAM role</b> whose <b>trust policy</b> says: “allow tokens from that provider, with audience <code>sts.amazonaws.com</code>, for subject <code>repo:Owner/Repo:...</code>”.","The workflow has <code>permissions: id-token: write</code>.","When the job runs, GitHub creates a short-lived, <b>signed token</b> that states which repository and branch the run is for.","The <code>configure-aws-credentials</code> action sends that token to <b>AWS STS</b> using <b>AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</b>."]}]},{"title":"OIDC, step by step","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["AWS checks the token’s signature and checks it against the trust policy’s conditions.","If it matches, STS returns <b>temporary credentials</b> for the role, valid for a limited time.","The rest of the job uses those credentials. Nothing secret is stored in GitHub."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ Say “temporary <b>credentials</b>”, not “temporary secrets”."},{"type":"p","html":"<code>StringEquals</code> <b>vs</b> <code>StringLike</code> in the trust policy: <code>StringEquals</code> needs an exact match; <code>StringLike</code> allows"}]},{"title":"OIDC, step by step","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"wildcards such as <code>repo:Owner/Repo:*</code>. Keep the wildcard as narrow as possible."}]},{"title":"Security in pipelines","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Never hard-code keys. Use OIDC for cloud access.","Use a <code>permissions:</code> block to give the workflow only what it needs.","Pin third-party actions to a version tag or a commit SHA, so the code can’t change underneath you.","Scan in the pipeline: <b>Trivy</b> (container images), <b>Checkov</b> (Terraform), <b>Dependabot</b> and <b>CodeQL</b> (dependencies and code).","This is <b>shift left</b>: find problems at the pull request stage, before deployment."]}]},{"title":"Deployment strategies","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Rolling:</b> replace old tasks with new ones a few at a time. The ECS default.","<b>Blue/green:</b> run two full environments, the current (blue) and the new (green). Test green, then switch all traffic to it. Switching back is fast.","<b>Canary:</b> send a small percentage of users to the new version first, then increase it if it’s healthy.","<b>Rollback:</b> go back to the last working version. With SHA tags, that means deploying the previous SHA image."]}]},{"title":"Deployment strategies","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ Blue/green is a deployment strategy, not a scaling method."}]},{"title":"Debugging a failed workflow","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Open the failed run and <b>read the log of the red step</b>.","<b>Re-run</b> it to see whether the failure is a one-off or happens every time.","Check the <b>YAML</b> (indentation, spelling), that every <b>secret and variable exists</b> with the exact name, and the <b>permissions</b>.","For AWS errors, check the OIDC trust policy and the role’s permissions."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The Memos pipeline: three workflows</b>"}]},{"title":"Debugging a failed workflow","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Workflow</b>","<b>Trigger</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["<b>build.yml</b>","push to main touching <code>app/**</code>, <code>Dockerfile</code> or <code>.dockerignore</code>; or manual","checkout → tfsec → OIDC → ECR login → reuse the SHA image if it exists, otherwise build and push"]]}]},{"title":"Debugging a failed workflow","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Workflow</b>","<b>Trigger</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["<b>deploy.yml</b>","<b>completed successfully</b> on main; or manual, with an <code>workflow_run</code>: build.yml <code>image_tag</code> input","checkout → tfsec → OIDC → check the SHA format and that the image exists → <code>fmt -check</code> → <code>init</code> → <code>validate</code> → <code>plan -var=image_url=…</code> <code>-out=tfplan</code> → <code>apply tfplan</code>. Then a separate <b>health-check</b> job: <code>sleep 60</code>, <code>curl https://tm.myegh.com/healthz</code>, fail unless 200"],["<b>destroy.yml</b>","manual only","OIDC → <code>terraform destroy</code>"]]}]},{"title":"Debugging a failed workflow","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Secrets:</b> just one, <code>AWS_ROLE_ARN</code>. No AWS keys anywhere.","<b>Actions used:</b> <code>actions/checkout@v4</code>, <code>aquasecurity/tfsec-action@v1.0.3</code>, <code>aws-actions/configure-aws-</code> <code>credentials@v4</code>, <code>aws-actions/amazon-ecr-login@v2</code>, <code>hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3</code> (Terraform 1.13.5).","<b>Delivery or deployment?</b> Continuous <b>deployment</b>: a passing push to main goes live with no approval (<code>apply</code> of the saved plan, with no prompt).","<b>Rollback:</b> run deploy.yml by hand with an older full SHA."]}]},{"title":"Debugging a failed workflow","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<b>Splitting build and deploy</b> means a deploy can be re-run, or pointed at any earlier image, without rebuilding."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>What I’d add next:</b> Go tests (the app has test files) and a Trivy image scan before pushing; <code>terraform plan</code> posted on pull requests; a <code>production</code> environment with required approval; a narrower OIDC trust and a least-privilege role; third-party actions pinned to commit SHAs; the ECS circuit breaker for automatic rollback; and <code>aws ecs wait services-stable</code> instead of a fixed <code>sleep 60</code>."}]}]},{"id":"devops-13-troubleshooting","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"13","title":"Troubleshooting","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"modules/ecs (log group, 7-day retention)"}],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Layer by layer, one change at a time.</b> Deploy → ECS → TG → ALB/network → logs → dependencies","<b>502/503:</b> TG healthy? → task running? → port 8081 everywhere? → health path answering? → SG 8081 from the ALB SG? → logs <code>/ecs/memos</code>","<b>Task won’t start:</b> stopped reason → logs → image in ECR? → execution role → NAT → CMD","Errors after a release → <b>roll back first</b>, find the root cause after"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["Monitoring = what · observability = why · logs / metrics / traces"]}]}]},{"title":"The method","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Work layer by layer, and change one thing at a time.</b> Changing several things at once means you don’t know which one fixed it.","Order for Memos: <b>deployment → ECS events → target group → ALB and network → application logs →</b> <b>dependencies.</b>","<b>Monitoring</b> tells you <b>what</b> is wrong (a metric is high, an alarm fired). <b>Observability</b> helps you find <b>why</b>.","The three types of data:","<b>Logs:</b> individual events and messages.","<b>Metrics:</b> numbers over time (CPU, request count, latency)."]}]},{"title":"The method","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":7,"items":["<b>Traces:</b> the path of one request through the system."]}]},{"title":"Playbooks","part":"1/7","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>1. The site returns 502 Bad Gateway, or is unreachable</b>","Target group: are any targets <b>healthy</b>?","ECS: is the task <b>running</b>, or does it keep stopping?","Does the port match <b>everywhere</b> (8081 in <code>MEMOS_PORT</code>, the task definition, the target group and both SG rules)?","Does the health check path answer with 200–399? (Mine checks <code>/</code>; <code>/healthz</code> is better.)","Does the ECS security group allow <b>8081 from the ALB security group</b>, and does the ALB SG allow <b>egress</b> to it?"]}]},{"title":"Playbooks","part":"2/7","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":7,"items":["CloudWatch <b>logs</b> (<code>/ecs/memos</code>): is the app crashing?"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["ALB codes: <b>502</b> = the target refused the connection or sent a bad reply · <b>503</b> = no targets registered (e.g. the ECS task keeps crashing, so it never registers) · <b>504</b> = the target didn’t reply in time. If every target is unhealthy, the ALB <b>fails open</b> and still sends traffic to them, so you’ll usually see 502s."]}]},{"title":"Playbooks","part":"3/7","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":2,"items":["<b>2. A timeout (the request hangs)</b> Traffic is being blocked. Check the security groups, the NACLs and the route tables. (Connection refused is different: the machine was reached, but nothing is listening.)","<b>3. The task won’t start</b>","Read the task’s <b>stopped reason</b> in ECS.","Read the <b>CloudWatch logs</b>.","Does the image and <b>tag exist in ECR</b>?","Can the <b>execution role</b> pull from ECR and write logs?"]}]},{"title":"Playbooks","part":"4/7","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":8,"items":["Can the private subnet reach ECR (<b>NAT gateway</b> or endpoints)?","Is the container’s start command (<b>ENTRYPOINT/CMD</b>) correct?","<b>4. The pipeline failed</b>","Read the red step’s log.","If it’s the AWS login step: does the OIDC trust policy’s <b>subject</b> match the repo and branch?","If it’s a later AWS step: does the role have <b>permission</b> for that action?","If it’s the build: dependency, toolchain or Dockerfile problem."]}]},{"title":"Playbooks","part":"5/7","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":15,"items":["<b>5. The app is slow</b> Latency and error metrics → task CPU and memory → ALB and target group metrics → the application → the database or other dependencies → was there a recent deployment? If users are affected, roll back first.","<b>6. Errors started after a release</b> Compare the time the errors started with the deploy time. <b>Roll back to the previous SHA first</b> to stop user impact, then find the root cause.","<b>7. Someone changed AWS in the console</b> That’s drift. Run <code>terraform plan</code> to see it, then fix it through the code."]}]},{"title":"Playbooks","part":"6/7","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":18,"items":["<b>8. Two people ran apply at the same time</b> State conflict or corruption. Prevent it with remote state and <b>locking</b>.","<b>9. AWS access keys were found in GitHub</b>","<b>Deactivate and delete the keys immediately.</b>","Check CloudTrail for misuse.","Remove them from Git history.","Switch to OIDC with a least-privilege role."]}]},{"title":"Playbooks","part":"7/7","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":24,"items":["<b>10. Where should a database password go?</b> <b>Secrets Manager</b> (or SSM Parameter Store for configuration). The task definition references it, and the execution role reads it at start-up. Never put it in code, the image or Git.","<b>11. CPU is at 90%</b> Confirm it’s really the bottleneck. Then scale <b>vertically</b> (more CPU and memory per task) or <b>horizontally</b> (more tasks, with Service Auto Scaling)."]}]},{"title":"If I haven’t used a tool","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"“I haven’t used New Relic hands-on yet, but I understand it as an observability platform for logs, metrics, traces, dashboards and APM. I’ve used CloudWatch, so the troubleshooting principles carry over.”"}]}]},{"id":"devops-14-memos-challenges-as-interview-stories","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"14","title":"Memos challenges as interview stories","key":true,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Quick recall","part":"1/21","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>⚡ Quick recall: the eight stories</b>"}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"2/21","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["OIDC wouldn’t authenticate → static keys only to unblock → rebuilt provider + role + trust policy → back to OIDC","<code>.gitignore</code> pattern <code>memos</code> also ignored <code>cmd/memos/</code> → main.go never committed → added it, pattern should be <code>/memos</code>","Go version: <code>golang:1.23</code> image vs a newer <code>go.mod</code> → <code>GOTOOLCHAIN=auto</code> → now <code>golang:1.25</code> to match","Port drift 5230 ↔︎ 8081 → targets unhealthy → one port everywhere (should be one variable)"]}]}]},{"title":"Quick recall","part":"3/21","blocks":[{"type":"callout","kind":"recall","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["CGO / C libraries → <code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code> static binary + pure-Go SQLite → plain Alpine runtime","Image size → three-stage build → 2.79 GB → 75.8 MB (~97%)","Immutable tags broke re-runs → build checks ECR and reuses the SHA image","Moving state and OIDC into bootstrap → ACM records already existed → <code>import</code> blocks, then removed"]}]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"4/21","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Tell each one as <b>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson.</b> Learn three really well (1, 2 and 6 are the strongest), and know the rest well enough to mention. All of these are in the commit history."},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>1. OIDC wouldn’t authenticate (March 2026)</b>"]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"5/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> the pipeline’s AWS login step kept failing. The role couldn’t be assumed.","<b>What I tried:</b> a series of “Test OIDC” commits; downgrading <code>aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials</code> from v4 to v3 (that didn’t fix it); and <b>temporarily switching to static access keys</b> just to unblock the rest of the pipeline.","<b>Cause:</b> the role’s <b>trust policy</b> didn’t match the token GitHub sends. The <code>sub</code> claim has to match <code>repo:Owner/Repo:…</code> exactly, and mine didn’t (I’d renamed the repo)."]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"6/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Fix:</b> recreated the OIDC provider and a fresh role with a correct trust policy, stored the role ARN as the <code>AWS_ROLE_ARN</code> secret, and switched back to OIDC. The trust policy now checks <code>aud = sts.amazonaws.com</code> (StringEquals) and <code>sub</code> like <code>repo:EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra:*</code> (StringLike). Later it moved into the bootstrap stack as code.","<b>Lesson:</b> OIDC failures are nearly always the trust policy conditions. Use static keys only as a short-term unblock, then delete them. (Make sure the IAM access key really was deactivated and deleted, because an interviewer may ask.)"]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"7/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["<b>Follow-up I’d make:</b> narrow <code>sub</code> to <code>ref:refs/heads/main</code>, and replace <code>AdministratorAccess</code> with least privilege."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":2,"items":["<b>2.</b> <code>.gitignore</code> <b>hid the program’s entry point</b>"]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"8/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> the image build failed because the main package <code>./cmd/memos</code> couldn’t be found, even though it was on my machine.","<b>Cause:</b> <code>app/.gitignore</code> contains the line <code>memos</code>, meant to ignore the <b>compiled binary</b>. A pattern without a slash matches <b>anywhere</b>, so it also ignored the folder <code>cmd/memos/</code>, and <code>main.go</code> was never committed.","<b>Fix:</b> added the folder explicitly (commit “Add memos cmd directory”). The correct pattern is <code>/memos</code>, which matches only at the top level."]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"9/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Lesson:</b> “works on my machine” can mean “not in Git”. Check <code>git status</code>, <code>git ls-files</code> and <code>git check-</code> <code>ignore -v &lt;path&gt;</code>."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":3,"items":["<b>3. Go toolchain mismatch</b>"]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"10/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> the Go build stage failed.","<b>Cause:</b> the Dockerfile used <code>golang:1.23-alpine</code>, but the code’s <code>go.mod</code> needed a newer Go.","<b>Fix:</b> first added <code>ENV GOTOOLCHAIN=auto</code> so Go downloads the required version; the Dockerfile now uses <code>golang:1.25-alpine</code>, matching <code>go 1.25</code> in <code>go.mod</code>, so nothing extra is downloaded.","<b>Lesson:</b> match the build image to the code’s declared version. Don’t rely on downloads during a build."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":4,"items":["<b>4. Port drift: 5230 vs 8081</b>"]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"11/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> the ALB showed targets as unhealthy and the site returned errors.","<b>Cause:</b> the port changed several times (Memos’s default 5230, then 8081 for the project spec), and for a while the Dockerfile, task definition, target group and security groups didn’t agree.","<b>Fix:</b> one port, <b>8081</b>, everywhere: <code>MEMOS_PORT</code>, <code>EXPOSE</code>, <code>container_port</code>, the target group port and health-check port, and both SG rules.","<b>Lesson:</b> a port lives in five places. Make it <b>one variable</b> (still to do: it’s hard-coded today)."]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"12/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["<b>5. CGO and C libraries</b>"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> an earlier build needed C libraries in the runtime image (an early Dockerfile installed <code>libc6-</code> <code>compat</code> to run the binary).","<b>Fix:</b> build with <code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code>, giving a fully static Go binary. That works because Memos’s SQLite driver (<code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>) is pure Go. The runtime image now needs only <code>ca-certificates</code> and <code>tzdata</code>.","<b>Lesson:</b> a static binary makes the runtime image tiny and removes a class of “library not found” errors."]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"13/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":6,"items":["<b>6. Image size: three-stage build</b>"]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"14/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> a single-stage image carried Node, Go, compilers and all the source code.","<b>Fix:</b> three stages: frontend (Node) → backend (Go, with the built frontend embedded) → Alpine runtime with just the binary.","<b>Result (measured with</b> <code>docker images</code><b> , linux/amd64):</b> single-stage <b>2.79 GB</b> disk / 633 MB content → multi-stage <b>75.8 MB</b> / 19.7 MB. <b>About 97% smaller.</b> <code>Dockerfile.single-stage</code> is kept in the repo so anyone can reproduce it.","<b>Lesson:</b> smaller images pull faster, deploy faster, and have far less to attack."]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"15/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":7,"items":["<b>7. Immutable tags broke pipeline re-runs</b>"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> after turning on <b>immutable tags</b> in ECR, re-running a build for the same commit failed: you can’t push the same tag twice.","<b>Fix:</b> the build step now calls <code>aws ecr describe-images</code> first. If the SHA tag exists, it <b>reuses</b> the image; if the error is <code>ImageNotFoundException</code>, it builds and pushes; any other error fails the job.","<b>Lesson:</b> make pipelines <b>idempotent</b>, so re-running the same commit is safe."]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"16/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":8,"items":["<b>8. Restructuring: bootstrap, native locking and imports (October 2026)</b>"]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"17/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Change:</b> moved the state bucket and the OIDC provider and role into a separate <b>bootstrap</b> stack, and switched the backend to <b>S3 native locking</b> (<code>use_lockfile = true</code>, no DynamoDB).","<b>Problem:</b> the ACM DNS validation records already existed in Route 53. Terraform wanted to create them again and would fail.","<b>Fix:</b> added <code>import</code> <b>blocks</b> for <code>module.acm.aws_route53_record.validation[\"myegh.com\"]</code> and <code>[\"tm.myegh.com\"]</code>, ran plan/apply until there were no changes, then deleted <code>imports.tf</code>."]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"18/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Lesson:</b> use import to adopt real resources instead of recreating them, and keep the resources that must outlive a destroy in their own stack."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Bonus: hardening with tfsec</b> After adding tfsec, I fixed what was worth fixing (TLS 1.3 policy, dropping invalid headers, ALB egress only to ECS on 8081, ECS egress only 443, no auto-assigned public IPs) and <b>documented the rest</b> as accepted exceptions with reasons (public ALB, no flow logs, default encryption, no Container Insights)."}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"19/21","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>“What would you improve?”</b> (in this order)"}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"20/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Persistent data:</b> RDS PostgreSQL (or EFS), with secrets in Secrets Manager.","<b>High availability:</b> 2+ tasks across AZs, plus Service Auto Scaling.","<b>Least-privilege OIDC role,</b> and restrict <code>sub</code> to the main branch or an environment.","<b>Health check path →</b> <code>/healthz</code><b> ,</b> plus the ECS deployment circuit breaker with automatic rollback.","<b>Pipeline gates:</b> Go tests, Trivy image scan, <code>plan</code> on PRs, a production approval environment."]}]},{"title":"14. Memos challenges as interview stories","part":"21/21","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":6,"items":["<b>Clean-up:</b> one port variable, delete or use the route53 module, fix the README, use conventional commit messages."]}]}]},{"id":"devops-16-common-mistakes","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"16","title":"Common mistakes","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"16. Common mistakes","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Wrong</b>","<b>Right</b>"],["“OIDC gives temporary secrets”","Temporary <b>credentials</b>"],["“The image is pushed to Fargate”","Pushed to <b>ECR</b>; Fargate <b>pulls</b> it"],["“<code>:latest</code> is the newest image”","It’s only the default tag name"],["“EXPOSE opens the port”","It only records the port"],["“<code>depends_on</code> waits until the database is ready”","It only controls start order"],["“<code>sensitive = true</code> protects the secret”","It hides it on screen; it’s still in state"],["“<code>TF_VAR_</code> beats tfvars”","<b>tfvars beats</b> <code>TF_VAR_</code>"]]}]},{"title":"16. Common mistakes","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Wrong</b>","<b>Right</b>"],["“User data runs on every boot”","First boot only"],["“Attaching an IGW makes a subnet public”","It also needs the <b>route</b> and public IPs"],["“SCPs grant permissions”","They only limit"],["“S3’s eleven nines is uptime”","That’s <b>durability</b>; availability is 99.99%"],["“An NLB can’t terminate TLS”","It can, with a TLS listener; it can’t read HTTP"],["“Only idle Elastic IPs cost money”","Every public IPv4 address is charged"],["“Ctrl+C sends signal 15”","It sends SIGINT, <b>2</b>"]]}]},{"title":"16. Common mistakes","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Wrong</b>","<b>Right</b>"],["“<code>uniq</code> removes all duplicates”","Only adjacent ones, so <code>sort</code> first"],["“<code>git diff</code> compares with the last commit”","It compares with <b>staging</b>; use <code>git diff HEAD</code>"],["“A stopped service is disabled”","Stopped = not running now; disabled = won’t start at boot"],["“<code>/etc/passwd</code> holds the passwords”","The hashes are in <code>/etc/shadow</code>"],["“Blue/green is a way to scale”","It’s a <b>deployment</b> strategy"],["“S3 locking needs DynamoDB”","Since 1.10, <code>use_lockfile = true</code>"]]}]}]},{"id":"devops-17-numbers-to-know","guide":"devops","guideTitle":"DevOps Revision Guide","part":"Core guide","num":"17","title":"Numbers to know","key":true,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"17. Numbers to know","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Topic</b>","<b>Numbers</b>"],["Ports","22 SSH · 53 DNS · 80 HTTP · 443 HTTPS · 3306 MySQL · 5432 Postgres · 3389 RDP · <b>8081 my</b> <b>Memos</b> (Memos default 5230)"],["CIDR","/32 = 1 · /28 = 16 (smallest AWS subnet) · /24 = 256 · /16 = 65,536 (largest VPC) · AWS reserves 5 per subnet"],["Availability","99.9% ≈ 8.8 h a year · 99.99% ≈ 53 min a year"],["ALB deregistration delay","300 s by default"],["Lambda","15 min max · 128 MB to 10 GB memory"],["Spot","2-minute warning · up to ~90% off"]]}]},{"title":"17. Numbers to know","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Topic</b>","<b>Numbers</b>"],["ECR login","valid 12 hours"],["Linux signals","2 SIGINT (Ctrl+C) · 9 SIGKILL · 15 SIGTERM · 20 SIGTSTP (Ctrl+Z)"],["Permissions","r = 4 · w = 2 · x = 1 · 755 · 644 · 600/400"],["Exit codes","0 success · 1 error · 2 wrong usage · 127 not found · 130 Ctrl+C"],["Memos","eu-west-1 · 2 AZs · 2 NAT · 7 modules + bootstrap · port 8081 · 0.25 vCPU / 512 MB · 1 task · 2.79 GB → 75.8 MB · TG check <code>/</code> · pipeline check <code>/healthz</code>"]]}]}]},{"id":"aws-tl-dr-the-whole-guide-in-three-points","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"TL;DR: the whole guide in three points","num":"","title":"TL;DR: the whole guide in three points","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"TL;DR: the whole guide in three points","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>AWS rents out computing resources over the internet.</b> Instead of buying your own servers, you rent them from AWS by the second or by the hour, and you stop paying when you delete them. AWS has data centres in many parts of the world, grouped into <b>Regions</b>. Each Region contains several separate data centre sites called <b>Availability Zones</b>, so an application can keep running if one site fails.","<b>Everything in AWS is locked down unless you open it.</b>","<b>IAM</b> decides which people and programs can do which actions."]}]},{"title":"TL;DR: the whole guide in three points","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>VPCs, subnets, route tables and security groups</b> decide which network traffic can reach which resource.","The rule is <b>least privilege</b>: give the smallest amount of access that still lets the job be done.","<b>Real applications combine many AWS services.</b> In Memos:"]}]},{"title":"TL;DR: the whole guide in three points","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Route 53</b> turns the name <code>tm.myegh.com</code> into an address.","A <b>load balancer</b> receives the visitor’s request and handles encryption using a certificate from <b>ACM</b>.","It passes the request to a <b>container</b> running on <b>ECS Fargate</b> inside a <b>private subnet</b>. The container image is stored in <b>ECR</b>.","Logs go to <b>CloudWatch</b>.","All of this is created by <b>Terraform</b> code and deployed by <b>GitHub Actions</b>, which logs in to AWS using <b>OIDC</b> instead of stored passwords."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-1-computers-servers-and-the-internet","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"The basics you need first","num":"1","title":"Computers, servers and the internet","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Chapter 1: Computers, servers and the internet","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"AWS is a way of renting computers and networks, so you first need to know what those are."}]},{"title":"Computers and servers","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>computer</b> has four main parts:","a <b>CPU</b> (the chip that runs instructions),","<b>memory</b>, also called <b>RAM</b> (fast, temporary storage that is cleared when the computer turns off),","<b>storage</b> (a disk that keeps data when the power is off),","a <b>network card</b> (the part that sends and receives data over a network).","An <b>operating system</b> (OS) is the main program that controls the computer and runs other programs. Examples: Linux, Windows, macOS."]}]},{"title":"Computers and servers","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":7,"items":["A <b>server</b> is a computer whose job is to answer requests from other computers. When you open a website, a server sends the page to your browser.","A <b>client</b> is the computer or program that sends the request. Your browser is a client."]}]},{"title":"How computers find each other: IP addresses","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Every device on a network has an <b>IP address</b>: a number that identifies it, such as <code>10.0.3.25</code>.","<b>IPv4</b> addresses are written as four numbers from 0 to 255, separated by dots. There are about 4.3 billion possible IPv4 addresses, which is not enough for every device in the world.","<b>IPv6</b> addresses are much longer, such as <code>2001:db8::1</code>. There are enough for every device.","<b>Public IP addresses</b> can be reached from anywhere on the internet."]}]},{"title":"How computers find each other: IP addresses","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<b>Private IP addresses</b> can only be reached inside one private network. Three ranges are reserved for private use:","<code>10.0.0.0</code> to <code>10.255.255.255</code>","<code>172.16.0.0</code> to <code>172.31.255.255</code>","<code>192.168.0.0</code> to <code>192.168.255.255</code>"]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 My Memos network uses the range starting <code>10.0.</code>."}]},{"title":"Ports","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["One computer can run many network programs at once. A <b>port</b> is a number from 0 to 65535 that says which program on the computer should receive the data.","Common ports:"]}]},{"title":"Ports","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Port</b>","<b>Used by</b>"],["22","SSH (logging in to a Linux server)"],["53","DNS (looking up names)"],["80","HTTP (web pages, not encrypted)"],["443","HTTPS (web pages, encrypted)"],["3306","MySQL database"],["5432","PostgreSQL database"],["3389","RDP (Windows remote desktop)"]]}]},{"title":"Ports","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Ports 0 to 1023 are <b>privileged</b>: on Linux, only the administrator account (called <b>root</b>) can start a program that listens on them. 🔧 That is why my Memos container, which does not run as root, listens on port <b>8081</b>."]}]},{"title":"Protocols: TCP, UDP, HTTP and HTTPS","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>protocol</b> is a set of rules for how two computers exchange data.","<b>TCP</b> makes sure every piece of data arrives, in the right order. Before sending data, the two computers do a <b>three-way handshake</b>: the client sends SYN, the server replies SYN-ACK, the client replies ACK. Websites, SSH and databases use TCP.","<b>UDP</b> sends data without checking that it arrived. It is faster but less reliable. DNS lookups and video calls use UDP.","<b>HTTP</b> is the protocol for web pages. The client sends a <b>request</b> with a <b>method</b>:"]}]},{"title":"Protocols: TCP, UDP, HTTP and HTTPS","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<code>GET</code> asks for something,","<code>POST</code> sends new data,","<code>PUT</code> replaces something,","<code>DELETE</code> removes something.","The server replies with a <b>status code</b>:","<b>2xx</b> means success (200 = OK),","<b>3xx</b> means “go somewhere else” (301 = moved permanently),","<b>4xx</b> means the client made a mistake (403 = forbidden, 404 = not found),"]}]},{"title":"Protocols: TCP, UDP, HTTP and HTTPS","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":13,"items":["<b>5xx</b> means the server failed (500 = internal error, 502 = bad gateway, 503 = service unavailable, 504 = gateway timeout).","<b>HTTPS</b> is HTTP with <b>encryption</b>. Encryption scrambles data so that only the intended receiver can read it. The encryption system is called <b>TLS</b>. The padlock icon in a browser means HTTPS is being used."]}]},{"title":"Names instead of numbers: DNS","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["People remember names such as <code>tm.myegh.com</code> better than numbers. <b>DNS</b> (Domain Name System) is the system that turns a name into an IP address.","When you type a website name, your computer asks a DNS server for the matching IP address, then connects to that address. Chapter 15 explains every step."]}]},{"title":"The client-server request, from start to finish","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["You type <code>https://tm.myegh.com</code> into the browser.","The browser asks DNS for the IP address of <code>tm.myegh.com</code>.","The browser opens a TCP connection to that IP address on port 443.","The browser and server set up TLS encryption.","The browser sends an HTTP <code>GET</code> request.","The server sends back a response with status 200 and the web page.","The browser shows the page."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-2-what-cloud-computing-is","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"The basics you need first","num":"2","title":"What cloud computing is","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Cloud computing</b> means using computers, storage, databases and networks that belong to another company, over the internet. You pay only for what you use. <b>AWS</b> (Amazon Web Services) is the largest company that provides cloud computing. Others include Microsoft <b>Azure</b> and <b>Google Cloud</b>."}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Before cloud computing, a company that wanted to run a website had to:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["buy servers, often weeks or months in advance,","put them in a building with power, cooling and network connections,","guess how much capacity it would need. If it guessed too low, the website became slow or crashed when busy. If it guessed too high, it paid for machines that sat unused.","replace broken hardware itself."]}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"With cloud computing, you can create a server in about a minute, delete it when you no longer need it, and stop paying immediately."}]},{"title":"The main benefits, in exact terms","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>No upfront cost:</b> you do not buy hardware. This changes <b>capital expenditure</b> (CapEx, money spent upfront on things you own) into <b>operational expenditure</b> (OpEx, money spent as you go).","<b>Pay as you go:</b> you are billed per second, per hour, per gigabyte stored or per request, depending on the service.","<b>Elasticity:</b> you can add resources when demand rises and remove them when it falls, automatically if you set it up.","<b>Speed:</b> new resources are ready in minutes."]}]},{"title":"The main benefits, in exact terms","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<b>Global reach:</b> you can run your application in many countries without building anything there.","<b>Economies of scale:</b> AWS buys hardware in enormous amounts, so its costs per server are lower than yours would be."]}]},{"title":"Service models: how much AWS manages for you","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Model</b>","<b>You manage</b>","<b>AWS manages</b>","<b>AWS example</b>"],["<b>IaaS</b> (Infrastructure as a Service)","The operating system, the software, your app and your data","The physical hardware, network and virtualisation","EC2 (virtual servers)"],["<b>PaaS</b> (Platform as a Service)","Your app and your data","Everything below the app, including the operating system","Elastic Beanstalk, Fargate"],["<b>SaaS</b> (Software as a Service)","Only how you use it","Everything","Gmail, Microsoft 365"]]}]},{"title":"Service models: how much AWS manages for you","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Serverless</b> means you do not manage any servers at all. The servers still exist, but AWS runs and patches them. You pay only when your code runs or your data is used. Examples: Lambda, S3, DynamoDB."}]},{"title":"Deployment models","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Public cloud:</b> resources run in the cloud provider’s data centres (AWS).","<b>Private cloud:</b> a company runs cloud-style infrastructure in its own data centre.","<b>Hybrid cloud:</b> a mix of both, connected together. Example: an office network connected to AWS by a VPN."]}]},{"title":"Virtualisation: how AWS can rent you “part” of a computer","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>physical server</b> in an AWS data centre is very powerful.","A program called a <b>hypervisor</b> splits one physical server into several <b>virtual machines</b> (VMs). Each VM gets its own share of CPU, memory and storage, and runs its own operating system. Each VM cannot see the others.","AWS’s own hypervisor system is called <b>Nitro</b>. When you rent a server in AWS (an “EC2 instance”), you are almost always renting a virtual machine."]}]},{"title":"Virtualisation: how AWS can rent you “part” of a computer","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Containers</b> (Chapter 20) are lighter than VMs: they share the host operating system’s core part, called the <b>kernel</b>, instead of each running a full operating system."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-3-aws-global-infrastructure","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"The basics you need first","num":"3","title":"AWS global infrastructure","key":true,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Regions","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>Region</b> is a separate geographic area where AWS has data centres. Examples:"]},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Region code</b>","<b>Location</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>eu-west-1</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Ireland"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>eu-west-2</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["London"],["North Virginia, USA"]]}]},{"title":"Regions","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>us-east-1</code>","AWS has more than 35 Regions around the world.","Regions are <b>independent</b>. Most resources you create exist in one Region only, and data does not leave a Region unless you copy it out.","Some services are <b>global</b>, meaning they are not tied to one Region: IAM, Route 53, CloudFront, and the AWS Organizations account structure."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>How to choose a Region</b> (in this order):"}]},{"title":"Regions","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Compliance:</b> laws or company rules about where data may be stored. Example: UK customer data might be required to stay in the UK, so you would choose <code>eu-west-2</code>.","<b>Latency: latency</b> is the time data takes to travel. A Region closer to your users gives faster responses.","<b>Service availability:</b> not every service or feature is in every Region. New features often arrive in <code>us-east-1</code> first.","<b>Price:</b> prices differ slightly between Regions."]}]},{"title":"Availability Zones (AZs)","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Each Region contains <b>at least three Availability Zones</b>. An AZ is one or more data centres with their own power supply, cooling and network connections.","AZs in a Region are physically separated by a meaningful distance (many kilometres), so one fire, flood or power cut does not affect all of them. They are still close enough to be connected by fast, private fibre-optic links with very low latency.","AZ names add a letter to the Region name: <code>eu-west-1a</code>, <code>eu-west-1b</code>, <code>eu-west-1c</code>."]}]},{"title":"Availability Zones (AZs)","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"recall","html":"⚡ <b>To make an application keep working when one data centre fails, run it in at least two AZs.</b> This is called <b>high availability</b>."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> everything runs in <code>eu-west-1</code> across two AZs, <code>eu-west-1a</code> and <code>eu-west-1b</code>. The first version, built by hand in the AWS website, used <code>eu-west-2</code> (London)."}]},{"title":"Edge locations","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Edge locations</b> (also called <b>points of presence</b>) are small AWS sites in hundreds of cities. They are much more numerous than Regions.","They are used by <b>CloudFront</b> (to store copies of website content close to users) and <b>Route 53</b> (to answer DNS questions quickly)."]}]},{"title":"Other locations (good to recognise)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Local Zones:</b> small extensions of a Region placed inside a large city, for very low latency.","<b>Wavelength Zones:</b> AWS resources inside mobile phone networks, for 5G applications.","<b>Outposts:</b> AWS hardware installed in your own building."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-4-the-shared-responsibility-model","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"The basics you need first","num":"4","title":"The shared responsibility model","key":true,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Security in AWS is split between AWS and you. AWS is responsible for <b>security of the cloud</b>. You are responsible for <b>security in the cloud</b>."}]},{"title":"What it is","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>AWS is responsible for</b>","<b>You are responsible for</b>"],["The physical buildings, guards and hardware","Who can log in to your account (IAM)"],["The physical network and the hypervisor","Your data, and whether it is encrypted"],["The software that runs AWS’s own services","Updating the operating system on your EC2 instances"],["","Your firewall rules (security groups, network ACLs)"],["","Your application’s code"]]}]},{"title":"How the split changes","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"The more AWS manages, the less you manage:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["On <b>EC2</b>, you must update the operating system yourself.","On <b>Fargate</b> or <b>Lambda</b>, AWS updates the operating system.","On <b>S3</b>, AWS manages everything except your data and who can access it."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <b>A common mistake:</b> thinking AWS will stop you exposing data. If you make an S3 bucket public by mistake, that is your responsibility, not AWS’s."}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-5-how-you-use-aws","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"The basics you need first","num":"5","title":"How you use AWS","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Chapter 5: How you use AWS","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"There are four ways to give AWS instructions. All of them send requests to the same place: the <b>AWS API</b>. An <b>API</b> (Application Programming Interface) is a set of commands that one program offers to other programs."}]},{"title":"Chapter 5: How you use AWS","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>The AWS Management Console:</b> the AWS website, where you click buttons. Good for learning and for looking at things. Changes made by clicking are hard to repeat exactly, and there is no record of why they were made.","<b>The AWS CLI</b> (Command Line Interface): a program you run in a terminal. Example: <code>aws s3 ls</code> lists your S3 buckets. Commands can be saved in scripts and repeated.","<b>SDKs</b> (Software Development Kits): libraries for programming languages such as Python (<code>boto3</code>), JavaScript and Go, so your own programs can call AWS."]}]},{"title":"Chapter 5: How you use AWS","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":4,"items":["<b>Infrastructure as Code (IaC):</b> files that describe the resources you want, which a tool then creates. Examples: <b>Terraform</b> (used in Memos) and <b>AWS CloudFormation</b>. This is the professional way to build infrastructure, because the files can be reviewed, stored in Git and run again to get the same result. Chapter 26 covers it."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> I first built everything by clicking in the Console, to learn how the parts fit. Then I rebuilt it all with Terraform, and finally automated it with GitHub Actions."}]},{"title":"Setting up the AWS CLI","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Install it from the AWS documentation (on macOS, <code>brew install awscli</code>).","Check it works: <code>aws --version</code>.","Log in. The recommended way is through <b>IAM Identity Center</b> (Chapter 7) with <code>aws configure sso</code>. The older way is <code>aws configure</code> with an access key, which stores a long-term password on your computer and is less safe."]}]},{"title":"Setting up the AWS CLI","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":4,"items":["Check who you are logged in as: <code>aws sts get-caller-identity</code>. It prints your account number and your user or role. Run this whenever a command says “access denied”: often you are logged in as the wrong identity.","Set your Region: either in the configuration, or per command with <code>--region eu-west-1</code>. ⚠️ A very common reason for “I can’t see my resources” is looking in the wrong Region."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-6-paying-for-aws-and-staying-safe-from-surprise-bill","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"The basics you need first","num":"6","title":"Paying for AWS and staying safe from surprise bills","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"How pricing works","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Every service has its own pricing. The most common ways you are charged:"},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>You pay for</b>","<b>Example</b>"],["Time a resource exists or runs","EC2 per second; a NAT gateway per hour"],["Amount of data stored","S3 per GB per month"],["Number of requests","Lambda per million requests"],["Data sent out of AWS to the internet","per GB. Data coming <b>in</b> is usually free"]]}]},{"title":"How pricing works","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"There are also some charges people often miss:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Every public IPv4 address costs money</b>, about $0.005 per hour, even when it is attached to a running resource.","<b>NAT gateways</b> are charged per hour and per GB of data, even when idle.","<b>EKS</b> clusters cost $0.10 per hour for the control plane alone, before any servers.","Data sent <b>between AZs</b> is charged."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>My Memos costs per month (approximate):</b>"}]},{"title":"How pricing works","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Item</b>","<b>Cost</b>"],["2 NAT gateways","about £50 (the largest cost)"],["Load balancer","about £5"],["One small Fargate task","about £8"],["Route 53 hosted zone","£0.40"],["ACM certificate","free"]]},{"type":"p","html":"This is why I have a workflow that deletes everything when I am not using it."}]},{"title":"The AWS Free Tier (current rules)","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"From <b>15 July 2025</b>, new AWS accounts choose a plan when they sign up:"}]},{"title":"The AWS Free Tier (current rules)","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Free plan:</b>","<b>$100 in credits</b> at sign-up, plus <b>up to $100 more</b> for completing activities that use services such as EC2. That is up to <b>$200</b> in total.","The plan lasts <b>6 months, or until the credits are used up</b>, whichever comes first.","You can then upgrade to the paid plan.","Some services are restricted on the free plan.","<b>Paid plan:</b> all services are available. The credits still apply."]}]},{"title":"The AWS Free Tier (current rules)","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":7,"items":["<b>Always-free offers</b> continue for more than 30 services, with monthly limits (for example, a number of Lambda requests each month).","Accounts created <b>before</b> 15 July 2025 keep the older rules: 12 months of free use of certain services within monthly limits."]}]},{"title":"Tools for watching costs","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>AWS Budgets:</b> set a monthly amount and get an email when you are close to it or go over it. ⚡ <b>Create a</b> <b>budget alert on the first day you use AWS.</b>","<b>Cost Explorer:</b> charts of what you have spent, by service, by day and by tag.","<b>Tags:</b> labels you attach to resources, such as <code>Project = Memos</code>. They let you see costs per project. 🔧 My Terraform adds <code>Project = Memos</code> and <code>ManagedBy = Terraform</code> to every resource automatically.","<b>AWS Pricing Calculator:</b> estimate costs before you build."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-7-setting-up-an-aws-account-safely-step-by-step","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"The basics you need first","num":"7","title":"Setting up an AWS account safely (step by step)","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Chapter 7: Setting up an AWS account safely (step by step)","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Do these steps in order on day one."}]},{"title":"Chapter 7: Setting up an AWS account safely (step by step)","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Create the account</b> at aws.amazon.com. The email address you use becomes the <b>root user</b>. The root user has complete control of the account, including closing it and changing payment details. It cannot be restricted.","<b>Turn on MFA for the root user.</b> MFA (multi-factor authentication) means that, as well as the password, you must enter a code from an app on your phone or use a hardware security key. This stops someone who steals your password from logging in.","<b>Create a budget alert</b> (Chapter 6)."]}]},{"title":"Chapter 7: Setting up an AWS account safely (step by step)","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":4,"items":["<b>Stop using the root user.</b> Create a separate identity for daily work:"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Recommended:</b> turn on <b>IAM Identity Center</b>, create a user for yourself, and give it the <code>AdministratorAccess</code> permission set. You then log in through a special sign-in page and receive temporary credentials.","<b>Simpler but older:</b> create an <b>IAM user</b> with administrator permissions and MFA."]}]},{"title":"Chapter 7: Setting up an AWS account safely (step by step)","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["<b>Never create access keys for the root user.</b> If any exist, delete them.","<b>Turn on CloudTrail</b> (Chapter 24) so there is a record of every action. A basic 90-day history is on by default; a “trail” keeps it longer in S3.","<b>Choose your main Region</b> and use it consistently."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <b>What goes wrong:</b>"}]},{"title":"Chapter 7: Setting up an AWS account safely (step by step)","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Using the root user every day.","No MFA.","No budget alert, so a forgotten resource runs up a bill for months.","Access keys pasted into code and pushed to GitHub. Automated programs scan GitHub for leaked AWS keys within minutes."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-8-your-first-look-around-the-console","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"The basics you need first","num":"8","title":"Your first look around the Console","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Chapter 8: Your first look around the Console","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"When you log in to the Console:"}]},{"title":"Chapter 8: Your first look around the Console","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Top right:</b> the Region selector. Most resources are shown only for the selected Region.","<b>Top left:</b> the search bar. Type a service name, such as “EC2”, to open it.","<b>The account menu:</b> shows your account ID (a 12-digit number) and the identity you are using.","<b>CloudShell:</b> the terminal icon at the top opens a terminal in your browser with the AWS CLI already installed and logged in as you. Useful when you cannot install the CLI."]}]},{"title":"Chapter 8: Your first look around the Console","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"link","html":"🔗 Next: IAM (Chapter 9), the first service you should understand properly."}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-9-iam-identity-and-access-management","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Identity and access","num":"9","title":"IAM (Identity and Access Management)","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/iam/main.tf","label":"modules/iam"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/oidc.tf","label":"bootstrap/oidc.tf"}],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>IAM</b> is the AWS service that controls two things:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Authentication:</b> proving <b>who</b> you are (logging in).","<b>Authorisation:</b> deciding <b>what</b> you are allowed to do once you are logged in."]},{"type":"p","html":"IAM is <b>global</b> (not tied to one Region) and <b>free</b>."}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"An AWS account can contain thousands of resources, and many people and programs need to use them. Without IAM, everyone would have full control. One mistake or one stolen password could delete everything. IAM lets you give each person and program exactly the access it needs, and nothing more."}]},{"title":"The building blocks","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Thing</b>","<b>What it is</b>","<b>Has long-term</b> <b>credentials?</b>"],["<b>Root</b> <b>user</b>","The identity created with the account. Can do everything; cannot be restricted by IAM policies","Yes (email + password)"],["<b>IAM</b> <b>user</b>","An identity for one person or one program, with its own permanent credentials","Yes (password and/or access keys)"],["<b>IAM</b> <b>group</b>","A collection of users. Permissions attached to the group apply to every user in it","No: it is not an identity you log in as"]]}]},{"title":"The building blocks","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Thing</b>","<b>What it is</b>","<b>Has long-term</b> <b>credentials?</b>"],["<b>IAM role</b>","An identity with permissions that a trusted person, program or AWS service can <b>assume</b> (temporarily use). Gives <b>temporary credentials</b> that expire","<b>No</b>"],["<b>Policy</b>","A document, written in JSON, that lists what is allowed or denied","n/a"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Credentials</b> are the secrets used to prove identity:"}]},{"title":"The building blocks","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>password</b> is for logging in to the Console.","An <b>access key</b> is a pair of strings (an access key ID and a secret access key) used by the CLI or programs. Access keys stay valid until someone deletes them, which is why leaked keys are dangerous.","<b>Temporary credentials</b> come from assuming a role. They include an expiry time, usually between 15 minutes and 12 hours."]}]},{"title":"Users vs roles: when to use which","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Use <b>IAM Identity Center users</b> (or IAM users, if you must) for <b>people</b>.","Use <b>roles</b> for <b>everything else</b>:","<b>AWS services</b> acting on your behalf (an EC2 instance, a Lambda function, an ECS task),","<b>programs outside AWS</b> (a GitHub Actions pipeline using OIDC, Chapter 27),","<b>people from another AWS account</b>, <b>people who need extra permissions for a short time.</b>"]}]},{"title":"Users vs roles: when to use which","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"recall","html":"⚡ <b>Prefer roles over access keys wherever possible.</b> There is no long-lived secret to leak."}]},{"title":"How a role works","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A role has two separate policies:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>The trust policy</b> says <b>who is allowed to assume</b> the role.","<b>The permissions policy</b> says <b>what the role can do</b> once assumed."]},{"type":"p","html":"When someone assumes a role, <b>AWS STS</b> (Security Token Service) checks the trust policy and, if allowed, returns temporary credentials."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>A real trust policy from Memos</b> (in Terraform). It lets ECS tasks assume the role:"}]},{"title":"How a role works","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"data \"aws_iam_policy_document\" \"ecs_assume_role_policy\" {\n  statement {\n    actions = [\"sts:AssumeRole\"]                     # the action of taking on the role\n    principals {\n      type        = \"Service\"                        # the trusted thing is an AWS service...\n      identifiers = [\"ecs-tasks.amazonaws.com\"]      # ...specifically, ECS tasks\n    }\n  }\n}"}]},{"title":"Policies, line by line","part":"1/6","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A policy is a JSON document containing one or more <b>statements</b>. Here is a policy that lets someone read files from one S3 bucket:"}]},{"title":"Policies, line by line","part":"2/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Sid\": \"ReadMemosBackups\",\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\"s3:GetObject\", \"s3:ListBucket\"],\n      \"Resource\": [\n        \"arn:aws:s3:::memos-backups\",\n        \"arn:aws:s3:::memos-backups/*\"\n      ],\n      \"Condition\": {\n        \"Bool\": { \"aws:SecureTransport\": \"true\" }\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}"}]},{"title":"Policies, line by line","part":"3/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Field</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Version</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["The policy language version. Always <code>2012-10-17</code> (that date is the version name, not today’s date)"],["A list of rules"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Statement</code>","<code>Sid</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["An optional name for the rule"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Effect</code>"]}]},{"title":"Policies, line by line","part":"4/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<code>Allow</code> or <code>Deny</code>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Action</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Which API actions, written as <code>service:Action</code>. <code>s3:GetObject</code> = download a file. <code>*</code> = every action (avoid this)"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Resource</code>"]}]},{"title":"Policies, line by line","part":"5/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","Which resources, written as <b>ARNs</b>. Here: the bucket itself (needed for <code>ListBucket</code>) and every object inside it (<code>/*</code>, needed for <code>GetObject</code>)"],["<b>Field</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Condition</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Optional extra rules. Here: only allow the request if it uses HTTPS"]]},{"type":"p","html":"An <b>ARN</b> (Amazon Resource Name) is the full unique name of a resource. Its format is:"}]},{"title":"Policies, line by line","part":"6/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"arn:aws:<service>:<region>:<account-id>:<resource>"},{"type":"p","html":"Example: <code>arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/github-actions-role</code>. IAM is global, so the region part is empty, which is why there are two colons in a row."}]},{"title":"Types of policy","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Type</b>","<b>What it is</b>"],["<b>AWS managed policy</b>","Written and updated by AWS, e.g. <code>AmazonECSTaskExecutionRolePolicy</code>, <code>ReadOnlyAccess</code>,"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>AdministratorAccess</code>"]}]},{"title":"Types of policy","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<b>Customer managed</b> <b>policy</b>","Written by you, reusable across many users and roles"],["<b>Inline policy</b>","Written inside one user or role, and deleted when that user or role is deleted"],["<b>Resource-based</b> <b>policy</b>","Attached to a <b>resource</b> instead of an identity, e.g. an S3 <b>bucket policy</b>. Says who can use that resource, and can allow other AWS accounts"],["<b>Permissions</b> <b>boundary</b>","Sets the <b>maximum</b> permissions a user or role can ever have, even if other policies allow more"]]}]},{"title":"Types of policy","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<b>Service control</b> <b>policy (SCP)</b>","Set at the AWS Organizations level (Chapter 10). Limits what whole accounts can do. <b>Never gives</b> <b>permissions, only limits them</b>"]]}]},{"title":"How AWS decides “allow” or “deny”","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Every request goes through these steps:"}]},{"title":"How AWS decides “allow” or “deny”","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>By default, everything is denied.</b> This is called <b>implicit deny</b>.","AWS looks at every policy that applies.","If <b>any</b> policy has a matching <code>Deny</code>, the request is <b>denied</b>. This is called <b>explicit deny</b>, and <b>it always wins</b>.","Otherwise, if <b>any</b> policy has a matching <code>Allow</code>, the request is <b>allowed</b>, as long as SCPs and permissions boundaries also allow it.","Otherwise, the request is <b>denied</b> (the default from step 1)."]}]},{"title":"How AWS decides “allow” or “deny”","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"recall","html":"⚡ <b>Default deny → an explicit Deny always wins → otherwise an Allow is needed.</b>"}]},{"title":"Least privilege, in practice","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Least privilege</b> means giving only the permissions that are actually needed. How to do it:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Start from nothing and add permissions as needed, rather than starting with administrator access and removing some.","Limit <code>Resource</code> to specific ARNs instead of <code>*</code>.","Use <b>IAM Access Analyzer</b>, which can show which permissions were actually used, and can generate a policy from what a role really did.","Review permissions regularly and remove what is unused."]}]},{"title":"Least privilege, in practice","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos, there are three roles:</b>"},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Role</b>","<b>Trusted by</b>","<b>Permissions</b>","<b>Comment</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>github-</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["GitHub’s OIDC"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>AdministratorAccess</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Too broad. A known"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>actions-role</code>"}]},{"title":"Least privilege, in practice","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["provider, only for my repository","improvement is to limit it to what Terraform manages"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>memos-ecs-</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["ECS tasks","<code>AmazonECSTaskExecutionRolePolicy</code> (pull","Exactly what ECS needs to start"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>execution-</code>"},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["images from ECR, write logs)","the container"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>role</code>"}]},{"title":"Least privilege, in practice","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>memos-ecs-</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["ECS tasks","<b>None</b>","Memos never calls AWS, so its code needs no permissions."]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>task-role</code>"},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["This is least privilege done correctly"]]}]},{"title":"MFA and password policies","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Turn on MFA for the root user and for every person.","MFA options:","<b>virtual authenticator apps</b> (such as Google Authenticator),","<b>hardware security keys</b> using FIDO2/passkeys (the most resistant to fake login pages, called <b>phishing</b>),","<b>hardware TOTP tokens</b>.","You can set an account <b>password policy</b>: minimum length, required character types, expiry."]}]},{"title":"IAM Identity Center","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>IAM Identity Center</b> (previously called AWS SSO) is the recommended way for <b>people</b> to log in to one or many AWS accounts.","You create users, or connect your company’s existing directory (Microsoft Entra ID, Okta, Google Workspace), and assign <b>permission sets</b> to each account.","People sign in once and receive <b>temporary credentials</b>. No permanent IAM users or access keys are needed."]}]},{"title":"Checking and troubleshooting access","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>aws sts get-caller-identity</code> shows which identity you are using.","An <code>AccessDenied</code> error message usually names the <b>action</b> and the <b>resource</b>. Look for an explicit Deny, a missing Allow, or a resource policy that does not allow you.","The <b>IAM policy simulator</b> tests whether a policy allows an action.","<b>CloudTrail</b> (Chapter 24) records every API call, including denied ones."]}]},{"title":"What goes wrong","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Using <code>\"Action\": \"*\"</code> and <code>\"Resource\": \"*\"</code> everywhere.","Long-lived access keys in code, in <code>.env</code> files committed to Git, or on laptops.","Thinking an SCP gives permissions. It only limits them.","Forgetting that <code>s3:ListBucket</code> applies to the bucket ARN, while <code>s3:GetObject</code> applies to the objects (<code>bucket/*</code>). Many “access denied” errors come from this.","Confusing the trust policy (who can assume) with the permissions policy (what it can do)."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-10-aws-organizations-and-multiple-accounts","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Identity and access","num":"10","title":"AWS Organizations and multiple accounts","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>AWS Organizations</b> lets one company manage many AWS accounts together:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["one <b>management account</b> at the top,","<b>member accounts</b> grouped into <b>organisational units</b> (OUs), such as “Production”, “Development” and “Security”,","<b>consolidated billing</b>: one bill for all accounts, with combined volume discounts."]}]},{"title":"Why companies use many accounts","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"An account is the strongest boundary in AWS. Resources and permissions in one account cannot affect another unless you deliberately allow it. Separate accounts for production, development and testing mean:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["a mistake in development cannot delete production;","costs are separated automatically;","each account can have different rules."]}]},{"title":"Service control policies (SCPs)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["SCPs are attached to OUs or accounts. They set the <b>maximum</b> actions allowed in those accounts. Examples:","“No account in the Development OU may use Regions outside Europe.”","“Nobody may turn off CloudTrail.”"]},{"type":"note","kind":"recall","html":"⚡ SCPs <b>never grant</b> permissions. A user still needs an IAM policy that allows the action."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["SCPs do not affect the management account."]}]},{"title":"AWS Control Tower","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Control Tower</b> sets up a recommended multi-account structure automatically:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["a <b>landing zone</b> (the starting set of accounts, OUs and settings),","logging and security accounts,","preventive and detective <b>guardrails</b> (rules that stop or report bad settings),","an <b>Account Factory</b> to create new accounts with the same baseline settings."]}]},{"title":"Scenario: “The developers want a new AWS account” (a real interview question)","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Ask what it is for: which team, production or experiments, what budget.","Create it under <b>AWS Organizations</b>, using Control Tower’s Account Factory if available.","Place it in the correct <b>OU</b>, so the right <b>SCPs</b> apply (allowed Regions, protected logging).","Set a <b>budget</b> with alerts.","Give access through <b>IAM Identity Center</b> with least-privilege permission sets. Do not create IAM users."]}]},{"title":"Scenario: “The developers want a new AWS account” (a real interview question)","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":6,"items":["Create baseline resources (networking, CloudTrail, logging) using <b>Terraform</b> from a shared repository.","Give the team documentation on how to use it."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-11-ec2-elastic-compute-cloud","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Compute (running programs)","num":"11","title":"EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud)","key":true,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>EC2</b> lets you rent <b>virtual servers</b>, called <b>instances</b>. You choose how powerful the instance is, which operating system it runs and which network it is in. You pay per second while it runs."}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Most software needs a computer to run on. EC2 gives you one in about a minute, in any Region, without buying hardware. When you no longer need it, you <b>terminate</b> (delete) it and the charges stop."}]},{"title":"What you choose when you launch an instance","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>AMI (Amazon Machine Image):</b> the template the instance starts from. It contains the operating system and any pre-installed software. Examples: Amazon Linux 2023, Ubuntu, Windows Server. You can create your own AMI from an instance you have set up. AMIs belong to one Region but can be copied to others.","<b>Instance type:</b> how much CPU, memory and network capacity the instance has. See below."]}]},{"title":"What you choose when you launch an instance","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":3,"items":["<b>Key pair:</b> for logging in with SSH. AWS keeps the <b>public key</b> and puts it on the instance; you download the <b>private key</b> once and keep it secret. Anyone with the private key can log in.","<b>Network settings:</b> which <b>VPC</b> and <b>subnet</b> (Chapter 14), whether it gets a <b>public IP address</b>, and which <b>security groups</b> (firewall rules) apply.","<b>Storage:</b> the size and type of the <b>EBS volume</b> (virtual disk, Chapter 19) that holds the operating system."]}]},{"title":"What you choose when you launch an instance","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":6,"items":["<b>IAM instance profile:</b> an IAM role attached to the instance, so programs on it can call AWS with temporary credentials instead of access keys.","<b>User data:</b> a script that runs <b>once</b>, automatically, <b>the first time</b> the instance starts, as the root user. It is used to install software and set things up. It does not run again on later restarts unless specially configured."]}]},{"title":"Instance types explained","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"An instance type name such as <code>t3.micro</code> or <code>m7g.2xlarge</code> has three parts:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>The letter = the family</b> (what it is designed for):"]}]},{"title":"Instance types explained","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Family</b>","<b>Designed for</b>"],["<b>T</b> (<code>t3</code>, <code>t4g</code>)","Burstable, cheap. Builds up “CPU credits” when quiet and spends them when busy. Good for small websites and tests"],["<b>M</b>","General purpose: a balance of CPU and memory"],["<b>C</b>","Compute optimised: more CPU (batch jobs, game servers)"],["<b>R / X</b>","Memory optimised: lots of RAM (databases, caches)"],["<b>I / D</b>","Storage optimised: very fast local disks"],["<b>G / P / Inf /</b> <b>Trn</b>","GPUs and machine-learning chips"]]}]},{"title":"Instance types explained","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>The number = the generation.</b> Higher is newer and usually better value. A <code>g</code> after the number (as in <code>m7g</code>) means an AWS <b>Graviton</b> chip, which is ARM-based: often cheaper, but programs must be built for ARM.","<b>The size:</b> <code>nano</code>, <code>micro</code>, <code>small</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>large</code>, <code>xlarge</code>, <code>2xlarge</code> and so on. Each step up usually doubles CPU and memory, and doubles the price."]}]},{"title":"Instance states","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>pending</b> → <b>running</b> (charged per second) → <b>stopping</b> → <b>stopped</b> (not charged for compute, still charged for the EBS disk) → <b>terminated</b> (deleted).","<b>Stopping</b> keeps the EBS disk. The instance usually gets a <b>new public IP</b> when started again, unless it has an <b>Elastic IP</b>: a fixed public IPv4 address you reserve.","<b>Terminating</b> deletes the instance. By default, the root disk is deleted too.","<b>Rebooting</b> keeps everything, including the IP addresses."]}]},{"title":"Ways to pay for EC2","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Option</b>","<b>How it works</b>","<b>Discount vs</b> <b>On-Demand</b>","<b>Use for</b>"],["<b>On-Demand</b>","Pay per second, no commitment","none","Short or unpredictable work, testing"],["<b>Savings Plans</b>","Commit to spend a fixed amount per hour for 1 or 3 years","up to about 72%","Steady, long-running work"],["<b>Reserved</b> <b>Instances</b>","Commit to a specific instance type in a Region for 1 or 3 years","up to about 72%","Steady work (Savings Plans are more flexible)"]]}]},{"title":"Ways to pay for EC2","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Option</b>","<b>How it works</b>","<b>Discount vs</b> <b>On-Demand</b>","<b>Use for</b>"],["<b>Spot Instances</b>","Use AWS’s spare capacity. AWS can take the instance back with a <b>2-minute warning</b>","up to about 90%","Work that can be interrupted: batch jobs, tests, CI runners"],["<b>Dedicated Hosts /</b> <b>Instances</b>","Hardware used only by you","costs more","Licensing rules or compliance"]]}]},{"title":"Connecting to an instance","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>SSH</b> (Linux): <code>ssh -i my-key.pem ec2-user@&lt;public-ip&gt;</code>. This needs port 22 open in the security group, a public IP (or a VPN), and the private key. The private key file must have strict permissions (<code>chmod 400 my-</code> <code>key.pem</code>), or SSH refuses to use it.","<b>EC2 Instance Connect:</b> pushes a temporary key for 60 seconds. You still need port 22."]}]},{"title":"Connecting to an instance","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":3,"items":["<b>Session Manager</b> (part of AWS Systems Manager): opens a shell in your browser or terminal <b>without</b> <b>opening port 22, without a public IP and without keys</b>. It needs the SSM agent on the instance (pre-installed on Amazon Linux) and an instance role with the <code>AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore</code> policy. Every session can be logged. ⚡ This is the recommended way."]}]},{"title":"The instance metadata service (IMDS)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["From inside an instance, programs can read information about it (its ID, its IP, and the temporary credentials of its IAM role) at the address <code>http://169.254.169.254</code>.","<b>IMDSv2</b> requires a session token first, which blocks a common type of attack. <b>Require IMDSv2</b> on all instances."]}]},{"title":"Placement groups (good to recognise)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Cluster:</b> instances close together for the fastest network between them.","<b>Spread:</b> each instance on different hardware, so one hardware failure affects only one.","<b>Partition:</b> groups of instances on separate racks (used by large distributed databases)."]}]},{"title":"Securing EC2: a checklist","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Use an <b>IAM role</b> for permissions, never access keys stored on the instance.","<b>Security groups</b> open only the ports needed. Never open port 22 or 3389 to <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> (the whole internet).","Use <b>Session Manager</b> instead of SSH.","Put instances in <b>private subnets</b> and expose them through a load balancer.","Keep the operating system <b>patched</b> (Systems Manager Patch Manager can automate this).","<b>Encrypt EBS volumes</b> (an account-wide default can be turned on)."]}]},{"title":"Securing EC2: a checklist","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":7,"items":["<b>Require IMDSv2.</b>","Monitor with <b>CloudWatch</b>, <b>GuardDuty</b> and <b>Inspector</b> (Chapters 23–24).","Take <b>snapshots</b> or use <b>AWS Backup</b> for backups."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> I do not use EC2 directly. I use <b>Fargate</b> (Chapter 20), where AWS manages the servers underneath my containers. Fargate still runs on EC2-style hardware behind the scenes, and EC2 is the most common interview topic, so it is worth knowing well."}]},{"title":"What goes wrong","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Losing the private key. You cannot download it again; use Session Manager or replace the key.","Expecting user data to run on every boot. It runs <b>once</b>.","Port 22 open to the whole internet.","Stopping an instance and losing its public IP (use an Elastic IP or a load balancer).","Forgetting that a <b>stopped</b> instance still costs money for its disk and any Elastic IP."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-12-elasticity-and-auto-scaling","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Compute (running programs)","num":"12","title":"Elasticity and Auto Scaling","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Scaling</b> means changing the amount of computing power to match demand."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Vertical scaling (scale up/down):</b> make one instance bigger or smaller. There is a maximum size, and resizing usually needs a stop and start.","<b>Horizontal scaling (scale out/in):</b> add or remove instances. There is no practical maximum, and this is the normal way in the cloud.","<b>Elasticity:</b> scaling out <b>and back in</b> automatically, so you pay only for what is needed at each moment."]}]},{"title":"Auto Scaling Groups (ASGs)","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"An <b>Auto Scaling Group</b> is a set of EC2 instances that AWS manages as one unit."}]},{"title":"Auto Scaling Groups (ASGs)","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Launch template:</b> says how to create each instance (AMI, instance type, security groups, user data, IAM role).","<b>Minimum, maximum and desired capacity:</b> the ASG never goes below the minimum or above the maximum, and aims for the desired number.","<b>Health checks:</b> if an instance fails its EC2 status check, or the load balancer’s health check (when that option is turned on), the ASG <b>replaces</b> it. This is called <b>self-healing</b>."]}]},{"title":"Auto Scaling Groups (ASGs)","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Multiple AZs:</b> the ASG spreads instances across the subnets you give it, and rebalances them if one AZ has more than the others.","<b>Load balancer integration:</b> new instances are automatically registered in the load balancer’s target group."]}]},{"title":"Scaling policies","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Policy</b>","<b>How it decides</b>","<b>Example</b>"],["<b>Target tracking</b>","Keeps a number at a target","“Keep average CPU at 50%”"],["<b>Step scaling</b>","Different changes for different alarm levels","“+1 instance at 60% CPU, +3 at 85%”"],["<b>Scheduled</b>","At set times","“At 08:45 on weekdays, set desired to 6”"],["<b>Predictive</b>","Uses past patterns to scale before demand arrives","Learns a daily traffic peak"]]}]},{"title":"Scaling policies","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Cooldown / warm-up:</b> a waiting time after a scaling action, so the group does not keep adding instances before the new ones are ready."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ Do not scale at 100% CPU. New instances take minutes to start, so set the target lower, such as 50–70%."}]},{"title":"Scenario: traffic peaks every Wednesday and Friday from 9 AM to 7 PM","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Use <b>scheduled scaling</b> to raise the minimum and desired capacity just before 9 AM on those days and lower it after 7 PM. Add <b>target tracking</b> as well, to handle unexpected spikes."}]},{"title":"Scaling containers and other services","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>ECS Service Auto Scaling</b> changes the number of <b>tasks</b> (containers), using target tracking on CPU, memory or requests per target.","<b>DynamoDB</b>, <b>Aurora</b> and <b>Lambda</b> have their own scaling settings."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> there is no auto scaling yet. The ECS service runs exactly one task (<code>desired_count = 1</code>). Adding ECS Service Auto Scaling with at least two tasks is a planned improvement. It first needs the data moved out of the container (Chapter 21)."}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-13-lambda-and-serverless-computing","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Compute (running programs)","num":"13","title":"Lambda and serverless computing","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>AWS Lambda</b> runs your code when an <b>event</b> happens, without you creating or managing any server. You upload a function, choose the language and memory, and connect it to a trigger."}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Many tasks are short and happen only sometimes: resize an image after upload, run a clean-up job every night, answer an API request. Keeping a server running all day for these wastes money. Lambda runs only when needed and charges only for that time."}]},{"title":"How it works","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Triggers (event sources):</b>","a file uploaded to <b>S3</b>,","an HTTP request through <b>API Gateway</b> or a <b>Lambda function URL</b>,","a schedule from <b>EventBridge</b> (for example, every day at 02:00),","a message in an <b>SQS</b> queue,","a change in a <b>DynamoDB</b> table, and many more.","<b>Limits to know</b> 🧠:","maximum running time <b>15 minutes</b>;"]}]},{"title":"How it works","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":9,"items":["memory <b>128 MB to 10,240 MB</b> (10 GB). CPU power increases with memory; temporary disk space in <code>/tmp</code> up to 10 GB.","<b>Pricing:</b> per request, plus per <b>GB-second</b>: memory multiplied by running time. Idle functions cost nothing.","<b>Scaling:</b> Lambda automatically runs more copies at the same time when more events arrive, up to an account limit (1,000 at once by default, which can be raised)."]}]},{"title":"How it works","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":12,"items":["<b>Cold start:</b> the first request after a quiet period takes longer, because Lambda has to prepare a new environment. <b>Provisioned concurrency</b> keeps environments ready, for a cost.","<b>Runtimes (languages):</b> AWS provides managed runtimes for Node.js, Python, Java, .NET and Ruby. Go and Rust run on the <b>OS-only runtime</b>. Any other language works using a <b>custom runtime</b> or by packaging the function as a <b>container image</b>.","<b>Permissions:</b> each function has an <b>execution role</b> (an IAM role) that decides what AWS services it can use."]}]},{"title":"How it works","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":15,"items":["<b>Networking:</b> by default a function can reach the internet. It can be attached to a VPC to reach private resources such as a database."]}]},{"title":"Serverless, explained exactly","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"“Serverless” does <b>not</b> mean there are no servers. It means:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["you do not create, update or manage servers;","capacity scales automatically;","you pay for use, not for idle time."]},{"type":"p","html":"Other serverless services: <b>S3</b>, <b>DynamoDB</b>, <b>SQS</b>, <b>SNS</b>, <b>API Gateway</b>, <b>Fargate</b>, <b>EventBridge</b>."}]},{"title":"Lambda vs containers vs EC2","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Use</b>","<b>Best choice</b>"],["Short jobs (under 15 minutes) triggered by events, or traffic that comes and goes","<b>Lambda</b>"],["A web application that runs all the time (like Memos)","<b>Containers (ECS/EKS on Fargate)</b>"],["Full control of the operating system, special software or licences","<b>EC2</b>"]]}]},{"title":"What goes wrong","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>The recursive loop:</b> a function triggered by uploads to a bucket, which saves its output into the <b>same</b> bucket, triggers itself again and again. This runs up a large bill. Always write the output to a <b>different</b> bucket (or a different prefix with a filter).","Jobs that take longer than 15 minutes. Use Fargate or AWS Batch instead.","Giving the execution role <code>*</code> permissions."]}]},{"title":"What goes wrong","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> Lambda is not used. A good use would be a <b>nightly backup function</b>, triggered by an EventBridge schedule."}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-14-vpc-virtual-private-cloud","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Networking","num":"14","title":"VPC (Virtual Private Cloud)","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"modules/vpc"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"modules/security"}],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A <b>VPC</b> is your own private, isolated network inside AWS. You choose its range of IP addresses, divide it into <b>subnets</b>, and control where traffic can go with <b>route tables</b>, <b>gateways</b> and <b>firewalls</b>. Every Region gives each account a <b>default VPC</b>, but real projects create their own."}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Resources such as servers and databases need IP addresses and a network to communicate. Different resources need different levels of exposure:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["a website’s load balancer must be reachable from the internet;","a database must <b>never</b> be."]},{"type":"p","html":"A VPC lets you build a network that enforces these rules."}]},{"title":"14.1 CIDR: how address ranges are written","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>CIDR notation</b> writes a range of IP addresses as a starting address and a number, such as <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>.","An IPv4 address is 32 <b>bits</b> (binary digits). The number after the slash says how many bits at the start are <b>fixed</b>. The rest can change, and each combination is one address."]},{"type":"note","kind":"recall","html":"⚡ <b>Formula: number of addresses = 2^(32 − the number after the slash).</b>"},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>CIDR</b>","<b>Addresses</b>","<b>Usable in an AWS subnet</b>","<b>Notes</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/16</code>"]}]},{"title":"14.1 CIDR: how address ranges are written","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["65,536","65,531","The largest VPC size allowed"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/20</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["4,096","4,091"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/24</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["256","<b>251</b>","A common subnet size"],["64","59",""]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/26</code>","<code>/28</code>"]}]},{"title":"14.1 CIDR: how address ranges are written","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["16","11","The smallest subnet size allowed"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>/32</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["1","n/a","Exactly one address (used in firewall rules)"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>0.0.0.0/0</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["every IPv4 address","n/a","Means “anywhere”"]]}]},{"title":"14.1 CIDR: how address ranges are written","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>AWS reserves 5 addresses in every subnet</b>:","the <b>first</b> (the network address),","the <b>second</b> (the VPC router),","the <b>third</b> (DNS),","the <b>fourth</b> (reserved for future use),","the <b>last</b> (the broadcast address).","So a <code>/24</code> has 256 − 5 = <b>251</b> usable addresses.","<b>Worked example:</b> <code>10.0.1.0/24</code> covers <code>10.0.1.0</code> to <code>10.0.1.255</code>. AWS keeps <code>.0</code>, <code>.1</code>, <code>.2</code>, <code>.3</code> and <code>.255</code>. You can use <code>.4</code> to <code>.254</code>."]}]},{"title":"14.1 CIDR: how address ranges are written","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":9,"items":["Splitting <code>10.0.0.0/24</code> into four <code>/26</code> subnets gives subnets starting at <code>.0</code>, <code>.64</code>, <code>.128</code> and <code>.192</code>, each with 64 addresses."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <b>Plan your ranges carefully.</b> Two VPCs whose ranges <b>overlap</b> cannot be connected later (peering or Transit Gateway). The same is true for your office network."}]},{"title":"14.2 Subnets","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>subnet</b> is a smaller range of addresses inside the VPC. ⚡ <b>Each subnet exists in exactly one AZ.</b>","To use two AZs, you need subnets in both.","A subnet is called <b>public</b> or <b>private only because of its route table</b> (next section). Its name does not change how it behaves."]},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Subnet type</b>","<b>Its route table has</b>","<b>Used for</b>"],["<b>Public</b>","",""]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>0.0.0.0/0 → internet gateway</code>"]}]},{"title":"14.2 Subnets","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","","Load balancers, NAT gateways, bastion hosts"],["<b>Private</b>","No route to an internet gateway. Often <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>","Application servers, containers"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>→ NAT gateway</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<b>Isolated</b> (private with no internet at all)","Only the local VPC route","Databases"]]}]},{"title":"14.3 Route tables","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>route table</b> is a list of rules. Each rule has:","a <b>destination</b> (a CIDR range),","a <b>target</b> (where to send traffic for that destination).","Every route table has a <b>local route</b> for the VPC’s own range (for example, <code>10.0.0.0/16 → local</code>), so all subnets in a VPC can reach each other. It cannot be removed.","When there are several matching routes, the <b>most specific one</b> (the longest prefix, the biggest number after the slash) is used."]}]},{"title":"14.3 Route tables","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["Each subnet is associated with <b>one</b> route table. A route table can be shared by many subnets."]}]},{"title":"14.4 The internet gateway (IGW)","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["An <b>internet gateway</b> connects a VPC to the internet. You create it and attach it to the VPC (one per VPC)."]},{"type":"note","kind":"recall","html":"⚡ <b>For an instance to be reachable from the internet, all of these must be true:</b>"}]},{"title":"14.4 The internet gateway (IGW)","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["an internet gateway is attached to the VPC;","the instance’s subnet route table has <code>0.0.0.0/0 → internet gateway</code>;","the instance has a <b>public IP</b> or an <b>Elastic IP</b>;","its <b>security group</b> allows the traffic;","the subnet’s <b>network ACL</b> allows the traffic in <b>and</b> the replies out."]}]},{"title":"14.5 NAT gateways","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Resources in <b>private</b> subnets often need to make <b>outgoing</b> connections: to download software updates, to pull container images, to call an outside API. They must not be reachable <b>from</b> the internet.","A <b>NAT gateway</b> solves this:","it sits in a <b>public</b> subnet with an <b>Elastic IP</b>;","private subnets’ route tables send <code>0.0.0.0/0 → NAT gateway</code>;"]}]},{"title":"14.5 NAT gateways","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["the NAT gateway replaces the private source address with its own public address (this is <b>network</b> <b>address translation</b>, NAT), sends the request out, and passes the reply back;","it does <b>not</b> allow connections that start from the internet.","A NAT gateway lives in <b>one AZ</b>. If that AZ fails, private subnets in other AZs that route through it lose internet access. For high availability, create <b>one NAT gateway per AZ</b>, with a private route table per AZ.","Cost: charged <b>per hour</b> and <b>per GB</b> processed, even when unused."]}]},{"title":"14.5 NAT gateways","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":9,"items":["A <b>NAT instance</b> is an older do-it-yourself version on EC2. It is cheaper but you must manage it."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos</b> (Terraform, <code>infra/modules/vpc/main.tf</code>, shortened):"},{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_nat_gateway\" \"main\" {\n  count         = length(var.public_subnet_cidrs)           # 2 → one per AZ\n  allocation_id = aws_eip.nat[count.index].id               # each NAT gets its own Elastic IP\n  subnet_id     = aws_subnet.public[count.index].id         # NAT gateways live in PUBLIC subnets\n}"}]},{"title":"14.5 NAT gateways","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_route_table\" \"private\" {\n  count  = length(var.private_subnet_cidrs)\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n  route {\n    cidr_block     = \"0.0.0.0/0\"                            # all internet-bound traffic...\n    nat_gateway_id = aws_nat_gateway.main[count.index].id   # ...goes to the NAT in the same AZ\n  }\n}"}]},{"title":"14.6 VPC endpoints","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>VPC endpoint</b> lets resources in your VPC reach an AWS service <b>privately</b>, without going through the internet or a NAT gateway."]},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Type</b>","<b>Works with</b>","<b>How it works</b>","<b>Cost</b>"],["<b>Gateway endpoint</b>","S3 and DynamoDB only","A route added to your route tables","<b>Free</b>"],["<b>Interface endpoint</b> (PrivateLink)","Most other services: ECR, CloudWatch Logs, Secrets Manager, SSM…","A network interface with a private IP in your subnet","Per hour + per GB"]]}]},{"title":"14.6 VPC endpoints","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Benefits: traffic stays inside AWS, and it can be cheaper than a NAT gateway when there is a lot of it."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> not used yet. Endpoints for ECR (<code>ecr.api</code> and <code>ecr.dkr</code>), S3 (the image layers are stored there) and CloudWatch Logs would cut NAT gateway traffic."}]},{"title":"14.7 Security groups (SGs)","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>security group</b> is a firewall attached to a resource’s <b>network interface</b> (an EC2 instance, a load balancer, an ECS task, a database).","Rules:","<b>Allow rules only.</b> You cannot write a deny rule.","<b>Stateful:</b> if incoming traffic is allowed, the reply is automatically allowed out, and the other way round.","<b>Default:</b> a new security group allows <b>no inbound</b> traffic and <b>all outbound</b> traffic."]}]},{"title":"14.7 Security groups (SGs)","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["A rule can use <b>another security group as the source</b>. This means “allow traffic from any resource that has that security group”. It keeps working as resources are added, removed or change IP address.","Each rule has a <b>protocol</b> (TCP, UDP, ICMP), a <b>port</b> or port range, and a <b>source</b> (inbound) or <b>destination</b> (outbound): a CIDR range or a security group."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos</b> (Terraform, <code>infra/modules/security/main.tf</code>, two of the five rules):"}]},{"title":"14.7 Security groups (SGs)","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"# Public HTTPS is required for visitors to access Memos.\n# tfsec:ignore:aws-ec2-no-public-ingress-sgr\nresource \"aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule\" \"alb_https\" {\n  security_group_id = aws_security_group.alb.id      # the load balancer's security group\n  cidr_ipv4         = \"0.0.0.0/0\"                    # from anywhere on the internet\n  ip_protocol       = \"tcp\"\n  from_port         = 443\n  to_port           = 443\n}"}]},{"title":"14.7 Security groups (SGs)","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule\" \"ecs_from_alb\" {\n  security_group_id            = aws_security_group.ecs.id   # the app's security group\n  referenced_security_group_id = aws_security_group.alb.id   # source = the load balancer's SG\n  ip_protocol                  = \"tcp\"\n  from_port                    = 8081\n  to_port                      = 8081\n}"}]},{"title":"14.7 Security groups (SGs)","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Security group</b>","<b>Allows in</b>","<b>Allows out</b>"],["Load balancer","80 and 443 from anywhere","only 8081 to the app’s security group"],["App (ECS tasks)","<b>only 8081 from the load balancer’s security group</b>","only 443 (to reach ECR and CloudWatch)"]]},{"type":"p","html":"So the internet can reach the load balancer, and only the load balancer can reach the app. The <code>tfsec:ignore</code> comment records a <b>deliberate exception</b>: a public load balancer must accept traffic from anywhere on port 443."}]},{"title":"14.8 Network ACLs (NACLs)","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>network ACL</b> is a firewall for a whole <b>subnet</b>."]}]},{"title":"14.8 Network ACLs (NACLs)","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>Security group</b>","<b>Network ACL</b>"],["Applies to","One network interface (resource)","A whole subnet"],["Rules","Allow only","Allow <b>and deny</b>"],["State","<b>Stateful</b> (replies allowed automatically)","<b>Stateless</b> (replies need their own rule)"],["Rule order","All rules checked together","Numbered rules, checked <b>lowest number first</b>; the first match wins"],["Default","No inbound, all outbound","The default NACL allows everything; a new custom NACL denies everything"]]}]},{"title":"14.8 Network ACLs (NACLs)","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Because NACLs are stateless, if you allow inbound port 443 you must also allow <b>outbound replies</b> to the <b>ephemeral ports</b>. These are the temporary high-numbered ports (1024–65535) that clients use for their side of a connection.","Use NACLs for broad subnet-level blocks, such as blocking a known bad IP range. Use security groups for everything else."]}]},{"title":"14.9 Connecting networks together (good to recognise)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Option</b>","<b>What it connects</b>"],["<b>VPC peering</b>","Two VPCs, directly (also across accounts and Regions). Not <b>transitive</b>: if A peers with B and B with C, A <b>cannot</b> reach C through B"],["<b>Transit</b> <b>Gateway</b>","Many VPCs and on-premises networks through one central hub"],["<b>Site-to-Site</b> <b>VPN</b>","Your office network to a VPC, over the internet, encrypted"],["<b>Direct</b> <b>Connect</b>","A private physical connection from your data centre to AWS: more consistent speed, not over the internet"],["<b>Client VPN</b>","Individual laptops to a VPC"]]}]},{"title":"14.10 VPC Flow Logs","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Flow Logs</b> record information about the IP traffic going to and from network interfaces: source, destination, port, and whether it was <b>ACCEPTED</b> or <b>REJECTED</b>. They are sent to CloudWatch Logs or S3. They are used to troubleshoot connectivity and investigate security incidents. They do <b>not</b> record the contents of the traffic."}]},{"title":"14.11 Building a VPC: the order of steps","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Create the <b>VPC</b> with a CIDR range, such as <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>.","Create <b>public and private subnets</b> in at least <b>two AZs</b>.","Create an <b>internet gateway</b> and attach it to the VPC.","Create a <b>public route table</b> with <code>0.0.0.0/0 → internet gateway</code>, and associate it with the public subnets.","Create a <b>NAT gateway</b> (with an Elastic IP) in each public subnet.","Create <b>private route table(s)</b> with <code>0.0.0.0/0 → NAT gateway</code>, and associate them with the private subnets."]}]},{"title":"14.11 Building a VPC: the order of steps","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":7,"items":["Create the <b>security groups</b>.","Optional: <b>VPC endpoints</b> and <b>Flow Logs</b>."]},{"type":"p","html":"To delete it, work in <b>reverse order</b>. A VPC cannot be deleted while resources still use it."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The Memos VPC:</b>"}]},{"title":"14.11 Building a VPC: the order of steps","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Part</b>","<b>Value</b>"],["VPC","<code>10.0.0.0/16</code>, <code>eu-west-1</code>"],["Public subnets","<code>10.0.1.0/24</code> (1a), <code>10.0.2.0/24</code> (1b): load balancer and NAT gateways"],["Private subnets","<code>10.0.3.0/24</code> (1a), <code>10.0.4.0/24</code> (1b): the app’s containers"],["Gateways","1 internet gateway, 2 NAT gateways (one per AZ)"]]}]},{"title":"14.12 Troubleshooting network problems","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>What you see</b>","<b>What it usually means</b>"],["<b>Connection timed out</b>","Something is <b>blocking</b> the traffic: a security group, a NACL or a missing route. The packets are being dropped silently"],["<b>Connection refused</b>","The traffic <b>reached</b> the machine, but <b>nothing is listening</b> on that port (the program is stopped, or listens on a different port)"],["Works from inside the VPC but not from the internet","Check the public IP, the internet gateway route, and the security group source range"]]}]},{"title":"14.12 Troubleshooting network problems","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>What you see</b>","<b>What it usually means</b>"],["Private instance cannot download updates","Check the NAT gateway route in its subnet’s route table, the NAT is in a public subnet, and the security group allows outbound"]]},{"type":"p","html":"Useful tools:"}]},{"title":"14.12 Troubleshooting network problems","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>ping</code> (uses ICMP, which is often blocked),","<code>traceroute</code> (shows each step on the way), <code>nc -zv host port</code> (tests whether one port is open),","<code>curl -v</code> (tests HTTP),","<code>dig</code> / <code>nslookup</code> (tests DNS),","<b>VPC Reachability Analyzer</b> (an AWS tool that checks the path between two resources),","<b>Flow Logs</b>."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-15-dns-and-route-53","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Networking","num":"15","title":"DNS and Route 53","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/alb/main.tf","label":"modules/alb"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/acm/main.tf","label":"modules/acm"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf (Route 53 record)"}],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>DNS</b> (Domain Name System) turns names such as <code>tm.myegh.com</code> into IP addresses. <b>Route 53</b> is AWS’s DNS service. It can also <b>register</b> (sell) domain names and <b>check the health</b> of servers. It is named after <b>port 53</b>, the port DNS uses. It is the only AWS service with a 100% availability promise (SLA)."}]},{"title":"The parts of a domain name","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"In <code>tm.myegh.com</code>:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>.com</code> is the <b>top-level domain</b> (TLD),","<code>myegh.com</code> is the <b>domain</b> you register,","<code>tm</code> is a <b>subdomain</b>."]}]},{"title":"How a DNS lookup works, step by step","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Your computer checks its own <b>cache</b> (recent answers it has stored) and its <code>hosts</code> file.","If it has no answer, it asks a <b>recursive resolver</b>. This is a DNS server run by your internet provider, your company, or a public one such as <code>8.8.8.8</code>.","The resolver asks a <b>root server</b>, which replies with the servers for <code>.com</code>.","The resolver asks a <code>.com</code> <b>TLD server</b>, which replies with the <b>name servers</b> for <code>myegh.com</code>."]}]},{"title":"How a DNS lookup works, step by step","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["The resolver asks the <b>authoritative name server</b> for the domain. This is the server that holds the real records; for my subdomain, it is Route 53. It replies with the answer.","The resolver gives the answer to your computer, and both keep it in their cache for the record’s <b>TTL</b> (time to live, in seconds)."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ When you change a record and nothing seems to change, the old answer is still cached until its TTL runs out."}]},{"title":"How a DNS lookup works, step by step","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<code>nslookup</code> saying <b>“Non-authoritative answer”</b> only means the answer came from a resolver’s cache, not"},{"type":"p","html":"directly from the authoritative server."}]},{"title":"Record types","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Type</b>","<b>Points a name to</b>","<b>Example</b>"],["<b>A</b>","An IPv4 address",""]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>app.example.com → 203.0.113.10</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<b>AAAA</b>","An IPv6 address"],["<b>CNAME</b>","<b>Another name</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>www.example.com → example.com</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<b>ALIAS</b> (Route 53","An <b>AWS resource</b>, such as a load balancer or CloudFront"]]}]},{"title":"Record types","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>tm.myegh.com → my load</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["only)"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>balancer</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<b>MX</b>","Mail servers for the domain"],["<b>TXT</b>","Text, used to prove ownership and for email security settings"],["<b>NS</b>","The name servers responsible for the domain"],["<b>SOA</b>","Administrative information about the zone"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Alias vs CNAME</b> 🧠:"}]},{"title":"Record types","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A CNAME <b>cannot be used at the zone apex</b>. The apex is the bare domain, like <code>myegh.com</code>, with nothing in front.","An <b>alias record can</b> be used at the apex.","An alias points at an AWS resource and <b>automatically follows it</b> if its IP addresses change.","Route 53 does not charge for alias queries to AWS resources.","An alias has no TTL of its own; it uses the target’s."]}]},{"title":"Hosted zones","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>hosted zone</b> is a container for all the records of one domain or subdomain.","A <b>public hosted zone</b> answers questions from the internet.","A <b>private hosted zone</b> answers only inside the VPCs you link it to. It is used for internal names such as <code>db.internal</code>.","Each hosted zone costs $0.50 per month (about £0.40), plus a small charge per query."]}]},{"title":"Registrar vs DNS hosting, and delegation","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>registrar</b> is the company where you <b>bought</b> the domain.","<b>DNS hosting</b> is the service that <b>answers</b> questions about it.","These can be different companies. The registrar’s <b>NS records</b> point to whoever does the DNS hosting.","<b>Delegation</b> means pointing a domain or subdomain at different name servers using NS records."]}]},{"title":"Registrar vs DNS hosting, and delegation","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> the domain <code>myegh.com</code> is registered at <b>Fasthosts</b>. I created a Route 53 hosted zone and added <b>Route 53’s four NS records for</b> <code>tm</code> at Fasthosts. From then on, Route 53 answers for <code>tm.myegh.com</code>. Until the change spread, the name did not resolve. <code>dig +trace tm.myegh.com</code> shows every step of the lookup and is the best way to find where it breaks."}]},{"title":"Routing policies","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Routing policies control <b>which answer</b> Route 53 gives. They do not control the traffic itself."}]},{"title":"Routing policies","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Policy</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["<b>Simple</b>","One answer (or a random one of several)"],["<b>Weighted</b>","Splits answers by percentage, e.g. 90% to version 1 and 10% to version 2"],["<b>Latency-based</b>","Answers with the Region that gives the user the lowest latency"],["<b>Failover</b>","Answers with the primary resource while its <b>health check</b> passes, otherwise with the secondary"],["<b>Geolocation</b>","Answers based on the user’s country or continent"],["<b>Geoproximity</b>","Answers based on distance, with an adjustable bias"]]}]},{"title":"Routing policies","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Policy</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["<b>Multivalue answer</b>","Up to 8 healthy answers"],["<b>IP-based</b>","Answers based on the user’s IP range"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Health checks</b> test an endpoint regularly. Failover routing uses them to stop sending users to a broken resource."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The Memos alias record</b> (Terraform, root <code>infra/main.tf</code>):"}]},{"title":"Routing policies","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"data \"aws_route53_zone\" \"main\" {        # the zone already exists, so READ it, don't create it\n  name         = var.domain_name\n  private_zone = false\n}"}]},{"title":"Routing policies","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_route53_record\" \"app\" {\n  zone_id = data.aws_route53_zone.main.zone_id\n  name    = \"tm.${var.domain_name}\"     # tm.myegh.com\n  type    = \"A\"\n  alias {\n    name                   = module.alb.dns_name\n    zone_id                = module.alb.alb_zone_id\n    evaluate_target_health = true       # only answer if the load balancer has healthy targets\n  }\n}"}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-16-load-balancers","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Networking","num":"16","title":"Load balancers","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/alb/main.tf","label":"modules/alb"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/acm/main.tf","label":"modules/acm"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf (Route 53 record)"}],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A <b>load balancer</b> is a single entry point that receives requests and sends each one to one of several <b>targets</b> (servers, containers, IP addresses or Lambda functions). It sends traffic only to targets that pass <b>health checks</b>. AWS’s load balancers are called <b>Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)</b>."}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["One server can fail, and one server can only handle so many users.","With a load balancer, you can run several targets across several AZs. Users connect to <b>one</b> address, and if a target fails, the load balancer stops sending it traffic.","It also lets you replace targets (new versions, scaling) without users noticing."]}]},{"title":"The types","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Type</b>","<b>Works at</b>","<b>Understands</b>","<b>Use for</b>"],["<b>Application Load</b> <b>Balancer (ALB)</b>","Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS)","Paths, hostnames, headers, cookies","Websites, APIs, containers. Can route <code>/api</code> and <code>/images</code> to different targets"],["<b>Network Load</b> <b>Balancer (NLB)</b>","Layer 4 (TCP/UDP/TLS)","IPs and ports only","Very high performance, static IP per AZ, non- HTTP traffic. <b>Can</b> terminate TLS"],["<b>Gateway Load</b> <b>Balancer (GWLB)</b>","Layer 3","Network packets","Placing firewall and inspection appliances in the path of traffic"]]}]},{"title":"The types","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Type</b>","<b>Works at</b>","<b>Understands</b>","<b>Use for</b>"],["<b>Classic Load Balancer</b>","Old","","Do not use for new projects"]]},{"type":"p","html":"“Layer 7” and “layer 4” refer to the <b>OSI model</b>, a 7-layer description of networking. Layer 7 is the application (HTTP), layer 4 is transport (TCP/UDP), layer 3 is network (IP)."}]},{"title":"How an ALB is put together","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Listener:</b> a port and protocol the load balancer accepts, such as HTTPS on 443. An HTTPS listener needs a <b>certificate</b>.","<b>Rules</b> on the listener: conditions (host, path, header) and an <b>action</b>:"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>forward</b> to a target group,","<b>redirect</b> (for example HTTP to HTTPS),","return a <b>fixed response</b>."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":3,"items":["<b>Target group:</b> the list of targets, with the port and protocol to reach them. Target types:"]}]},{"title":"How an ALB is put together","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>instance</b> (EC2 instance ID),","<b>ip</b> (needed for Fargate),","<b>lambda</b>."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":4,"items":["<b>Health check:</b> a regular request to each target, such as <code>GET /</code> every 30 seconds. A target is <b>healthy</b> after a set number of successes (the <b>healthy threshold</b>) and <b>unhealthy</b> after a set number of failures (the <b>unhealthy threshold</b>). Only healthy targets receive traffic."]}]},{"title":"Other features to know","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>TLS termination:</b> the load balancer decrypts HTTPS and passes plain HTTP to targets inside the private network. Certificates are managed in one place. (For encryption all the way to the target, the target must also use TLS.)","<code>X-Forwarded-For</code> <b>header:</b> the load balancer puts the visitor’s real IP address in this header, because targets","see the load balancer’s address as the source.","<b>Sticky sessions:</b> send one user to the same target every time, using a cookie. Avoid them where possible, because they make balancing uneven."]}]},{"title":"Other features to know","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<b>Deregistration delay</b> (connection draining): when a target is removed, it gets time (300 seconds by default) to finish requests already in progress.","<b>Cross-zone load balancing:</b> spreads traffic evenly across targets in all AZs.","<b>Internet-facing vs internal:</b> an internet-facing load balancer has public addresses; an internal one has only private addresses."]}]},{"title":"What the error codes mean","part":"1/6","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<b>Code</b>","<b>Meaning</b>","🔧 <b>My real cause in Memos</b>"],["<b>502 Bad Gateway</b>","The load balancer reached a target, but the reply was invalid or the connection closed","The app was listening on a different port from the target group"],["<b>503 Service</b> <b>Unavailable</b>","There are <b>no healthy targets</b>","Every task was failing its health check"],["<b>504 Gateway</b> <b>Timeout</b>","The target did not reply in time","The app’s security group was blocking the load balancer"]]}]},{"title":"What the error codes mean","part":"2/6","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The Memos load balancer</b> (Terraform, <code>infra/modules/alb/main.tf</code>):"}]},{"title":"What the error codes mean","part":"3/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_lb_listener\" \"http\" {\n  load_balancer_arn = aws_lb.this.arn\n  port              = 80\n  protocol          = \"HTTP\"\n  default_action {\n    type = \"redirect\"                 # port 80 never serves the app...\n    redirect {\n      port        = \"443\"             # ...it sends the browser to HTTPS\n      protocol    = \"HTTPS\"\n      status_code = \"HTTP_301\"        # 301 = moved permanently\n    }\n  }\n}"}]},{"title":"What the error codes mean","part":"4/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_lb_listener\" \"https\" {\n  load_balancer_arn = aws_lb.this.arn\n  port              = 443\n  protocol          = \"HTTPS\"\n  ssl_policy        = \"ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06\"   # allows TLS 1.2 and 1.3 only\n  certificate_arn   = var.certificate_arn                     # the ACM certificate\n  default_action {\n    type             = \"forward\"\n    target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.this.arn\n  }\n}"}]},{"title":"What the error codes mean","part":"5/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_lb_target_group\" \"this\" {\n  name        = \"${var.project_name}-tg\"\n  port        = 8081                  # the port the app listens on\n  protocol    = \"HTTP\"                # plain HTTP inside the VPC: TLS already ended at the ALB\n  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id\n  target_type = \"ip\"                  # Fargate tasks are registered by IP address\n  health_check {\n    path                = \"/\"\n    matcher             = \"200-399\"   # any of these status codes counts as healthy\n    interval            = 30          # seconds between checks\n    timeout             = 5           # seconds to wait for a reply\n    healthy_threshold   = 2\n    unhealthy_threshold = 2\n  }\n}"}]},{"title":"What the error codes mean","part":"6/6","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"The load balancer also sets <code>drop_invalid_header_fields = true</code>, a security setting that removes malformed HTTP headers."}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-17-certificates-acm-and-cloudfront","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Networking","num":"17","title":"Certificates (ACM) and CloudFront","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/alb/main.tf","label":"modules/alb"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/acm/main.tf","label":"modules/acm"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf (Route 53 record)"}],"cards":[{"title":"ACM (AWS Certificate Manager)","part":"1/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> ACM issues <b>TLS certificates</b> for HTTPS. A certificate is a file that proves a website owns its name. It contains the <b>public key</b> used to set up encryption, and is signed by a trusted <b>certificate authority</b> (CA).","<b>Public certificates from ACM are free</b> and <b>renew automatically</b>. They can only be used <b>on AWS services</b>: load balancers, CloudFront and API Gateway. You cannot download them to install elsewhere.","<b>Proving you own the domain:</b>"]}]},{"title":"ACM (AWS Certificate Manager)","part":"2/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>DNS validation (recommended):</b> ACM gives you a special <b>CNAME record</b>. When it exists in DNS, ACM issues the certificate and keeps renewing it automatically, as long as the record stays.","<b>Email validation:</b> ACM emails the domain owner.","<b>Region rule:</b> a certificate for a load balancer must be in the <b>same Region</b> as the load balancer. A certificate for <b>CloudFront</b> must be in <code>us-east-1</code>.","<b>Wildcard certificates:</b> <code>*.example.com</code> covers every subdomain one level down."]}]},{"title":"ACM (AWS Certificate Manager)","part":"3/6","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>ACM validation in Memos, fully automated</b> (<code>infra/modules/acm/main.tf</code>):"},{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_acm_certificate\" \"this\" {\n  domain_name               = var.domain_name              # myegh.com\n  subject_alternative_names = [\"tm.${var.domain_name}\"]    # also covers tm.myegh.com\n  validation_method         = \"DNS\"\n  lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }               # make the new cert before removing the old\n}"}]},{"title":"ACM (AWS Certificate Manager)","part":"4/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_route53_record\" \"validation\" {\n  for_each = {                                             # one record per name on the certificate\n    for option in aws_acm_certificate.this.domain_validation_options :\n    option.domain_name => {\n      name   = option.resource_record_name\n      type   = option.resource_record_type\n      record = option.resource_record_value\n    }\n  }\n  zone_id = var.zone_id\n  name    = each.value.name\n  type    = each.value.type\n  records = [each.value.record]\n  ttl     = 60\n}"}]},{"title":"ACM (AWS Certificate Manager)","part":"5/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_acm_certificate_validation\" \"this\" {         # waits until ACM says \"Issued\"\n  certificate_arn         = aws_acm_certificate.this.arn\n  validation_record_fqdns = [for record in aws_route53_record.validation : record.fqdn]\n}"}]},{"title":"ACM (AWS Certificate Manager)","part":"6/6","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>What went wrong first:</b> before this was automated, the certificate stayed at <b>“Pending validation”</b> for <code>tm.myegh.com</code> because the validation record was missing. I created the record with the CLI (<code>aws route53</code> <code>change-resource-record-sets</code>) and checked it with <code>dig _xxxx.tm.myegh.com CNAME</code>. Separately, Terraform once tried to create the HTTPS listener before the certificate was ready and failed with <code>UnsupportedCertificate</code>. The <code>aws_acm_certificate_validation</code> resource fixes this by making Terraform wait."}]},{"title":"CloudFront","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> AWS’s <b>content delivery network (CDN)</b>. It keeps <b>cached copies</b> of your content at <b>edge</b> <b>locations</b> near users. A cache is a stored copy that can be returned without asking the original server again.","<b>The origin</b> is where the real content comes from: an <b>S3 bucket</b>, a <b>load balancer</b>, or any web server.","<b>How a request is answered:</b>"]}]},{"title":"CloudFront","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["A user requests a file.","The nearest edge location checks its cache.","If it has a valid copy, that is a <b>cache hit</b>, and it returns it immediately.","If not (a <b>cache miss</b>), it fetches the file from the origin, returns it, and stores it for next time, according to the cache settings (<b>TTL</b>)."]}]},{"title":"CloudFront","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Benefits:</b> faster pages for distant users, less load on the origin, protection from <b>AWS Shield Standard</b> (DDoS protection), and the option to add <b>AWS WAF</b> (web application firewall).","<b>Private S3 origins:</b> use <b>Origin Access Control (OAC)</b>, which replaces the older Origin Access Identity (OAI). The bucket then stays private, and only CloudFront can read it.","<b>Invalidation:</b> forces edge locations to delete cached copies before their TTL runs out, after you publish new content."]}]},{"title":"CloudFront","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> CloudFront is not used. A reviewer asked how I would serve users in other countries. The answer is to put CloudFront in front of the load balancer."}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-18-s3-simple-storage-service","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Storage","num":"18","title":"S3 (Simple Storage Service)","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/state.tf","label":"bootstrap/state.tf (state bucket)"}],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>S3</b> stores <b>objects</b> (files of any type) inside <b>buckets</b>. It can hold an unlimited amount of data. You reach it over HTTPS, through the Console, the CLI, SDKs or a URL."}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Applications need somewhere to keep files that is:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["always available,","extremely unlikely to lose data,","reachable from anywhere,","and that never runs out of space."]},{"type":"p","html":"Building that yourself takes many servers and disks. S3 provides it as a service, and you pay only per GB stored and per request."}]},{"title":"Words to know","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Word</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>Bucket</b>","A container for objects. Its name must be <b>unique across all AWS accounts in the world</b>, 3–63 characters, lowercase. A bucket is created in one Region"],["<b>Object</b>","One file plus its <b>metadata</b> (information about it, such as content type). An object can be up to <b>50 TB</b>"],["<b>Key</b>","The object’s full name, e.g. <code>backups/2026/notes.db</code>. S3 has no real folders: the <code>/</code> characters in keys are shown as folders in the Console. The part before the file name is called a <b>prefix</b>"]]}]},{"title":"Words to know","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Word</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>Version</b> <b>ID</b>","When versioning is on, every change creates a new version with its own ID"]]}]},{"title":"Durability and availability (often confused)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Durability</b> is how unlikely it is that data is <b>lost</b>. S3 Standard is designed for <b>99.999999999%</b> (“eleven nines”) durability, because it stores copies across at least three AZs.","<b>Availability</b> is how often you can <b>reach</b> the data. S3 Standard is designed for 99.99%.","S3 has <b>strong read-after-write consistency</b>: after you write or delete an object, the next read immediately sees the change."]}]},{"title":"Storage classes","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Class</b>","<b>Use for</b>","<b>Retrieval</b>"],["<b>S3 Standard</b>","Data used often","Instant"],["<b>S3 Intelligent-Tiering</b>","Data with unknown or changing use. S3 moves objects between tiers automatically","Instant (for the frequent and infrequent tiers)"],["<b>S3 Standard-IA</b> (Infrequent Access)","Data used rarely but needed instantly. Cheaper storage, but a charge per GB retrieved","Instant"],["<b>S3 One Zone-IA</b>","Like Standard-IA but in <b>one AZ only</b> (lost if that AZ is destroyed). For data you can recreate","Instant"]]}]},{"title":"Storage classes","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Class</b>","<b>Use for</b>","<b>Retrieval</b>"],["<b>S3 Express One Zone</b>","Very fast access, in one AZ","Instant, very low latency"],["<b>S3 Glacier Instant</b> <b>Retrieval</b>","Archive data read about once a quarter","Instant"],["<b>S3 Glacier Flexible</b> <b>Retrieval</b>","Archives","Minutes to hours"],["<b>S3 Glacier Deep Archive</b>","Long-term archives (kept for years, e.g. for legal reasons). Cheapest storage","Up to 12–48 hours"]]}]},{"title":"Storage classes","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Cheaper storage classes usually have a <b>minimum storage time</b> and a <b>retrieval charge</b>."}]},{"title":"Lifecycle rules","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Lifecycle rules</b> move or delete objects automatically based on their age. Example:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["move logs to Standard-IA after 30 days,","move them to Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days,","delete them after 365 days."]},{"type":"p","html":"Rules can also delete old <b>non-current versions</b> and incomplete uploads."}]},{"title":"Versioning","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["When <b>versioning</b> is on, overwriting an object keeps the old version.","Deleting an object adds a <b>delete marker</b> (the old versions are still there, so the object can be restored).","It protects against accidental deletes and overwrites.","Once turned on, it can only be <b>suspended</b>, not fully removed."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 Versioning is turned on for my <b>Terraform state bucket</b>, so an older state file can be restored if the current one is damaged."}]},{"title":"Security for S3","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Block Public Access:</b> four settings that stop any bucket or object being made public. <b>On by default for new</b> <b>buckets.</b> Leave them on unless the bucket really must be public (such as a public website).","<b>Bucket policies:</b> resource-based policies (Chapter 9) attached to the bucket. They are used to:","allow other AWS accounts,","require HTTPS,","allow only certain VPC endpoints."]}]},{"title":"Security for S3","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["<b>ACLs</b> (access control lists) are an old way to set permissions. They are <b>disabled by default</b> on new buckets (“bucket owner enforced”). Use policies instead.","<b>Encryption at rest</b> (encrypting stored data):","<b>SSE-S3:</b> S3 manages the keys. Applied to <b>all new objects by default</b>.","<b>SSE-KMS:</b> keys in AWS KMS (Chapter 25). Gives control of who can use the key, and a record of every use.","<b>DSSE-KMS:</b> two layers of encryption."]}]},{"title":"Security for S3","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":11,"items":["<b>Client-side encryption:</b> you encrypt the data before uploading.","<b>Encryption in transit:</b> use HTTPS. A bucket policy can deny any request that is not HTTPS.","<b>Presigned URLs:</b> a URL that gives temporary access (for example, 15 minutes) to one private object, without making it public. Created with an SDK or <code>aws s3 presign</code>.","<b>Object Lock:</b> stops objects being deleted or changed for a set period. Used for legal and compliance records."]}]},{"title":"Security for S3","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The Memos Terraform state bucket</b> has versioning, AES256 encryption, all four Block Public Access settings on, <code>prevent_destroy</code> in Terraform, and a policy that <b>denies any request not using HTTPS</b>:"}]},{"title":"Security for S3","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_s3_bucket_policy\" \"state\" {\n  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.state.id\n  policy = jsonencode({\n    Version = \"2012-10-17\"\n    Statement = [{\n      Sid       = \"DenyInsecureTransport\"\n      Effect    = \"Deny\"                                   # an explicit deny always wins\n      Principal = \"*\"                                      # applies to everyone\n      Action    = \"s3:*\"\n      Resource  = [aws_s3_bucket.state.arn, \"${aws_s3_bucket.state.arn}/*\"]\n      Condition = { Bool = { \"aws:SecureTransport\" = \"false\" } }   # when the request is NOT HTTPS\n    }]\n  })\n}"}]},{"title":"Other S3 features","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Static website hosting:</b> S3 can serve a website made only of files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript). Usually you put <b>CloudFront</b> in front of it, with a private bucket and OAC.","<b>Replication:</b>","<b>Cross-Region Replication (CRR)</b> copies objects to a bucket in another Region (for disaster recovery);","<b>Same-Region Replication (SRR)</b> copies to another bucket in the same Region.","Versioning must be on."]}]},{"title":"Other S3 features","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["<b>Event notifications:</b> S3 can trigger Lambda, SQS, SNS or EventBridge when objects are created or deleted.","<b>Multipart upload:</b> large files are uploaded in parts, in parallel. It is recommended above 100 MB and needed above 5 GB.","<b>Transfer Acceleration:</b> faster uploads from far away, through edge locations.","<b>S3 Inventory, Storage Lens:</b> reports of what you are storing."]}]},{"title":"Useful commands","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"aws s3 mb s3://my-unique-bucket-name                 # make a bucket\naws s3 cp notes.txt s3://my-unique-bucket-name/      # upload a file\naws s3 ls s3://my-unique-bucket-name/                # list objects\naws s3 sync ./site s3://my-unique-bucket-name/       # upload only changed files\naws s3 presign s3://my-unique-bucket-name/notes.txt --expires-in 900   # 15-minute link\naws s3 rb s3://my-unique-bucket-name --force         # delete the bucket and everything in it"}]},{"title":"What goes wrong","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>BucketAlreadyExists</code>: the name is taken by someone, anywhere in the world. 🔧 This happened to me.","Turning off Block Public Access “to make it work”. Find the real permission problem instead.","Confusing durability with availability.","Deleting a versioned object and expecting the space to be freed. The old versions are still stored, so add a lifecycle rule."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-19-block-storage-file-storage-and-backups","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Storage","num":"19","title":"Block storage, file storage and backups","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/state.tf","label":"bootstrap/state.tf (state bucket)"}],"cards":[{"title":"EBS (Elastic Block Store)","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> a virtual hard disk, called a <b>volume</b>, that you attach to an EC2 instance. “Block storage” means the operating system sees it as a raw disk that it formats with a file system.","<b>Rules:</b>","An EBS volume lives in <b>one AZ</b> and can only attach to instances in that AZ.","Usually one instance at a time (some types allow “Multi-Attach”).","It keeps its data when the instance stops.","<b>Volume types:</b>"]}]},{"title":"EBS (Elastic Block Store)","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Type</b>","<b>Kind</b>","<b>Use for</b>"],["<b>gp3</b>","SSD, general purpose. 3,000 IOPS and 125 MB/s included, more can be added separately from size","Most workloads (the default choice)"],["<b>gp2</b>","Older SSD type. Speed depends on size","Older setups"],["<b>io2 Block</b> <b>Express</b>","SSD with the highest, guaranteed performance","Large databases"],["<b>st1</b>","Hard disk, high throughput","Big sequential reads (logs, data processing)"],["<b>sc1</b>","Hard disk, lowest cost","Data rarely accessed"]]}]},{"title":"EBS (Elastic Block Store)","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>IOPS</b> = input/output operations per second (how many reads and writes per second). <b>Throughput</b> = MB per second. - <b>Snapshots:</b> - A <b>snapshot</b> is a backup copy of a volume, stored by AWS in S3 (you do not see it in your buckets). - Snapshots are <b>incremental</b>: after the first, only changed blocks are saved. - You can create a new volume from a snapshot, <b>in any AZ</b>. This is how you move a volume between AZs. - You can copy snapshots to other Regions. - <b>Encryption:</b> EBS volumes can be encrypted with KMS. Snapshots of encrypted volumes are encrypted too. You can turn on encryption by default for the whole account."}]},{"title":"Instance store","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Some EC2 instance types have <b>instance store</b>: disks physically attached to the host server. They are very fast."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ The data is <b>lost</b> when the instance stops, is terminated, or the hardware fails. Use it only for temporary data such as caches."}]},{"title":"EFS (Elastic File System)","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> a shared <b>file system</b> (folders and files) that <b>many</b> EC2 instances, containers and Lambda functions can use at the same time, across <b>multiple AZs</b>. It uses the <b>NFS</b> protocol (Linux).","It grows and shrinks automatically. You pay per GB stored.","Storage classes include Standard and Infrequent Access, with lifecycle management.","It costs more per GB than EBS."]}]},{"title":"EFS (Elastic File System)","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> the notes are stored in an SQLite database file <b>inside the container</b>. When the container is replaced, on every deploy, <b>the data is lost</b>. Mounting <b>EFS</b> at <code>/var/opt/memos</code> would fix this. So would moving to a database service (Chapter 21)."}]},{"title":"Other storage (good to recognise)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Service</b>","<b>What it is</b>"],["<b>FSx for Windows File Server</b>","Shared Windows file shares (SMB)"],["<b>FSx for Lustre</b>","Very fast file system for high-performance computing"],["<b>Storage Gateway</b>","Connects on-premises systems to cloud storage"],["<b>Snowball</b>","A physical device AWS posts to you, to move very large amounts of data"]]}]},{"title":"Choosing storage","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>You need</b>","<b>Use</b>"],["Files of any size, reachable over HTTPS, from anywhere","<b>S3</b>"],["A disk for one EC2 instance","<b>EBS</b>"],["A folder shared by many servers or containers","<b>EFS</b> (Linux) or <b>FSx</b> (Windows)"],["Temporary, very fast scratch space","<b>Instance store</b>"],["Long-term archive","<b>S3 Glacier</b> classes"]]}]},{"title":"AWS Backup","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>AWS Backup</b> creates and manages backups for many services (EBS, EFS, RDS, DynamoDB, S3) from one place:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>backup plans</b> set a schedule and how long to keep each backup;","backups can be copied to other Regions or accounts;","<b>vault lock</b> stops backups being deleted early."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-20-containers-on-aws-docker-ecr-ecs-fargate-eks","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Containers and databases","num":"20","title":"Containers on AWS (Docker, ECR, ECS, Fargate, EKS)","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile","label":"Dockerfile (3 stages)"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile.single-stage","label":"Dockerfile.single-stage"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"modules/ecs"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"modules/ecr"}],"cards":[{"title":"What a container is","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>container</b> is a running program packaged together with everything it needs:","its code,","the language runtime (such as Node.js or Go),","libraries,","settings.","It runs the same way on any machine that can run containers.","Containers are isolated from each other using two Linux kernel features:","<b>namespaces</b>, which control what a container can <b>see</b> (its own processes, network and files);"]}]},{"title":"What a container is","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":9,"items":["<b>cgroups</b>, which control how much it can <b>use</b> (CPU and memory).","Containers share the host’s <b>kernel</b>, the core part of the operating system. So they are much smaller than virtual machines (megabytes instead of gigabytes) and start in seconds.","An <b>image</b> is the read-only template a container is created from. A <b>container</b> is a running instance of an image. One image can run as many containers.","<b>Docker</b> is the most common tool for building images and running containers."]}]},{"title":"What a container is","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":13,"items":["A <b>Dockerfile</b> is a text file with the instructions to build an image."]}]},{"title":"The Dockerfile instructions you need","part":"1/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Instruction</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["","The base image to start from, e.g. <code>alpine:3.21</code>. Always pin a version, not <code>latest</code>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>FROM</code>","<code>WORKDIR</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Sets the current folder for the following instructions"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>COPY</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Copies files from your computer into the image"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>RUN</code>"]}]},{"title":"The Dockerfile instructions you need","part":"2/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Runs a command <b>while building</b> the image (e.g. install software). Each <code>RUN</code> adds a <b>layer</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>ENV</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Sets environment variables (settings the program reads when it starts)"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>USER</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Which user the container runs as. Use a non-root user"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>EXPOSE</code>"]}]},{"title":"The Dockerfile instructions you need","part":"3/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>Documents</b> the port the program listens on. It does not open or publish anything"],["<code>ENTRYPOINT</code> /","The command run <b>every time</b> a container starts. <code>ENTRYPOINT</code> is the program; <code>CMD</code> gives default"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>CMD</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["arguments that can be overridden"]]}]},{"title":"The Dockerfile instructions you need","part":"4/9","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Layers and caching:</b> each instruction creates a layer. If the files used by an instruction have not changed, Docker reuses the stored layer, which makes builds faster. So copy the dependency list and install dependencies <b>before</b> copying your code: dependencies change less often than code."}]},{"title":"The Dockerfile instructions you need","part":"5/9","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Multi-stage builds:</b> you use one stage with all the build tools to compile the program, then copy <b>only the</b> <b>finished program</b> into a small final stage. The final image contains no compilers or source code, so it is smaller and has fewer security problems."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The Memos Dockerfile, explained:</b>"}]},{"title":"The Dockerfile instructions you need","part":"6/9","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"# Stage 1: build the web interface (JavaScript) with Node.js\nFROM node:22-alpine AS frontend\nWORKDIR /build/web\nRUN npm install --global pnpm@10.24.0\n# dependency list first (this layer is cached until the files change)\nCOPY app/web/package.json app/web/pnpm-lock.yaml ./\n# install exactly the versions in the lock file\nRUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile\n# then the source code, and build the static web files\nCOPY app/web/ ./\nRUN pnpm release"}]},{"title":"The Dockerfile instructions you need","part":"7/9","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"# Stage 2: build the Go program, with the web files embedded in it\nFROM golang:1.25-alpine AS backend\nWORKDIR /build\nRUN apk add --no-cache git ca-certificates\nCOPY app/go.mod app/go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY app/ ./\nCOPY --from=frontend /build/server/router/frontend/dist ./server/router/frontend/dist\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -tags netgo,osusergo -o /memos ./cmd/memos"}]},{"title":"The Dockerfile instructions you need","part":"8/9","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"# Stage 3: the small final image that actually runs\nFROM alpine:3.21 AS runtime\nRUN apk add --no-cache ca-certificates tzdata && \\\n    addgroup -S -g 1001 appuser && \\\n    adduser -S -D -u 1001 -G appuser appuser && \\\n    mkdir -p /var/opt/memos && \\\n    chown appuser:appuser /var/opt/memos\n# copy only the finished program from stage 2\nCOPY --from=backend /memos /usr/local/bin/memos\nWORKDIR /var/opt/memos\nENV MEMOS_PORT=8081 MEMOS_DATA=/var/opt/memos\n# run as a normal user, not root\nUSER 1001:1001\nEXPOSE 8081\nENTRYPOINT [\"memos\"]"}]},{"title":"The Dockerfile instructions you need","part":"9/9","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Note: in a Dockerfile, a comment must be on its own line starting with <code>#</code>. A <code>#</code> at the end of an instruction line is not treated as a comment."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Result:</b> the image went from <b>2.79 GB</b> (a single stage) to <b>75.8 MB</b>, about 97% smaller.","<code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code> makes one self-contained program that needs no C libraries, so it runs on the minimal Alpine","image.","The non-root user (1001) cannot use ports below 1024, which is why the app listens on <b>8081</b>."]}]},{"title":"Running containers on AWS: the options","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Service</b>","<b>What it is</b>","<b>You manage</b>"],["<b>ECS on Fargate</b>","AWS’s container service; AWS runs the servers","Only your containers and settings"],["<b>ECS on EC2</b>","AWS’s container service on EC2 instances you provide","The EC2 instances too"],["<b>EKS</b>","Managed <b>Kubernetes</b> (the open-source container system)","Worker nodes (unless using Fargate or Auto Mode), plus Kubernetes itself"],["<b>App Runner</b>","Simplest: give it an image, get a website","Almost nothing"]]}]},{"title":"Running containers on AWS: the options","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Service</b>","<b>What it is</b>","<b>You manage</b>"],["<b>Lambda (container</b> <b>image)</b>","Run a container image as a Lambda function","Under 15 minutes per run"]]}]},{"title":"ECR (Elastic Container Registry)","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> a <b>private registry</b>, a storage service for container images, in your AWS account. Docker Hub is a public registry; ECR is private by default.","<b>Repository:</b> one ECR repository holds the versions of one image. Each version is identified by a <b>tag</b> (such as a git commit ID) and a <b>digest</b> (a unique hash of the content).","<b>Settings to know:</b>","<b>Tag immutability:</b> when on, a tag can never be overwritten with a different image."]}]},{"title":"ECR (Elastic Container Registry)","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<b>Scan on push:</b> checks images for known security problems (<b>vulnerabilities</b>).","<b>Lifecycle policies:</b> delete old images automatically.","<b>Logging in and pushing:</b>"]},{"type":"pre","text":"aws ecr get-login-password --region eu-west-1 | \\\n  docker login --username AWS --password-stdin <account-id>.dkr.ecr.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com\ndocker tag memos <account-id>.dkr.ecr.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/memos:<git-sha>\ndocker push <account-id>.dkr.ecr.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/memos:<git-sha>"},{"type":"p","html":"The login lasts 12 hours."}]},{"title":"ECR (Elastic Container Registry)","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> the repository <code>memos</code> has <b>immutable tags</b>, <b>scan on push</b>, and <code>force_delete = true</code>. Without <code>force_delete</code>, <code>terraform destroy</code> failed with <code>RepositoryNotEmptyException</code> while images were inside. Images are tagged with the <b>full 40-character git commit ID</b>."}]},{"title":"ECS (Elastic Container Service): the words","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Word</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>Cluster</b>","A logical group that tasks and services run in"],["<b>Task definition</b>","The instructions for running containers: image, CPU, memory, ports, environment variables, secrets, log settings, IAM roles. Every change creates a new numbered <b>revision</b>"],["<b>Task</b>","One running copy of a task definition"],["<b>Service</b>","Keeps a chosen number of tasks running (<b>desired count</b>), replaces tasks that stop, registers them with a load balancer target group, and handles <b>deployments</b>"]]}]},{"title":"ECS (Elastic Container Service): the words","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Word</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>Launch type /</b> <b>capacity provider</b>","Fargate (no servers to manage) or EC2"]]}]},{"title":"The two IAM roles of an ECS task","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Role</b>","<b>Used by</b>","<b>Typical permissions</b>"],["<b>Task execution</b> <b>role</b>","<b>ECS itself</b>, to <b>start</b> the task","Pull the image from ECR, write logs to CloudWatch, read secrets to inject them"],["<b>Task role</b>","<b>Your application code</b> inside the container","Whatever AWS calls your app makes (e.g. read an S3 bucket)"]]},{"type":"note","kind":"recall","html":"⚡ The execution role is what ECS needs to start the container. The task role is what your code inside the container may do."}]},{"title":"Fargate networking ( awsvpc mode)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Each Fargate task gets its <b>own network interface</b> and <b>private IP address</b> in your subnet, with its own security group.","That is why load balancer target groups for Fargate use <b>target type</b> <code>ip</code>.","Tasks in <b>private subnets</b> need a <b>NAT gateway</b> or <b>VPC endpoints</b> to reach ECR and CloudWatch."]}]},{"title":"How an ECS deployment works (rolling update)","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["A new task definition revision is registered with the new image.","The service starts new tasks from it.","Each new task registers with the target group and must pass health checks.","When the new tasks are healthy, the old tasks are <b>deregistered</b> (draining finishes in-progress requests) and stopped."]}]},{"title":"How an ECS deployment works (rolling update)","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Two settings control this: <code>minimumHealthyPercent</code> and <code>maximumPercent</code>. With the defaults of 100% and 200%, ECS can briefly run double the tasks so capacity never drops."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Rollback</b> means deploying the previous image again. <b>Blue/green deployment</b> (two target groups and AWS CodeDeploy switching traffic) is an alternative that allows instant switching back."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The Memos task definition and service</b> (Terraform, shortened):"}]},{"title":"How an ECS deployment works (rolling update)","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_ecs_task_definition\" \"this\" {\n  family                   = \"memos-task\"\n  requires_compatibilities = [\"FARGATE\"]\n  network_mode             = \"awsvpc\"                 # required for Fargate\n  cpu                      = 256                      # 0.25 vCPU\n  memory                   = 512                      # MB\n  execution_role_arn       = var.execution_role_arn\n  task_role_arn            = var.task_role_arn\n  container_definitions = jsonencode([{\n    name         = \"memos\"\n    image        = var.image_url                      # .../memos:<git-sha>\n    essential    = true                               # if this container stops, the task stops\n    portMappings = [{ containerPort = 8081, hostPort = 8081, protocol = \"tcp\" }]\n    logConfiguration = {\n      logDriver = \"awslogs\"                           # send output to CloudWatch Logs\n      options = {\n        \"awslogs-group\"         = \"/ecs/memos\"\n        \"awslogs-region\"        = \"eu-west-1\"\n        \"awslogs-stream-prefix\" = \"ecs\"\n      }\n    }\n  }])\n}"}]},{"title":"How an ECS deployment works (rolling update)","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"resource \"aws_ecs_service\" \"this\" {\n  name            = \"memos-service\"\n  cluster         = aws_ecs_cluster.this.id\n  task_definition = aws_ecs_task_definition.this.arn\n  desired_count   = 1\n  launch_type     = \"FARGATE\"\n  network_configuration {\n    subnets          = var.private_subnet_ids        # private subnets only\n    security_groups  = [var.ecs_security_group_id]\n    assign_public_ip = false                         # not reachable directly from the internet\n  }\n  load_balancer {\n    target_group_arn = var.target_group_arn\n    container_name   = \"memos\"\n    container_port   = 8081\n  }\n}"}]},{"title":"Debugging ECS","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Service events</b> (in the Console, or <code>aws ecs describe-services ... --query 'services[0].events[:5]'</code>) show what the service is doing and why.","<b>Stopped reason:</b> <code>aws ecs describe-tasks ... --query 'tasks[0].stoppedReason'</code>.","<b>Logs:</b> <code>aws logs tail /ecs/memos --follow</code>. Fargate has <b>no SSH</b>, so logs are how you see inside. <b>ECS Exec</b> can open a shell in a running task if you turn it on.","<b>Force a redeploy</b> of the same task definition: <code>aws ecs update-service --cluster &lt;c&gt; --service &lt;s&gt; --</code> <code>force-new-deployment</code>."]}]},{"title":"What went wrong in Memos","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Problem</b>","<b>Cause</b>","<b>Fix</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>CannotPullContainerError</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","The ECR repository was still empty","Push an image before the first deploy"],["Targets unhealthy, <code>Target.Timeout</code>","The settings said port 5230; the app actually listened on 8081","Found the real port in CloudWatch Logs; used 8081 in the app, task definition, target group and security group"]]}]},{"title":"What went wrong in Memos","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>listen tcp :80: bind: permission</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["A non-root container tried to","Use a port above 1023"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>denied</code>"},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","use port 80",""],["New image pushed with the same tag, nothing changed","ECS only redeploys when the task definition changes","Tag with the commit ID, so every build has a new tag"]]}]},{"title":"EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service), briefly","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Kubernetes</b> is an open-source system for running containers across many machines. It is the industry standard and works on every cloud. It has its own words: pods, deployments, services, ingress.","<b>EKS</b> runs the Kubernetes control plane for you, for $0.10 per hour per cluster. You run the worker nodes (EC2), or use Fargate or EKS Auto Mode.","<b>ECS vs EKS:</b> ECS is simpler and works only on AWS. EKS is more complex, but portable, with a large ecosystem of tools. My separate Kubernetes for Beginners guide covers it in full."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-21-databases-on-aws","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Containers and databases","num":"21","title":"Databases on AWS","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Why use a managed database","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A database stores structured data so programs can search and update it quickly. You can install a database on EC2 yourself, but then you must handle installation, updates, backups, failover and scaling. <b>Managed databases</b> do this for you."}]},{"title":"Relational vs non-relational","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>Relational (SQL)</b>","<b>Non-relational (NoSQL)</b>"],["Data shape","<b>Tables</b> with rows and columns and a fixed <b>schema</b> (structure), linked by <b>keys</b>","Flexible: key-value items, documents, graphs"],["Query language","<b>SQL</b>","Each database’s own API"],["Strengths","Complex queries, joins, transactions","Huge scale, predictable speed, flexible data"],["AWS services","<b>RDS</b>, <b>Aurora</b>","<b>DynamoDB</b>, DocumentDB, Neptune"]]}]},{"title":"RDS (Relational Database Service)","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Runs managed <b>MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Oracle, SQL Server</b> (and Db2).","You choose the <b>instance class</b> (size) and <b>storage</b>; AWS handles installation, patching (in a <b>maintenance</b> <b>window</b> you choose) and backups.","<b>Automated backups:</b> daily snapshots plus transaction logs. You can restore to <b>any point in time</b> within the retention period (up to 35 days). <b>Manual snapshots</b> are kept until you delete them."]}]},{"title":"RDS (Relational Database Service)","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Multi-AZ:</b> keeps a <b>standby copy</b> in another AZ, updated <b>synchronously</b> (every write is saved in both places before it is confirmed). If the main database fails, RDS <b>fails over</b> to the standby automatically, usually within one to two minutes, using the same DNS name. ⚡ <b>Multi-AZ is for availability, not for extra read capacity.</b>","<b>Read replicas:</b> copies updated <b>asynchronously</b> (with a short delay) that serve <b>read</b> queries, to spread load. They can be in other Regions and can be <b>promoted</b> to a standalone database. ⚡ <b>Read replicas are for read</b> <b>scaling.</b>"]}]},{"title":"RDS (Relational Database Service)","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["<b>Security:</b>","put the database in <b>private (isolated) subnets</b> (a <b>DB subnet group</b> lists them);","its security group allows the database port <b>only from the app’s security group</b>;","turn on <b>encryption at rest</b> (chosen at creation);","store the password in <b>Secrets Manager</b>, with automatic rotation.","<b>RDS Proxy</b> pools database connections, which helps applications (especially Lambda) that open many connections."]}]},{"title":"Aurora","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["AWS’s own database engine, compatible with <b>MySQL</b> and <b>PostgreSQL</b>.","Its storage is spread across <b>3 AZs with 6 copies</b> and grows automatically.","Up to <b>15 read replicas</b> with very short delay, and fast failover.","<b>Aurora Serverless v2</b> scales capacity up and down automatically with demand."]}]},{"title":"DynamoDB","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>serverless NoSQL key-value and document database</b>. There are no servers or instances to choose.","Each <b>table</b> needs a <b>partition key</b>. A partition key with an optional <b>sort key</b> together form the <b>primary key</b>, which identifies each <b>item</b>.","Consistent, single-digit-millisecond response times at almost any size.","<b>Capacity modes:</b>","<b>on-demand</b> (pay per request, no planning);","<b>provisioned</b> (set read and write capacity, optionally with auto scaling)."]}]},{"title":"DynamoDB","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":7,"items":["Features:","<b>DynamoDB Streams</b> (a record of changes that can trigger Lambda),","<b>global tables</b> (copies in several Regions),","<b>point-in-time recovery</b>,","<b>TTL</b> (automatic deletion of expired items).","<b>DAX</b> is an in-memory cache for DynamoDB.","It also stores <b>Terraform locks</b> in older setups (Chapter 26)."]}]},{"title":"ElastiCache","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Managed <b>Redis</b>/<b>Valkey</b> and <b>Memcached</b>: <b>in-memory</b> data stores. They keep data in RAM for very fast access.","Used as a <b>cache</b> in front of a database (to store results of slow queries), and for user <b>sessions</b> and leaderboards."]}]},{"title":"Choosing a database","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>You need</b>","<b>Use</b>"],["A normal relational database for an application","<b>RDS (PostgreSQL/MySQL)</b> or <b>Aurora</b>"],["Massive scale, simple lookups by key, serverless","<b>DynamoDB</b>"],["Very fast temporary data, caching","<b>ElastiCache</b>"],["Analytics over large amounts of data","<b>Redshift</b> (data warehouse) or <b>Athena</b> (SQL queries directly on files in S3)"]]}]},{"title":"Choosing a database","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> Memos uses <b>SQLite</b>, a database stored in a single file, inside the container. It is simple, but the data is lost on every deploy, and two copies of the app cannot share it. Memos also supports PostgreSQL and MySQL. Moving it to <b>RDS PostgreSQL in Multi-AZ</b> in private subnets, with the password in Secrets Manager, would make the data safe and allow more than one task. That would make Memos a full <b>three-tier</b> application (Chapter 28)."}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-22-messaging-and-integration-sqs-sns-eventbridge-api","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Connecting services together","num":"22","title":"Messaging and integration (SQS, SNS, EventBridge, API Gateway)","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Why these services exist","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["When one part of a system calls another directly and waits for the answer, the two are <b>tightly coupled</b>. If the second part is slow or down, the first part fails too.","Messaging services let parts of a system communicate <b>without waiting</b> for each other. This is called <b>decoupling</b>, or <b>asynchronous</b> communication. Each part can fail, restart or scale on its own."]}]},{"title":"SQS (Simple Queue Service)","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> a <b>queue</b>, a managed list of <b>messages</b> waiting to be processed.","A <b>producer</b> sends messages.","A <b>consumer</b> asks for messages (this is called <b>polling</b>), processes them, then <b>deletes</b> them.","<b>How it works:</b>"]}]},{"title":"SQS (Simple Queue Service)","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["The producer sends a message (up to <b>1 MiB</b>).","The message is stored redundantly across several AZs. By default it is kept for <b>4 days</b> (you can set 1 minute to <b>14 days</b>).","A consumer receives the message. It becomes <b>invisible</b> to other consumers for the <b>visibility timeout</b> (default 30 seconds).","If the consumer deletes it within that time, it is gone. If not (for example, the consumer crashed), the message becomes visible again and another consumer can try."]}]},{"title":"SQS (Simple Queue Service)","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["After a set number of failed attempts, it can be moved to a <b>dead-letter queue (DLQ)</b> for investigation."]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Queue types:</b>"]},{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Type</b>","<b>Order</b>","<b>Delivery</b>"],["<b>Standard</b>","Best effort (usually in order, not guaranteed)","<b>At least once</b> (occasionally a message arrives twice)"],["<b>FIFO</b> (“first in, first out”)","<b>Strict order</b>","<b>Exactly once</b>"]]}]},{"title":"SQS (Simple Queue Service)","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"With a Standard queue, your code must be able to handle the same message twice. - <b>Long polling</b> (waiting up to 20 seconds for messages) reduces empty responses and cost. - <b>Use for:</b> spreading work across many workers, absorbing sudden bursts of traffic, and making sure work is not lost if a worker fails."}]},{"title":"SNS (Simple Notification Service)","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> a <b>publish/subscribe</b> (pub/sub) service.","A <b>publisher</b> sends a message to a <b>topic</b>.","SNS immediately <b>pushes</b> a copy to <b>every subscriber</b>: email, SMS, HTTP endpoints, SQS queues, Lambda functions, mobile push.","<b>Fan-out pattern:</b> SNS topic → several SQS queues. One event is processed by several independent systems, each at its own pace.","<b>Message filtering:</b> subscribers can receive only the messages that match a filter."]}]},{"title":"SNS (Simple Notification Service)","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["<b>Use for:</b> alerts (CloudWatch alarms send to SNS), notifications, and fan-out."]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>SQS vs SNS</b> 🧠:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["SQS is a <b>queue</b>: consumers <b>pull</b>, and each message is processed by <b>one</b> consumer.","SNS is <b>pub/sub</b>: SNS <b>pushes</b>, and <b>every</b> subscriber gets a copy."]}]},{"title":"EventBridge","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> an <b>event bus</b>: a service that receives <b>events</b> (records that something happened) and sends them to targets according to <b>rules</b>.","Sources include almost every AWS service (for example, “an EC2 instance stopped”, “an object was uploaded to S3”), your own applications, and other companies’ software.","<b>Rules</b> match events by their content and send them to targets: Lambda, SQS, SNS, Step Functions, ECS tasks and more.","<b>EventBridge Scheduler</b> runs targets on a schedule, written as <code>rate(1 hour)</code> or a <b>cron</b> expression."]}]},{"title":"EventBridge","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":5,"items":["<b>Use for:</b> reacting to changes across services, and scheduled jobs."]}]},{"title":"API Gateway","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> a managed service for creating <b>APIs</b>: HTTP endpoints that programs call.","It receives requests, checks them (<b>authentication</b> with IAM, Cognito or JWT tokens; <b>throttling</b>, which is limiting the request rate),","sends them to a <b>backend</b> (Lambda, an HTTP server or another AWS service),","and returns the response.","<b>Types: HTTP API</b> (simpler and cheaper), <b>REST API</b> (more features), <b>WebSocket API</b> (two-way connections)."]}]},{"title":"API Gateway","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["A common <b>serverless</b> pattern: API Gateway → Lambda → DynamoDB."]}]},{"title":"Step Functions","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Step Functions</b> runs <b>workflows</b>, sequences of steps, defined as a <b>state machine</b> (a diagram of steps and the conditions between them). It handles retries, waiting, parallel branches and errors. It is used to coordinate several Lambda functions or services in a fixed order."}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-23-cloudwatch-monitoring-and-logs","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Monitoring, security and secrets","num":"23","title":"CloudWatch (monitoring and logs)","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"modules/ecs (log group, 7-day retention)"}],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>CloudWatch</b> collects and shows information about your resources and applications:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>metrics</b> (numbers over time),","<b>logs</b> (text records of events),","<b>alarms</b> (actions when a metric crosses a limit),","<b>dashboards</b> (pages of graphs)."]}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"You cannot fix a problem you cannot see. Without monitoring, you find out that something is wrong when users complain. With it, you see problems early, and you have the evidence to find the cause."}]},{"title":"Metrics","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["A <b>metric</b> is a measurement recorded over time, such as an EC2 instance’s <code>CPUUtilization</code> or a load balancer’s <code>HTTPCode_Target_5XX_Count</code>.","AWS services send many metrics <b>automatically</b>. EC2 sends basic metrics every <b>5 minutes</b>; <b>detailed</b> <b>monitoring</b> sends them every minute, for a cost."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <b>EC2 memory and disk-space usage are NOT included by default</b>, because AWS cannot see inside your operating system. Install the <b>CloudWatch agent</b> on the instance to send them."}]},{"title":"Metrics","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Each metric is identified by a <b>namespace</b> (e.g. <code>AWS/EC2</code>), a <b>name</b>, and <b>dimensions</b> (e.g. <code>InstanceId=i-</code> <code>0abc…</code>).","<b>Statistics:</b> average, sum, minimum, maximum, sample count, and <b>percentiles</b>. For example, <code>p99</code> is the value below which 99% of measurements fall, which is useful for response times."]}]},{"title":"Logs","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>CloudWatch Logs</b> stores text logs:","a <b>log group</b> holds the logs of one application (e.g. <code>/ecs/memos</code>);","a <b>log stream</b> is the sequence of logs from one source (e.g. one task);","each log group has a <b>retention</b> period (how long logs are kept), from 1 day to forever.","<b>Logs Insights</b> lets you search and analyse logs with a query language. Example:"]}]},{"title":"Logs","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"fields @timestamp, @message\n| filter @message like /error/\n| sort @timestamp desc\n| limit 20"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Metric filters</b> turn log patterns into metrics, such as counting lines that contain “ERROR”.","<b>Subscription filters</b> stream logs to Lambda, Kinesis or OpenSearch."]}]},{"title":"Alarms","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["An <b>alarm</b> watches one metric (or a calculation using several) and has three states:","<b>OK</b>,","<b>ALARM</b>,","<b>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</b> (not enough data yet).","You set a <b>threshold</b> and how many periods must break it. Example: “CPU above 80% for 3 periods of 5 minutes”.","<b>Actions</b> when the state changes: send to an <b>SNS topic</b> (email or chat), scale an Auto Scaling Group, or stop, reboot or recover an EC2 instance."]}]},{"title":"Alarms","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":7,"items":["<b>Composite alarms</b> combine several alarms with AND/OR, to reduce noise."]}]},{"title":"Other monitoring tools","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Tool</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["<b>Dashboards</b>","Pages of graphs you design, for one view of a system"],["<b>Container Insights</b>","Detailed metrics and logs for ECS and EKS"],["<b>Application Signals</b>","Ready-made monitoring of an application’s requests, errors and response times"],["<b>AWS X-Ray</b>","<b>Tracing</b>: follows one request through every service it touches, to find which part is slow or failing"],["<b>Synthetics canaries</b>","Scripts that test your website on a schedule, as a real user would"]]}]},{"title":"Other monitoring tools","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Monitoring vs observability:</b> monitoring tells you <b>that</b> something is wrong (a known measurement crossed a limit). <b>Observability</b> is having enough data (<b>metrics, logs and traces</b>, called the “three pillars”) to work out <b>why</b>, including for problems you did not predict."},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b>"}]},{"title":"Other monitoring tools","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["The task definition sends container output to the log group <code>/ecs/memos</code>, kept for <b>7 days</b>.","Fargate has no SSH, so these logs are the only way to see inside the container. That is how I found the app was really listening on <b>8081</b>: <code>aws logs tail /ecs/memos --follow --region eu-west-1</code>.","Planned improvements: CloudWatch alarms on the load balancer’s 5xx errors, unhealthy targets and task CPU, sent to SNS."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-24-cloudtrail-and-aws-security-services","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Monitoring, security and secrets","num":"24","title":"CloudTrail and AWS security services","key":true,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"CloudTrail","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> a record of <b>every API call</b> made in your account: <b>who</b> made it (identity), <b>what</b> (action), <b>when</b>, <b>from where</b> (IP address), and whether it <b>succeeded</b>. Every Console click, CLI command and Terraform change is an API call, so all of them are recorded.","<b>Event history</b> shows the last <b>90 days</b> of management events, free, by default.","A <b>trail</b> saves events permanently to an <b>S3 bucket</b> (and optionally CloudWatch Logs). Use an <b>organisation</b> <b>trail</b> for every account, turn on <b>log file validation</b> (detects tampering), and protect the bucket."]}]},{"title":"CloudTrail","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["<b>Management events:</b> changes to resources (create, delete, change settings). <b>Data events:</b> high-volume actions on data, such as S3 object reads or Lambda invocations. These cost extra and are off by default.","<b>CloudWatch vs CloudTrail</b> ⚡: CloudWatch shows <b>how resources are performing</b> (metrics and logs). CloudTrail shows <b>who did what</b> in the account (an audit record)."]}]},{"title":"The security services you should recognise","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Service</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["<b>GuardDuty</b>","<b>Threat detection.</b> Analyses CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs and DNS logs with machine learning to find suspicious activity: stolen credentials, crypto-mining, contact with known bad IPs"],["<b>Inspector</b>","<b>Vulnerability scanning</b> of EC2 instances, ECR images and Lambda functions for known software vulnerabilities (<b>CVEs</b>) and network exposure"]]}]},{"title":"The security services you should recognise","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Service</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["<b>Security Hub</b>","Collects findings from GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie and others in one place, and checks your account against security standards (e.g. the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices)"],["<b>AWS Config</b>","Records the <b>configuration</b> of your resources over time, and checks them against <b>rules</b> (e.g. “all EBS volumes must be encrypted”). Shows who changed what and when"],["<b>Macie</b>","Finds <b>sensitive data</b> (such as personal information) stored in S3"]]}]},{"title":"The security services you should recognise","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Service</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["<b>IAM Access</b> <b>Analyzer</b>","Finds resources shared outside your account, and unused permissions"],["<b>AWS WAF</b>","<b>Web application firewall</b>. Filters HTTP requests to CloudFront, ALB or API Gateway using rules: block known attack patterns (SQL injection, cross-site scripting), limit the request rate per IP, block countries"]]}]},{"title":"The security services you should recognise","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Service</b>","<b>What it does</b>"],["<b>AWS Shield</b>","<b>DDoS protection.</b> A DDoS attack (distributed denial of service) floods a system with traffic to make it unavailable. <b>Shield Standard</b> is free and automatic. <b>Shield Advanced</b> is paid, with extra protection, cost protection and a response team"],["<b>Firewall</b> <b>Manager</b>","Manages WAF and security group rules across many accounts"],["<b>Detective</b>","Helps investigate security findings by linking related events"]]}]},{"title":"Scenario: which services protect against a DDoS attack?","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Shield</b> (Standard is always on; Advanced for more),","<b>WAF</b> (rate limits and filtering),","<b>CloudFront</b> (absorbs traffic at edge locations),","<b>Route 53</b> (spread across many edge locations),","an <b>ALB with Auto Scaling</b> (handles more load)."]}]},{"title":"Scenario: AWS access keys were found in a public GitHub repository","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Do these in order:"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Deactivate and delete</b> the keys immediately, in IAM.","<b>Check CloudTrail</b> for what the keys were used for. Look for new users, roles or instances, especially in Regions you do not use.","<b>Remove anything the attacker created</b>, and check your bill.","<b>Remove the keys from Git history.</b> Deleting the file in a new commit is not enough, because the key is still in old commits. Use a tool such as <code>git filter-repo</code>."]}]},{"title":"Scenario: AWS access keys were found in a public GitHub repository","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["<b>Stop using long-lived keys:</b> use <b>OIDC</b> roles for pipelines (Chapter 27) and IAM Identity Center for people.","Turn on <b>GuardDuty</b> if it is not already on."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-25-encryption-and-secrets-kms-secrets-manager-parame","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Monitoring, security and secrets","num":"25","title":"Encryption and secrets (KMS, Secrets Manager, Parameter Store)","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Encryption basics","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Encryption</b> turns readable data into unreadable data using a <b>key</b>. Only someone with the right key can turn it back (<b>decrypt</b>).","<b>Encryption at rest:</b> data stored on disk is encrypted.","<b>Encryption in transit:</b> data travelling over a network is encrypted (TLS/HTTPS).","<b>Symmetric encryption</b> uses <b>one</b> key to encrypt and decrypt. <b>Asymmetric encryption</b> uses a <b>key pair</b>: a <b>public key</b> to encrypt (or to check signatures), and a <b>private key</b> to decrypt (or to sign)."]}]},{"title":"KMS (Key Management Service)","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>What it is:</b> creates and stores <b>encryption keys</b>, and controls who can use them. The keys never leave KMS unencrypted.","<b>Key types:</b>","<b>AWS owned keys</b> (used by AWS for you; you never see them),","<b>AWS managed keys</b> (e.g. <code>aws/s3</code>, created when a service needs one),","<b>customer managed keys</b> (you create them, write their <b>key policy</b>, turn on <b>automatic rotation</b>, and can disable them)."]}]},{"title":"KMS (Key Management Service)","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["<b>Envelope encryption:</b> KMS gives a service a <b>data key</b> to encrypt the actual data. The data key itself is then encrypted with the KMS key and stored next to the data. This is how S3, EBS and RDS use KMS.","Every use of a key is recorded in <b>CloudTrail</b>.","<b>CloudHSM</b> gives you dedicated hardware security modules, for strict compliance needs."]}]},{"title":"Secrets Manager vs Parameter Store","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"A <b>secret</b> is sensitive data such as a database password, an API key or a token. ⚡ <b>Never put secrets in your</b> <b>code, your Docker image or your Git repository.</b>"}]},{"title":"Secrets Manager vs Parameter Store","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>Secrets Manager</b>","<b>SSM Parameter Store</b>"],["Made for","Secrets (passwords, API keys, credentials)","Configuration values <b>and</b> secrets"],["Automatic rotation","<b>Yes</b>, built in (e.g. changes RDS passwords automatically)","No (you build it yourself)"],["Encryption","Always, with KMS","Optional: <code>SecureString</code> parameters use KMS"],["Cost","About $0.40 per secret per month + API calls","<b>Standard tier is free</b>; Advanced tier is paid"],["Other features","Cross-Region replication of secrets","Hierarchies such as <code>/memos/prod/db_host</code>, version history"]]}]},{"title":"How secrets reach an application","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>ECS:</b> in the task definition’s <code>secrets</code> section, give the environment variable name and the ARN of the secret or parameter. When the task starts, the <b>task execution role</b> reads the value and passes it to the container as an environment variable. The value is never stored in the task definition."]},{"type":"pre","text":"\"secrets\": [\n  { \"name\": \"MEMOS_DSN\", \"valueFrom\": \"arn:aws:secretsmanager:eu-west-1:<account>:secret:memos/db-\nAbCdEf\" }\n]"}]},{"title":"How secrets reach an application","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Lambda:</b> read the secret at start-up using the SDK. The function’s execution role must allow it.","<b>EC2:</b> the instance’s role allows reading the secret, and the app reads it with the SDK or CLI.","<b>Terraform:</b> read with a <code>data</code> source. Remember that values end up in the <b>state file</b>, so protect the state."]}]},{"title":"How secrets reach an application","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>In Memos:</b> the app has no secrets today. If it moved to RDS (Chapter 21), the database connection string would go in <b>Secrets Manager</b>, and the ECS execution role would be given permission to read that one secret."}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-26-infrastructure-as-code-terraform-and-cloudformati","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Building and deploying with code","num":"26","title":"Infrastructure as Code (Terraform and CloudFormation)","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/provider.tf","label":"infra/provider.tf (S3 backend)"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/state.tf","label":"bootstrap/state.tf"}],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Infrastructure as Code (IaC)</b> means writing your infrastructure (networks, servers, databases, permissions) in <b>text files</b>. A tool reads the files and creates or changes the real resources to match."}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Clicking in the Console has four problems:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["you cannot repeat it exactly;","nobody can review it before it happens;","there is no history of why things changed;","building a second, identical environment (for testing) takes as long as the first."]},{"type":"p","html":"With IaC:"}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["the files live in <b>Git</b> (history, review, rollback);","the same files build <b>identical environments</b> (development, staging, production);","the tool shows a <b>plan</b> of changes before making them."]}]},{"title":"Terraform vs CloudFormation","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["","<b>Terraform</b>","<b>AWS CloudFormation</b>"],["Made by","HashiCorp","AWS"],["Language","HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language)","YAML or JSON (and the AWS CDK, in programming languages)"],["Clouds","AWS, Azure, Google Cloud and hundreds more, through <b>providers</b>","AWS only"],["Where state is kept","A <b>state file</b> you store (e.g. in S3)","Managed by AWS in the <b>stack</b>"],["Preview","","<b>Change sets</b>"]]}]},{"title":"Terraform vs CloudFormation","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform plan</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["changes"]]},{"type":"p","html":"Both are <b>declarative</b>: you describe the end result you want, not the steps."}]},{"title":"Terraform: the words","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Word</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>Provider</b>","A plugin that talks to one platform’s API (e.g. <code>hashicorp/aws</code>)"],["<b>Resource</b>","Something Terraform creates and manages, e.g. <code>aws_vpc</code>"],["<b>Data source</b>","Something Terraform only <b>reads</b>, e.g. an existing Route 53 zone"],["<b>Variable</b>","An input value (<code>variables.tf</code>), so the same code works with different values"],["<b>Output</b>","A value exported from a module (e.g. the VPC ID) for other modules or for you"],["<b>Local</b>","A named value calculated once and reused inside a module"]]}]},{"title":"Terraform: the words","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Word</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>Module</b>","A folder of Terraform files used as one reusable unit. The top folder is the <b>root module</b>"],["<b>State</b>","A file mapping your code to the real resource IDs in AWS"],["<b>Backend</b>","Where the state file is stored, e.g. an S3 bucket"]]}]},{"title":"The Terraform workflow","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Command</b>","<b>What it does</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform init</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Downloads providers and modules, connects to the backend. Run it first, and again after adding modules or providers"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform fmt</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Formats the files neatly. <code>fmt -check</code> fails if anything needs formatting"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform</code>"]}]},{"title":"The Terraform workflow","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Checks the syntax and references"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>validate</code>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform plan</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Compares your code, the state and the real resources, and shows what would be <b>created, changed</b> <b>or destroyed</b>. Changes nothing. <code>-out=tfplan</code> saves the plan"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform</code>"]}]},{"title":"The Terraform workflow","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Makes the changes. <code>apply tfplan</code> applies exactly the saved plan"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>apply</code>"},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Deletes everything in the state"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>terraform</code>"]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>destroy</code>"}]},{"title":"State, in detail","part":"1/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Code</b> = what you <b>want</b>. <b>State</b> = what Terraform <b>knows</b> it created. <b>Real infrastructure</b> = what <b>actually</b> <b>exists</b>. <code>plan</code> compares all three."]},{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ The state file can contain <b>secrets in plain text</b> (passwords, keys), so it must be protected."}]},{"title":"State, in detail","part":"2/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Keep state <b>remote</b> (shared by everyone and by the pipeline) and <b>locked</b> (so two people cannot change it at the same time and corrupt it).","<b>Drift</b> is when real infrastructure is changed outside Terraform (for example, someone edits a security group in the Console). The next <code>plan</code> shows it. <code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code> shows drift without proposing other changes.","Bring an existing resource under Terraform with an <code>import</code> block or <code>terraform import</code>."]}]},{"title":"State, in detail","part":"3/6","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The Memos backend and the bootstrap stack:</b>"}]},{"title":"State, in detail","part":"4/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"terraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.10.0, < 2.0.0\"\n  required_providers {\n    aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\", version = \"~> 5.0\" }\n  }\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket       = \"memos-terraform-state-enamul\"\n    key          = \"prod/terraform.tfstate\"     # the path of the state file inside the bucket\n    region       = \"eu-west-1\"\n    encrypt      = true\n    use_lockfile = true                         # S3 native locking: no DynamoDB table needed\n  }\n}"}]},{"title":"State, in detail","part":"5/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Locking:</b> older setups locked state with a <b>DynamoDB table</b>. Since <b>Terraform 1.10</b>, <code>use_lockfile = true</code> makes S3 itself hold a lock file (<code>.tflock</code>). DynamoDB locking is now deprecated.","<b>The chicken-and-egg problem:</b> Terraform cannot create the bucket that stores its own state in the same configuration, because it needs the bucket first. So Memos has a separate small <b>bootstrap</b> stack, applied once. It creates:","the state bucket (versioning, encryption, public access blocked, HTTPS-only policy, <code>prevent_destroy</code>),"]}]},{"title":"State, in detail","part":"6/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":4,"items":["and the GitHub OIDC role (Chapter 27)."]}]},{"title":"Modules and how data flows between them","part":"1/3","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 Memos has <b>seven modules</b>: vpc, security, acm, alb, ecr, iam and ecs. The root <code>main.tf</code> connects them by passing one module’s <b>outputs</b> into another’s <b>inputs</b>:"},{"type":"pre","text":"module \"alb\" {\n  source          = \"./modules/alb\"\n  vpc_id          = module.vpc.vpc_id                       # output of vpc → input of alb\n  public_subnets  = module.vpc.public_subnets\n  security_group  = module.security.alb_security_group_id\n  certificate_arn = module.acm.certificate_arn\n}"}]},{"title":"Modules and how data flows between them","part":"2/3","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"module \"ecs\" {\n  source             = \"./modules/ecs\"\n  private_subnet_ids = module.vpc.private_subnets\n  image_url          = var.image_url != \"\" ? var.image_url : module.ecr.repository_url\n  execution_role_arn = module.iam.execution_role_arn\n  task_role_arn      = module.iam.task_role_arn\n  target_group_arn   = module.alb.target_group_arn\n}"}]},{"title":"Modules and how data flows between them","part":"3/3","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Terraform works out the <b>order</b> of creation from these references (a <b>dependency graph</b>), not from the order in the file.","The <code>image_url</code> line uses a <b>conditional expression</b>: if the pipeline passed an image URL, use it; otherwise use the repository URL."]}]},{"title":"Variable precedence (lowest to highest)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["The <code>default</code> in the variable block.","An environment variable <code>TF_VAR_name</code>.","<code>terraform.tfvars</code>.","<code>*.auto.tfvars</code> files.","<code>-var</code> or <code>-var-file</code> on the command line (always wins)."]}]},{"title":"count vs for_each","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>count</code> makes numbered copies (my NAT gateways: <code>[0]</code> and <code>[1]</code>).","<code>for_each</code> makes copies keyed by name (my ACM validation records, keyed by domain name). With <code>for_each</code>, removing one item does not renumber and recreate the others."]}]},{"title":"Real Terraform errors from Memos","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Error</b>","<b>Cause</b>","<b>Fix</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Missing required argument</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["A module needs an input the root did not pass","Pass the other module’s output"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Unsupported argument</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","Passed an input the module does not declare","Remove it, or declare it in the module"],["<b>Error</b>","<b>Cause</b>","<b>Fix</b>"]]}]},{"title":"Real Terraform errors from Memos","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>Reference to undeclared input</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Used <code>var.x</code> without declaring it","Add it to <code>variables.tf</code>"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>variable</code>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>aws_lb_target_group.main is not</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Wrong resource name","Use the name in the code (<code>this</code>)"]]},{"type":"p","html":"<code>declared</code>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>UnsupportedCertificate</code>"]}]},{"title":"Real Terraform errors from Memos","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["The listener was created before"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>aws_acm_certificate_validation</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","the certificate was valid"],["<code>RepositoryNotEmptyException</code> on","ECR still had images"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>force_delete = true</code>"]}]},{"title":"Real Terraform errors from Memos","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["destroy","",""],["destroy <code>ResourceInUse</code> for a target group on","A listener still used it","Delete the listener first"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>No configuration files</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["Ran it in the wrong folder"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>cd infra</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<code>fmt -check</code> exits with code 3 in CI","Files not formatted","committing <code>terraform fmt -recursive</code> before"]]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-27-ci-cd-with-github-actions-and-oidc","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Building and deploying with code","num":"27","title":"CI/CD with GitHub Actions and OIDC","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/build.yml","label":"build.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":"deploy.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/destroy.yml","label":"destroy.yml"}],"cards":[{"title":"What it is","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>CI (Continuous Integration):</b> every change pushed to the repository is automatically <b>built and tested</b>.","<b>Continuous Delivery:</b> every change that passes is <b>ready</b> to release, and a person approves the release.","<b>Continuous Deployment:</b> every change that passes is <b>released automatically</b>. 🔧 Memos does continuous deployment.","<b>GitHub Actions</b> runs <b>workflows</b> written in YAML in <code>.github/workflows/</code>:","<b>events</b> (<code>on:</code>) start them;","<b>jobs</b> run on <b>runners</b> (temporary virtual machines);"]}]},{"title":"What it is","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":7,"items":["each job has <b>steps</b> (shell commands, or reusable <b>actions</b>)."]}]},{"title":"Why it exists","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Deploying by hand from a laptop:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["is slow,","depends on one person’s computer and credentials,","skips checks,","and is not recorded."]},{"type":"p","html":"A pipeline runs the same checks every time, keeps a log, and uses no personal credentials."}]},{"title":"OIDC: logging in to AWS without stored keys","part":"1/6","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>OIDC</b> (OpenID Connect) lets GitHub prove to AWS which repository and branch a workflow run belongs to, so AWS can give it <b>temporary credentials</b>. No AWS access keys are stored in GitHub."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>The steps:</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>One-time setup in AWS:</b>"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["create an <b>OIDC identity provider</b> for <code>token.actions.githubusercontent.com</code>;","create an <b>IAM role</b> whose <b>trust policy</b> allows tokens from that provider, for your repository only."]}]},{"title":"OIDC: logging in to AWS without stored keys","part":"2/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":2,"items":["The workflow has <code>permissions: id-token: write</code>, which allows it to request a token.","GitHub creates a short-lived, signed <b>token</b> (a <b>JWT</b>: a signed piece of data). It says which repository, branch and workflow the run is for.","The <code>aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials</code> action sends the token to <b>AWS STS</b> (<code>AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code>).","AWS checks the token’s signature and the trust policy’s <b>conditions</b>.","AWS returns <b>temporary credentials</b> (valid for about an hour), and the next steps use them."]}]},{"title":"OIDC: logging in to AWS without stored keys","part":"3/6","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The Memos trust policy:</b>"}]},{"title":"OIDC: logging in to AWS without stored keys","part":"4/6","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"assume_role_policy = jsonencode({\n  Version = \"2012-10-17\"\n  Statement = [{\n    Effect    = \"Allow\"\n    Principal = { Federated = aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.github.arn }   # GitHub's OIDC provider\n    Action    = \"sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity\"\n    Condition = {\n      StringEquals = { \"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud\" = \"sts.amazonaws.com\" }\n      StringLike   = { \"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub\" = \"repo:EnamulRahman/memos-aws-\ninfra:*\" }\n    }\n  }]\n})"}]},{"title":"OIDC: logging in to AWS without stored keys","part":"5/6","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>aud</code> (audience) must be STS, meaning the token was made for AWS.","<code>sub</code> (subject) names the repository. After the second colon comes the branch, environment or pull request.","<b>What went wrong:</b> after I renamed the repository, the trust policy still had the <b>old name</b>, so every run failed at <code>AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code>. The fix was to update the name and use <code>StringLike</code> with <code>:*</code>."]}]},{"title":"OIDC: logging in to AWS without stored keys","part":"6/6","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ <b>Honest weakness:</b> <code>:*</code> allows <b>any</b> branch of the repository, and the role has <b>AdministratorAccess</b>. A stricter version limits <code>sub</code> to <code>repo:EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra:ref:refs/heads/main</code> and uses a narrower policy."}]},{"title":"The Memos pipeline","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Workflow</b>","<b>Starts when</b>","<b>Steps</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>build.yml</code>"]},{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["A push to <code>main</code> that changes the app or Docker files (<b>path filters</b>)","Checkout → <b>tfsec</b> security scan of the Terraform → OIDC login → ECR login → build the image → tag it with the <b>commit ID</b> → push (or reuse if that tag already exists)"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>deploy.yml</code>"]}]},{"title":"The Memos pipeline","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["<b>successfully</b>, or run by hand with an <code>image_tag</code> (for <b>rollback</b>) <code>build.yml</code> <b>finished</b>","Check the tag is a full 40-character commit ID and exists in ECR → <code>var=\"image_url=…\" -out=tfplan</code> → <code>apply tfplan</code>. Then a separate <b>health-check</b> job waits 60 s and checks <code>https://tm.myegh.com/healthz</code> returns <b>200</b> <code>terraform fmt -check</code> → <code>init</code> → <code>validate</code> → <code>plan -</code>"],["Run by hand only","OIDC login → <code>terraform init</code> → <code>terraform destroy</code>"]]}]},{"title":"The Memos pipeline","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>destroy.yml</code>"]},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Why build only if the tag is new:</b> ECR tags are <b>immutable</b>, so pushing the same tag twice fails. The step checks first, and builds only when the error is exactly <code>ImageNotFoundException</code>. Any other error stops the run."},{"type":"p","html":"<b>Why the</b> <code>if:</code> <b>check matters:</b> a <code>workflow_run</code> trigger fires when the build <b>completes</b>, whether it passed or failed. The deploy job checks <code>github.event.workflow_run.conclusion == 'success'</code>, so a failed build never deploys."}]},{"title":"The Memos pipeline","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"<b>Why the commit ID as the tag (not</b> <code>latest</code><b> ):</b>"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["every running image maps to exactly one commit;","rollback is exact;","it works with immutable tags;","ECS only deploys when the task definition changes. A new tag changes it; re-using <code>latest</code> does not."]}]},{"title":"Security scanning in pipelines","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Tool</b>","<b>Scans</b>"],["<b>tfsec / Trivy config / Checkov</b>","Infrastructure code (Terraform, Kubernetes files, Dockerfiles) for insecure settings"],["<b>Trivy / ECR scanning / Inspector</b>","Built images for known vulnerabilities"],["<b>git-secrets / trufflehog / Gitleaks</b>","Code for leaked secrets"]]}]},{"title":"Security scanning in pipelines","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"warn","html":"⚠️ Memos runs <b>tfsec</b> only. Image scanning with Trivy before pushing, and an approval step before production, are planned improvements, not done."}]},{"title":"Interview questions about pipelines","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>“The pipeline takes 20 minutes. What do you do?”</b>"]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Time each step to find the slowest one.","Cache dependencies and Docker layers.","Run independent jobs in parallel.","Use smaller base images and multi-stage builds.","Add path filters so unrelated changes do not trigger builds."]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>“How would you release a new feature safely?”</b>"]}]},{"title":"Interview questions about pipelines","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["Feature branch → pull request with checks and the Terraform plan.","Review, then merge.","Build, scan and deploy to <b>staging</b>, then test.","Promote the <b>same image</b> to production after approval.","Monitor, and roll back by commit ID if needed."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-28-high-availability-disaster-recovery-and-the-well-","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Designing good systems","num":"28","title":"High availability, disaster recovery and the Well-Architected Framework","key":true,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Words to know","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Word</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>High availability (HA)</b>","The system keeps working when one part fails, with at most a short interruption. Usually achieved by running in <b>2+ AZs</b>"],["<b>Fault tolerance</b>","The system keeps working with <b>no interruption at all</b> when parts fail. Stricter and more expensive than HA"],["<b>Scalability</b>","The system can handle more load by adding resources"],["<b>Elasticity</b>","Resources are added <b>and removed</b> automatically as load changes"]]}]},{"title":"Words to know","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Word</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>Single point of failure</b> <b>(SPOF)</b>","One part whose failure stops the whole system. Example: one NAT gateway, one database, one task"],["<b>SLA</b> (service level agreement)","A promised availability, e.g. 99.9%"],["<b>Disaster recovery (DR)</b>","How you recover after a large failure, such as a whole Region being unavailable"],["<b>RPO</b> (recovery point objective)","The most <b>data</b> you can afford to lose, measured in time. RPO = 1 hour means backups at least every hour"]]}]},{"title":"Words to know","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Word</b>","<b>Meaning</b>"],["<b>RTO</b> (recovery time objective)","The most <b>time</b> the system can be down before it is working again"]]},{"type":"p","html":"What uptime percentages mean per year:"}]},{"title":"Words to know","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Uptime</b>","<b>Allowed downtime per year</b>"],["99%","about 3.65 days"],["99.9%","about 8.8 hours"],["99.99%","about 53 minutes"],["99.999%","about 5 minutes"]]}]},{"title":"The four disaster recovery strategies (cheapest and slowest first)","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Strategy</b>","<b>What runs in the second Region</b>","<b>RTO / RPO</b>"],["<b>Backup and restore</b>","Nothing. Backups are copied there, and everything is rebuilt after a disaster (IaC makes this faster)","Hours"],["<b>Pilot light</b>","Only the core (e.g. the database replicating), everything else off","Tens of minutes"],["<b>Warm standby</b>","A complete but <b>smaller</b> copy, scaled up during a disaster","Minutes"],["<b>Multi-site</b> <b>active/active</b>","A <b>full</b> copy serving users all the time","Near zero"]]}]},{"title":"The AWS Well-Architected Framework: six pillars","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Operational excellence:</b> automate operations, make small and reversible changes, monitor everything, learn from failures.","<b>Security:</b> least privilege, protect data at rest and in transit, record everything (CloudTrail), automate security checks.","<b>Reliability:</b> recover from failures automatically, scale horizontally, test recovery, stop guessing capacity.","<b>Performance efficiency:</b> choose the right resource types and sizes, go global in minutes, use serverless where it fits."]}]},{"title":"The AWS Well-Architected Framework: six pillars","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["<b>Cost optimisation:</b> pay only for what you use, measure spending, remove idle resources, use the right pricing model.","<b>Sustainability:</b> reduce the energy and resources your workload uses."]},{"type":"p","html":"AWS provides a free <b>Well-Architected Tool</b> in the Console that asks questions about your workload and lists risks."}]},{"title":"Cost optimisation checklist","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Turn off or delete</b> what is not being used. Schedule development environments to stop at night.","<b>Right-size:</b> choose smaller instances when usage is low. <b>Compute Optimizer</b> suggests sizes.","Use <b>Savings Plans</b> for steady usage and <b>Spot</b> for interruptible work.","Use <b>S3 lifecycle rules</b> and the right <b>storage classes</b>.","Watch <b>always-on charges</b>: NAT gateways, load balancers, public IPv4 addresses, idle EBS volumes, old snapshots."]}]},{"title":"Cost optimisation checklist","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":6,"items":["Use <b>VPC endpoints</b> for heavy traffic to S3 and ECR.","<b>Tag</b> everything, and check <b>Cost Explorer</b> weekly."]}]},{"title":"The classic three-tier web application","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Many interview questions ask you to design this."},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>The network:</b> a VPC across <b>2+ AZs</b>, with public, private and isolated subnets in each AZ.","<b>Presentation tier (web tier):</b> an <b>ALB</b> in the public subnets with an <b>ACM</b> certificate and an HTTP→HTTPS redirect, optionally behind <b>CloudFront</b> and <b>WAF</b>.","<b>Application tier:</b> EC2 instances in an <b>Auto Scaling Group</b>, or <b>ECS/EKS tasks</b>, in <b>private</b> subnets. Their security group allows traffic <b>only from the ALB’s</b> security group."]}]},{"title":"The classic three-tier web application","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":4,"items":["<b>Data tier: RDS Multi-AZ</b> (or Aurora) in <b>isolated</b> subnets. Its security group allows the database port <b>only</b> <b>from the application tier’s</b> security group. Encrypted, with automated backups.","<b>Around it:</b>"]}]},{"title":"The classic three-tier web application","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>Route 53</b> (alias record),","<b>NAT gateways</b> (one per AZ) or <b>VPC endpoints</b>,","<b>Secrets Manager</b> for the database password,","<b>CloudWatch</b> alarms and logs,","<b>CloudTrail</b>,","everything built with <b>Terraform</b> and deployed by a <b>pipeline</b> using <b>OIDC</b>."]},{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>Memos against this design:</b>"}]},{"title":"The classic three-tier web application","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["It has the network, the presentation tier and the application tier.","It is <b>missing the data tier</b>: its data is in SQLite inside the container.","It runs only <b>one task</b>, which is a <b>single point of failure</b>."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-29-troubleshooting-on-aws-a-method","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Designing good systems","num":"29","title":"Troubleshooting on AWS: a method","key":false,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"modules/ecs (log group, 7-day retention)"}],"cards":[{"title":"The method: follow the request","part":"1/4","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Check each part in the same order the request travels."},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>DNS:</b> does the name resolve to the right place? <code>dig +short tm.myegh.com</code>. If not, check the record and the NS delegation (<code>dig +trace</code>).","<b>Certificate:</b> does the browser show a certificate warning? Check that the certificate is <b>Issued</b>, covers this exact name, and is attached to the listener.","<b>Load balancer:</b> what status code comes back?"]}]},{"title":"The method: follow the request","part":"2/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<b>503:</b> no healthy targets.","<b>502:</b> bad reply from the target (often the wrong port).","<b>504:</b> the target did not reply in time (often a security group)."]}]},{"title":"The method: follow the request","part":"3/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":4,"items":["<b>Target health:</b> <code>aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn &lt;arn&gt;</code>. The reason codes include <code>Target.Timeout</code> and <code>Target.ResponseCodeMismatch</code>.","<b>The service and tasks:</b> ECS service events, and the task’s <b>stopped reason</b>.","<b>The application:</b> the logs (<code>aws logs tail /ecs/memos --follow</code>).","<b>The network rules:</b> do the security groups, NACLs and route tables allow the traffic? Is the <b>port the same</b> <b>everywhere</b>?"]}]},{"title":"The method: follow the request","part":"4/4","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":8,"items":["<b>Permissions:</b> for an <code>AccessDenied</code> error, <code>aws sts get-caller-identity</code>, then check the policies and CloudTrail."]}]},{"title":"Common problems and their usual causes","part":"1/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Problem</b>","<b>Usual causes</b>"],["Cannot see my resources","Wrong <b>Region</b> selected, or wrong <b>account</b>"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>AccessDenied</code>"]}]},{"title":"Common problems and their usual causes","part":"2/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","Wrong identity; missing Allow; an explicit Deny; an SCP; a bucket policy; <code>ListBucket</code> vs <code>GetObject</code> resource mix-up"],["Cannot SSH to an instance","No public IP; security group does not allow port 22 from your IP; NACL; no route to the internet gateway; wrong key or user name; key file permissions"],["Private instance or task cannot reach the internet","No NAT gateway route; NAT gateway in a private subnet; no outbound security group rule"]]}]},{"title":"Common problems and their usual causes","part":"3/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["ECS task keeps stopping","App crashes (check logs); missing environment variable or secret; not enough memory; image cannot be pulled; failing health checks"]]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["<code>CannotPullContainerError</code>"]}]},{"title":"Common problems and their usual causes","part":"4/5","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":false,"rows":[["","Image or tag does not exist; execution role missing ECR permissions; no route to ECR"],["Unexpected bill","NAT gateways, idle load balancers, forgotten instances or EBS volumes, public IPv4 addresses, data transfer. Check <b>Cost Explorer</b> by service and set <b>Budgets</b>"],["Terraform plan wants to change things you did not change","<b>Drift</b>: someone changed the resource in the Console"]]}]},{"title":"Common problems and their usual causes","part":"5/5","blocks":[{"type":"note","kind":"memos","html":"🔧 <b>The rule I follow:</b> “I follow the request: DNS → listener and certificate → target group health → service events → container logs → security groups.”"}]}]},{"id":"aws-chapter-30-putting-it-all-together-the-memos-project-from-st","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Designing good systems","num":"30","title":"Putting it all together: the Memos project from start to finish","key":true,"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/README.md","label":"README"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"}],"cards":[{"title":"Chapter 30: Putting it all together: the Memos project from start to finish","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"This chapter walks through my real project in one place, so you can see how every service connects."}]},{"title":"What it is","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"p","html":"Memos is an open-source note-taking web app (a Go backend with a React frontend). I deployed it to AWS so that <code>https://tm.myegh.com</code> serves it. Everything is created by Terraform and deployed by GitHub Actions."}]},{"title":"The architecture","part":"","blocks":[{"type":"pre","text":"                           Internet\n                               │\n              Route 53: tm.myegh.com (A alias → load balancer)\n                               │\n ┌─────────────────────────────┼──────────── VPC 10.0.0.0/16, eu-west-1 ─┐\n │  Public subnets  10.0.1.0/24 (1a)    10.0.2.0/24 (1b)                  │\n │      ┌───────────── Application Load Balancer ────────┐                │\n │      │  :80  → 301 redirect to HTTPS                   │  NAT gateway  │\n │      │  :443 → TLS (ACM certificate) → forward         │  ×2 (per AZ)  │\n │      └───────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘               │\n │                              │ HTTP to port 8081 (target type ip)      │\n │  Private subnets 10.0.3.0/24 (1a)    10.0.4.0/24 (1b)                  │\n │      ECS cluster memos-cluster → service memos-service                 │\n │      Fargate task: 0.25 vCPU / 512 MB, container \"memos\" on 8081       │\n │      logs → CloudWatch /ecs/memos (7 days)                             │\n └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n   Tasks pull the image from ECR \"memos\" through the NAT gateway (HTTPS)."}]},{"title":"What happens when someone visits the site","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["The browser asks DNS for <code>tm.myegh.com</code>. The question reaches <b>Route 53</b> (through the NS delegation at Fasthosts), which answers with the load balancer’s addresses using the <b>alias record</b>.","If the visitor typed <code>http://</code>, the port-80 <b>listener</b> replies with a <b>301 redirect</b> to <code>https://</code>.","The browser connects on <b>port 443</b>. The load balancer presents the <b>ACM certificate</b> and decrypts the request.","The HTTPS listener <b>forwards</b> the request to the <b>target group</b>."]}]},{"title":"What happens when someone visits the site","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["The target group sends it to a <b>healthy</b> task’s private IP on <b>port 8081</b>. The app’s <b>security group</b> allows this only because the source is the <b>load balancer’s security group</b>.","Memos answers, and the reply goes back the same way."]}]},{"title":"What happens when I push a code change","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["I push to <code>main</code>.","<code>build.yml</code><b> :</b> tfsec scans the Terraform → <b>OIDC</b> login → build the Docker image (three stages, 75.8 MB) → tag it with the <b>commit ID</b> → push to <b>ECR</b> (immutable tags, scan on push).","<code>deploy.yml</code>, only if the build succeeded:"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["check the image exists,","<code>terraform fmt -check</code>, <code>init</code>, <code>validate</code>,","<code>plan</code> with the new image URL,","<code>apply</code> the saved plan."]}]},{"title":"What happens when I push a code change","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":4,"items":["Terraform registers a <b>new task definition revision</b>. ECS starts a new task, waits for it to pass the <b>health</b> <b>check</b>, then drains and stops the old one (<b>rolling update</b>).","The <b>health-check job</b> checks <code>https://tm.myegh.com/healthz</code> returns 200. If not, the pipeline fails.","<b>Rollback:</b> run <code>deploy.yml</code> by hand with the previous commit ID."]}]},{"title":"How the project grew (three phases)","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>By hand in the Console (February 2026,</b> <code>eu-west-2</code><b> ):</b>"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Containers in <b>public</b> subnets with public IPs, on port 5230.","Health check on <code>/healthz</code> with relaxed timings.","The <code>tm</code> subdomain delegated from Fasthosts to Route 53."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":2,"items":["<b>Rebuilt as Terraform (</b><code>eu-west-1</code><b> ):</b>"]}]},{"title":"How the project grew (three phases)","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["Seven modules.","Containers moved into <b>private subnets behind NAT gateways</b>: the most important design change."]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":3,"items":["<b>Automated:</b>"]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"start":1,"items":["GitHub Actions with OIDC.","After a project review, I added path filters, tfsec, the bootstrap stack, S3 native state locking, separate security group rule resources, and automated certificate validation."]}]},{"title":"Every AWS service in Memos and its job","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Service</b>","<b>Job in Memos</b>","<b>Chapter</b>"],["IAM","Three roles: GitHub pipeline, ECS execution, ECS task","9"],["VPC, subnets, route tables, IGW, NAT","The private network across two AZs","14"],["Security groups","Internet → load balancer → app, nothing else","14"],["Route 53","DNS for <code>tm.myegh.com</code>, alias to the load balancer","15"],["ALB","HTTPS entry point, redirect, health checks","16"],["ACM","Free TLS certificate, validated automatically","17"],["S3","Terraform state (versioned, encrypted, HTTPS-only)","18, 26"],["ECR","Private image storage (immutable tags, scanning)","20"]]}]},{"title":"Every AWS service in Memos and its job","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>Service</b>","<b>Job in Memos</b>","<b>Chapter</b>"],["ECS + Fargate","Runs the container in private subnets","20"],["CloudWatch Logs","Container logs (<code>/ecs/memos</code>)","23"],["STS + OIDC","Pipeline logs in without stored keys","27"]]}]},{"title":"Known limitations (be honest about these)","part":"1/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":["<b>Data is not persistent:</b> SQLite inside the container is lost on every deploy. Fix: RDS PostgreSQL or EFS.","<b>One task:</b> a short outage if it fails. Fix: at least 2 tasks with ECS Service Auto Scaling (after fix 1).","<b>The pipeline role is too powerful:</b> AdministratorAccess and a wildcard on branches. Fix: limit to <code>main</code> and to the needed permissions.","<b>No image scanning before push, and no approval gate.</b> Fix: Trivy in <code>build.yml</code>, a protected environment."]}]},{"title":"Known limitations (be honest about these)","part":"2/2","blocks":[{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":5,"items":["<b>No alarms.</b> Fix: CloudWatch alarms on 5xx errors and unhealthy targets, sent to SNS.","<b>Cost:</b> two NAT gateways are about £50 a month. Fix: VPC endpoints, or one NAT gateway for development.","<code>sleep 60</code> <b>before the health check.</b> Fix: <code>aws ecs wait services-stable</code>."]}]}]},{"id":"aws-common-confusions-and-mistakes","guide":"aws","guideTitle":"AWS From Zero","part":"Revision tools","num":"","title":"Common confusions and mistakes","key":false,"links":[],"cards":[{"title":"Common confusions and mistakes","part":"1/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>People often think…</b>","<b>What is actually true</b>"],["“A Region and an Availability Zone are the same”","A Region is a geographic area. An AZ is one separate data centre site inside a Region. Each Region has at least three"],["“Running in two AZs means there is never downtime”","Only if you also run <b>2+ copies</b> of each part. Memos has subnets in two AZs but only one task"],["“AWS will stop me from exposing my data”","Under the shared responsibility model, <b>your</b> settings are <b>your</b> responsibility"]]}]},{"title":"Common confusions and mistakes","part":"2/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>People often think…</b>","<b>What is actually true</b>"],["“The root user is fine for daily work”","Use it only for the few tasks that need it. Protect it with MFA"],["“An IAM role has a password”","Roles have <b>no</b> long-term credentials. Assuming one gives <b>temporary</b> credentials"],["“Allow beats Deny”","An <b>explicit Deny always wins</b>"],["“An SCP gives permissions”","SCPs only <b>limit</b>. An IAM policy must still allow the action"],["“User data runs on every boot”","Only the <b>first</b> boot"]]}]},{"title":"Common confusions and mistakes","part":"3/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>People often think…</b>","<b>What is actually true</b>"],["“Stopping an instance keeps its public IP”","It usually gets a <b>new</b> one, unless it has an Elastic IP"],["“Stopped instances cost nothing”","You still pay for EBS volumes and Elastic IPs"],["“A subnet is public because of its name”","Only if its <b>route table</b> sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to an internet gateway"],["“An internet gateway alone makes an instance reachable”","It also needs a route, a public IP, and security group and NACL rules"]]}]},{"title":"Common confusions and mistakes","part":"4/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>People often think…</b>","<b>What is actually true</b>"],["“A NAT gateway lets the internet reach private servers”","It allows <b>outgoing</b> connections only"],["“One NAT gateway gives high availability”","It lives in one AZ. Use one per AZ"],["“A <code>/24</code> gives 256 usable addresses”","<b>251</b> in AWS: 5 are reserved"],["“Security groups and NACLs work the same way”","SG: one resource, allow only, <b>stateful</b>. NACL: subnet, allow and deny, <b>stateless</b>, numbered order"]]}]},{"title":"Common confusions and mistakes","part":"5/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>People often think…</b>","<b>What is actually true</b>"],["“Timeout and connection refused mean the same”","<b>Timeout</b> = blocked. <b>Refused</b> = reached, but nothing listening"],["“A CNAME can be used for the bare domain”","Not at the zone apex. Use a Route 53 <b>alias</b>"],["“An NLB cannot handle TLS”","It <b>can</b> terminate TLS. It just cannot route by HTTP path or host"],["“S3’s eleven nines means it is never down”","That is <b>durability</b> (not losing data). Availability is about 99.99%"],["“S3 has folders”","Keys contain <code>/</code>. The Console only <b>shows</b> them as folders"]]}]},{"title":"Common confusions and mistakes","part":"6/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>People often think…</b>","<b>What is actually true</b>"],["“Deleting a versioned object frees the space”","It adds a delete marker. Old versions remain until removed"],["“EBS volumes can be attached in any AZ”","Only in their own AZ. Use a snapshot to move one"],["“Instance store keeps data”","It is lost when the instance stops or fails"],["“Execution role and task role are the same”","Execution role = what <b>ECS</b> needs to start the task. Task role = what <b>your code</b> may do"]]}]},{"title":"Common confusions and mistakes","part":"7/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>People often think…</b>","<b>What is actually true</b>"],["“<code>latest</code> means the newest image”","It is only a default tag name. Use the commit ID"],["“<code>EXPOSE</code> opens a port”","It only <b>documents</b> the port"],["“Data in a container is saved”","Containers are replaced, and their files go with them. Use EFS, S3 or a database"],["“RDS Multi-AZ adds read capacity”","Multi-AZ is for <b>failover</b>. <b>Read replicas</b> add read capacity"],["“Serverless means no servers”","It means <b>you do not manage</b> them"],["“Lambda can run for hours”","Maximum <b>15 minutes</b>"]]}]},{"title":"Common confusions and mistakes","part":"8/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>People often think…</b>","<b>What is actually true</b>"],["“SQS and SNS do the same thing”","SQS: queue, consumers <b>pull</b>, one consumer per message. SNS: <b>push</b> to every subscriber"],["“CloudWatch and CloudTrail do the same thing”","CloudWatch: <b>performance</b> (metrics, logs, alarms). CloudTrail: <b>who did what</b> (API record)"],["“CloudWatch shows EC2 memory automatically”","It needs the <b>CloudWatch agent</b>"],["“Secrets can go in environment variables in the code”","Use Secrets Manager or Parameter Store, injected at runtime"]]}]},{"title":"Common confusions and mistakes","part":"9/9","blocks":[{"type":"table","head":true,"rows":[["<b>People often think…</b>","<b>What is actually true</b>"],["“<code>terraform plan</code> changes things”","It only <b>shows</b> changes. <code>apply</code> makes them"],["“Access keys in GitHub secrets are fine”","Prefer <b>OIDC</b>: there are no long-lived keys to leak"],["“Only unused Elastic IPs cost money”","<b>Every</b> public IPv4 address is charged"],["“Spot is just cheaper On-Demand”","AWS can take Spot instances back with a 2-minute warning"],["“Deleting a leaked key from the latest commit is enough”","It is still in Git history. Rotate the key first, then clean the history"]]}]}]}];
